      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0010" n="art0010">
        <p>.'+4+</p>
        <p>WOMAN'S WORLD.</p>
        <p>CHAT and GOSSIP.</p>
        <p>except for those devotees who had ziothing bet- ter to do than give much time to mastering each so-called improvement as it appeared.</p>
        <p>Territorials in the Willie City. The White City at Shepheitl's Bush is no longer a pleasure resort, but the headquarters for Territorials and their officers. The Stadium is used for drill, the Garden Club has been shorn of its one-time luxury and comfort, and is inhabited by busy officers intent on bringing their men to a high standard of perfection in the shortest possible time. The conversion of the Royal pavilion into a hospital brings home more forcibly than anything else the grim realities of war. The Hall of Science and the huge Machinery Hall have been converted into sleeping quarters for the men, and instead of exhibits as heretofore, 'contains rows upon rows of neat 'beds, each covered with its army blanket.</p>
        <p>A Womon Town trier. Since the war broke out, women in France and Russia have filled many posts vacated by men, from which they are barred during peace, but Cossack women have for generations fought side by side with the men. British women are following their lead in filling civilian positions hitherto considered the privilege of the sterner set. A roman town crier is quite an innoll tion, in which the town of Chertsey led the way The holder of the office enlisted for active ‘er- vice, and his wife has been permitted to fill the vacant post. A couple of women volunteers are now assisting at the Westminster Police Court. They wear a smart blue uniform of dark serge, and look quite efficient and fit to perform the duties of their office, which is as novel as it is necessary.</p>
        <p>Wedding Tailor-mades. Brides, whose grooms are on active service necessitating hurried wedding arrangements, show a preference for highly-finished tailored suits of velvet, when the conventional wedding dress is dispensed with. Chiffon velvet is usually chosen, and the results are eminently satisfactory. For these beautiful suits the Rus- sian blouse is particularly effective and suit- able when trimmed with ermine or thinchilla.</p>
        <p>Se popular are Russian styles that to be without a Vouch of this all-pervading influence is a sure fign of being behind the times. That the long Dervish tunic and the long coat will reign supreme for the next six months is certain, for both are Russian in style. The full-backed coat or redingote should be rather ihort if a good effect is desired, and is at its best when some- what shorter in front than at the back. The Highwayman's coat is a great favourite just now as a winter overcoat. It has flowing lines, and gives plenty of freedom, besides being warm and comfortable.</p>
        <p>Camisoles and Blouses. With the return of the semi-transparent blouse for afternoon wear, manufacturers are. giving more attention to the turning out of dainty camisoles. These are being shown in great variety, and those made of fine Japanese silk are very nearly, if not quite, as warm as others of woollen material. They are 'daintily trimmed at the neck and sleeves with Valen- ciennes lace or embroidery. The fulness is drawn into a band at the waist, where a little basque edged with lace makes a suitable finish. These camisoles could be easily and quickly made at home, odd pieces of silk and lace being requisitioned, or as economy in dress is the first consideration just now, the best parts of a well- worn silk shirt could be used, and made to give satisfactory results. For washable blouses noth- ing is more useful than sets of pins and buttons of different colours. The former fastens a jabot, or can be used for the neck, and the latter are easily removable, being furnished with slip rings, or double bars. The enamel sets are par- ticularly effective on white or ivory silk. Ninon blouses that match the skirts of afternoon dresses are frequently mounted on net or lace, and are apparently far too flimsy for winter wear, but a silk camisole may be relied upon to give the necessary warmth.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0011" n="art0011">
        <head>By MARJORIE.</head>
        <p>By MARJORIE.</p>
        <p>Christmas Fare. Christmas will be shorn of many of its fes- tivities this nal., but the Smithfield Club is living up.to the maxim, "Business as usual," and the cattle show will be held according to custom at the Agricultural Hall from December 7th to 11th. The war will make little or no difference to the show, which attracts so many country visitors to London, but it will largely cut off the supply of foreign turkeys, which usually arrive in large quantities from the Con- tinent. Irish and British birds will be in re- .quest, but with Christmassing generally on a reduced scale, there will be less demand, and that will not be without its effect on prices of Christmas fare. Economy is the watchword in nearly every household, and "doing with less" is making a better cook of the average house- wife. The war has reduced larder waste and it has taught many a housewife how to improvise a dainty meal from inexpensive materials, and turn broken victuals to the best possible accoitnt. In three out of four households the extra cost of tea, owing to the war, could be saved without it being noticed. It is just as easy to waste tea as to N. aste mustard, and it could be prevented by carefully measuring the quantities for daily use. That may not help the Chancellor of the Exchequer, but needless waste is as bad for the nation as for the individual.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0012" n="art0012">
        <head>Passing of fridge Parties.</head>
        <p>The Passing of fridge Parties.</p>
        <p>Profitable Recipes and Useful Suggestions.</p>
        <p>1</p>
        <p>Roast Goose.</p>
        <p>MY DEAR CHILDREN,-</p>
        <p>"BOBS."</p>
        <p>„</p>
        <p>Since the beginning of the war most women have been more profitably employed than in spending their time in artificial entertaining on "At home" days, and there will be little or no card playing, and few bridge parties this winter. Even before the war the ponlarity of bridge had greatly declined, owing largely to the endless complications introduced into the game gener- ally from America. Intricate rules and regula- tions led to wrangling and spoilt the game</p>
        <p>Take a young, tender goose, stuff with a dress- ing made of six mashed potatoes, a teaspoon- ful of salt, half a teaspoonful of pepper, half a minced onion, a teaspoonful of finely powdered sage, and two tablespoonfuls of butter. Truss, and dredge with peyper, salt and flour. If pos- sible, roast before the fire; if not, in a very hot oven; baste frequently with the drippings. Serve with apple sauce and currant jelly. Herrings Plentiful.</p>
        <p>In past years enormous quantities of herring, amounting in the year 1913 to something more than eleven million cwts., have been landed in the United Kingdom by' British fishing vessels. Probably nine-tenths of this quantity of fish has found its way to foreign markets, being ex- ported mainly in the form of "pickled" herring '—pickled in barrels of brine. The chief coun- tries of export have been in the past Germany and Russia. The German market is closed, and the Russian market is extremely difficult to reach. On the outbreak of war there were some 317,000 barrels of pickled herring, the product mainly of the Scottish fishery, ready for export. The greater part of this stock is still in the country. The English fishery is usually at its height in the months of September, October and November, and, in spite of inetaable restric- tions of this fishery owing to t eltisider- able quantities may yet be landed. As a result of the closure of foreign markets the greater part of the herring landed in this country should be available for home consumption. It has been stated that two salt herrings con- tain as much animal protein as need enter into the daily diet of an ordinary working man. It is important to consider the value of salted or " pickled" herring, because in this state of preservation they can be kept for 12 months or more in good condition. Professor Hopkins, F.R.S., reader in chemical physiology at the University of Cambridge, states that "the results of several researches made in various countries indicate that the nutritive value to man of the flesh of fish is fully equal to that of mammalian flesh (beef or mutton)." As regards the North Sea herring, he states that "when fresh it contains on the average 18.6 per cent. protein and 3.44 per cent. fat. Fresh lean beef</p>
        <p>_,J,)=7-=.0,-e0,,vw,ver.,,t(w,54y„,0000,A43 CHILDREN'S CORNER. Conducted by UNCLE NED. 5)42.0()%2A 'll22:OWri5W-cVdtqA2rol-6-W-'4}lr4(A.2sel</p>
        <p>I was very pleased to receive a letter on Thursday morning from Ida Barlow in re- sponse to my appeal last week. The letter is printed in full below, and shows how these, of my nieces, have attempted to do their little share to help the needy. All I can add is that I am pleased to welcome them to the corner, and ask them to send in their addresses for my big book. You must not forget the competition an- nounced last week, for I would like to receive an essay from every nephew and niece. Your loving UNCLE NED.</p>
        <p>I DA'S LETTER. 50, Lancaster-street, Warrington. Dear Uncle Ned,—We read in the Cjaildren's Corner in last week's "Examiner" that you were asking if any of the girls in Lancaster- street were your nieces. We were not at the time, but would be glad to become your nieces now. Our names are Eva Dennett, Elsie Oak- ley, Amy Barlow and myself. You were asking how we got the concert up. First of all we decided to have it in Mrs. Jenks' yard, because it was the largest and would accommodate the largest number of people. Then we wrote some tickets out, one of which we have enclosed, and took them to our neigh- bours and friends, to whom we sold them. By this method we raised ten shillings, which we took to the head mistress of our school to buy wool to knit into socks, &amp;c., for the soldiers. The two shillings and sixpence was raised by selling apples at the interval, each half-apple selling at one halfpenny.</p>
        <p>From your loving nieces, per IDA BARLOW. P.S.—So you se the boys are a long way behind us.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts always knew how to control ' men. One incident is reported in which he made a very tall man look small. The incident 'occurred in a club room in London, where, amongst the company, was a very tall wan who tried to be funny. lie said to Lord Roberts, only a who, as probably you are, aware, was little man, "I have often heard ..of you. but I</p>
        <p>contains on the average 22.4 per cent. protein and 3.0 per cent. fat."</p>
        <p>The Pancake.</p>
        <p>The pancake is a distinctively American in- stitution, says an American paper. It is eaten only in secret in our best families. It would be eaten openly and above-board were it not that folk of the upper circle have to maintain their dignity before the servants. Properly made, the pancake is a thing of beauty and a joy for the time being. Improperly made, as it usually is, it is a blight upon life and a harassment to the stom- ach. A wrongly prepared pancake can stay with you longer than the after effects of pneu- monia.</p>
        <p>If our girls were taught how to make pan- cakes civilisation would go forward so rapidly that those who are now trying to reform our social structure would be back numbers by day after to-morrow.</p>
