      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0013" n="art0013">
        <head>HERE AND THERE.</head>
        <p>HERE AND THERE.</p>
        <p>A man could make all the money in the world, says an American paper, by inventing for women a looking-glass that would show only good figures. A Serious Affair.</p>
        <p>A Chinese marriage is all ceremony—no talk, no levity, and much crying. The solemnity of , a funeral prevails. Resented by the Bridegroom. "Even the English language emphasises the insignificance of a man al his own wedding," said the prospective bridegroom disconsolately. "There isn't an independent word to designate him. He is merely called the groom of the bride, as if he were just about on a level with the bridesmaids and a little below the maid of honour. Best man, of course,, means the bride- groom's best man, but the phrase itself tends to exalt this individual at the expense of his superior. "Then there's no adjective to describe what pertains to the male half of the affair. You can't speak of the bridegroomal ' trousers or necktie. On the other hand, 'bridal' applies not only to the possessions of the girl, but to what relates to both of them equally. The ~ery words 'matrimony' and 'matrimonial are from the feminine side only. 'Patrimony' has nothing to do with the nuptials. It applies only to wealth and signifies that a man's part in the affair is to get out and hustle for the cash."</p>
        <p>cannot see much of you!" The men who were present, began to laugh, but they roared when " Bobs " replied: "I can see a lot of you, but I have never heard of you!" The tall man felt snubbed and I have heard it said that he did not speak again that night. During Queen Victoria's Jubilee celebrations, some Indian cavalry were entertained to dinner in London, and Lord Roberts was asked to be present to welcome the native troops. Subadar Singh, the Indian officer, saluted "Bobs," and then offered his sword, which signified that the Indian troops were willing to do what they were told. However, Lord Roberts refused to take the sword, saying, "I don't want your sword. Give me your hand. We are comrades." This little act made the Indian officer feel very proud. It was no wonder that the gallant soldier was beloved in India. Although he did not like cats, the hero of a hundred battles was very fond of children. The story has been told that one day when be was in India he visited a shop kept by a native. While he , was waiting a pretty little black girl came running into the shop. The great soldier was so struck with the beauty of the child that he could not help picking her lip and pretend- ing to run away with her. The little girl's mother, however, thought that "Bobs" was really running away with-the child, and she ran after him and drove him out of the building. You all know how much Lord Roberts loved his country, so that you will hardly be surprised when I say that there is a sentence painted in the hall of his late residence at Ascot which reads: "What are you doing for your country, which has done so much for you?" From this you will gather how interested he was in our great Empire.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>SOLDIERS' PETS.</head>
        <p>SOLDIERS' PETS.</p>
        <p>Recently you read of a famous dog which was the pet of its regiment. Dogs, however, ' are not the only form of pets that soldiers take with them to battle. For instance, one of the Canadian regiments has brought a tame white rat, which sat on a soldier's shoulders playing with his ear as the troops marched through Plymouth.. Another regiment has stated its intention to bring a small black bear, a pet they captured in the Canadian woods. "Billy," the pet goat of the Welsh Regiment, is stated to be very much alive, and has marched every mile with the regiment since it landed in France..</p>
        <p>BRITAIN: AN ACROSTIC. (BY JESSIE Be the foeman ne'er so strong, Right at last shall vanquish wrong. In the fight may many die— To the skies their souls shall, fly. All that is, at all, we gain In the hour of deepest pain: None may live, but first are slain.</p>
        <p>A Drawback. Two tramps were discussing the ways and means of living in this hard world without being laid up with bodily exhaustion. "If it only wortet Bich a long way off, Bill, we might go to the South Sea Islands. Cokernnts and bananas all over the place for nuthin'." Bill ruminated deeply for some time and queried: "I suppose yer 'as to pick 'em?" " Yus, o' course !" "Ugh !" was the disgusted reply. •`-` I knew there'd be some drorback !"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>Origin of the Marseillaise.</head>
        <p>The Origin of the Marseillaise.</p>
        <p>It may not be generally known that the Mar- seillaise is very similar to an old Irish air sung by Orangemen in the 17th century. The French national anthem is one of the most inspiring marches ever written. It is a call to battle, and was composed just before its author left for the field.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Language.</head>
        <p>HINTS ON HEALTH. Useful Information in Popular Language.</p>
        <p>Pneumonia. Abundance of fresh air should be secured and maintained at a temperature of 65deg. Fahren- heit. As heated air rises, the window should be. ()Pen top and bottom, but care inust be taken that the patient does not lie in a draught. The room should be thoroughly ventilated twice a day. The patient must lie down in bed, if possible. He must not exert himself in any way, and the nurse should help in lifting him, making his bed, and drawing through his sheet. He must be perfectly free from worry, and should not be talked to. He should Near a flannel bedgown fastened down the back with tapes, which thus made would obviate moving him unnecessarily. A pneumonia jacket may also be needed. It is made of gamgee tissue and fastened down the sides with tapes. There should be a thorough and daily action of the bowels. During the time he is on liquid diet his mouth should be swabbed out with glycerine and boracic three times a day and once during the night. Tepid sponging is beneficial for re- ducing the high femperature. In convalescence there should be a generous and carefully sel- ected diet, constant fresh air, and sufficient rest. A change of air is beneficial for the patient.— Nursing Mirror." Dust Danger. Pasteur once made the remark that the dust- brush and broom were a thousand-fold more dangerous than gunpowder, and cost the world thousands of lives to one of the latter. Con- stant stirring up of dust in the house is a menace to health and a direct destroyer of life. The time will come when' such a thing as a carpet will not be permitted in a civilised household, and when the floor-brush and broom will have disappeared for ever. Pew people realise that they may bring in from the street on their shoes or skirts every form of disease that flesh is heir to. This is ground off into the carpet, the warmth of the apartment helps on the process of development, the housemaid de- liberately sets the germs in motion with her broom, and the air is filled with them. They settle on the furniture and ornaments, and with the dust-brush she whisks them off again on to the carpet, where they remain, possibly to propagate, certainly to live, until by the next stirring P they may float into somebody's lungs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>BUSY PORT OF LONDON. Uninterrupted Imports.</head>
        <p>THE BUSY PORT OF LONDON. Uninterrupted Imports.</p>
        <p>On this,. the 115th day of the war, says Thursday's "Times," the Port of London re- mains open and accessible. Far from business being adversely affected there is every sign that it is abnormal. At no time has there been so great a quantity of food in London, nor so large a quantity of timber, as there is now. This is largely attributable to two causes—the vast purchases of the Sugar Commission and the increased importation of timber for build- ing huts., for soldiers. During the month of October there was a considerable decline in the supplies of tea brought to London. This decline was due al- most entirely to the activities of the Emden, , and very soon after the Sydney's notable achievement and the disappearance of the Ger- man cruiser from the high seas, the tea imports began to rise and supplies of the commodity recently singled out for extra taxation are in- creasing every day. Sugar is coming in from Java, America, and Mauritius in unprecedented quantities, and special provisi4l has had to be made at the docks for its storage. The supply of meat from abroad continues without interruption, and the latest available figures show that during the last week of Sep- tember 13,000 tons in excess of the quantity imported during the same period of last year were brought in. With regard to timber there was a swift decline in imports during the first two months of the war, as indeed there was bound to be, in view of the fact that the ports from which it comes are in the White Sea and the Baltic. But since the end of September business has gradu- ally recovered its normal condition, and is likely to remain undisturbed until the advance of winter makes shipping in the eastern waters an impossibility. The enemy has in no way succeeded in reduc- ing our grain supply; it continues to reach us regularly and nothing has happened to suggest that it will cease to do so. Meanwhile, with this great acenmulation of supplies, there comes a shortage of barges. The Government have taken over a number of barges, and are using them for mili- tary purposes. The result is that, with ware- houses stored to overflowing, there are many barges lying in the river with cargo still aboard. They are what are technically known as "dumb" barges, and are used in strings hauled by a tug for conveying goods from the vessel which has brought them to the Port, to wharves and rail- way depots. At the smaller stations the trans- ferred goods are being received in such quan- tities that they cannot be dealt with quickly enough to allow the barges to do the amount of work of which they are capable. In view of the loss thus incurred by the owifer it has been decided to charge demurrage at the rate of 10s. a day, to begin two clear days after the barge has been loaded and her cargo has remained aboard. .</p>
        <p>Happily there is plenty of employment at the docks at present and many labourers have even been transferred to French ports.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>HOME NOTES.</head>
        <p>HOME NOTES.</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
        <p>• ••• .••• ••••:* 4. +4, ..•.9.* . ..+.:..0)4+.•.)+ 4* 4' •:• •</p>
        <p>FARM AND GARDEN.</p>
        <p>CATTLE AND ACORN EATING. I hear from the farmers hereabout that many cattle have been, lost by eating acorns. I never allow oak trees in fences and rarely in fields. The place for the oak is the wood or grovo, where cattle do not go.—" Sussex" in "Farm and Home."</p>
        <p>FOOT AND MOUTH REGULATIONS WITHDRAWN.</p>
        <p>The Board .of Agriculture and Fisheries have issued orders withdrawing all the remaining restrictions on the movement of animals which were imposed by them in connection with the outbreaks confirmed on the 18th ult. of foot-and- mouth disease at Orlingbury, Northampton- shire, and Veal, Kent, no cases of the disease having occurred since that date. The orders take immediate effect.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>"COUNTRY BUTTER."</head>
        <p>"COUNTRY BUTTER."</p>
        <p>In' the "'Scottish Smallholder" Miss Lily Strang, N.D.D., of the West of Scotland Agri- cultural College, condemns the practice of keep- ing milk or cream too long. "This is the most common fault of any. When milking is over, the inilk should at once be 'placed in a clean vessel—the shallow pan prefekable. When skimmed, any cream which is left for churn- ing should be put in an earthenware 'crock, and daily, when a fresh supply is added, the whole should be well stirred. It is advisable 'that churning should take place when the cream has been gathered for three days, and when gathered it is advisable to leave it for twelve hours before churning. It is better to make 11b. of good butter than to wait till more cream has been gathered and make 21b. of very bad butter. I have often known local grocers in districts where I have been working to bury as many as 20Ib. of 'country butter,' simply because it was really so bad they could not get it sold."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>OUR AGRICULTURAL WORKERS.</head>
        <p>OUR AGRICULTURAL WORKERS.</p>
        <p>Years ago England's agricultural population' equalled her industrial population, and she pro- duced almost all the necessary foodstuffs she consumed, but from 1841 to 1901, the total popu- lation of England and Wales has increased in inverse ratio to her agricultural population. Fortunately, there is now an apparently decisive turn in the tide flowing from the villages into the town and colonies, and we take heart again! From the census of 1911 we find that the total population of the United Kingdom was some 45,365,589 souls, of whom, very approximately, rather more than one-third are actually "workers," and of these workers some 12.66 per cent. are engaged in agricultural callings, or, in other words, about 5.58 per cent. of our total population follow that occupation.—" Agricul- tural Economist and Horticultural Review."</p>
        <p>SIXTEEN SHEEP KILLED BY A TRAIN.</p>
        <p>Sixteen sheep which had strayed on to the railway lino near Old Trafford Station were killed by a passing .train at nine o'clock on Sunday. night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>BELGIAN REFUGEES.</head>
        <p>BELGIAN REFUGEES.</p>
        <p>Refugees from Belgium who have been en- gaged in agriculture are to be provided, by the generosity of Colonel Lloyd Evans, of Broom Hall, Pwllheli, with a residential agricultural college in South Carnarvonshire, together with allotments of good cultivatable land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>FEMALE STUDENTS.</head>
        <p>FEMALE STUDENTS.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the re-constituted Agri- cultural (previously Rural) Education Confer- ence was held on Tuesday afternoon in Com- mittee Room "A" of the House of Lords, Lord Barnard being in the chair. The Conference appointed a committee, com- posed partly of its own members and partly of women co-opted for the special purpose, to consider the following reference received from the Board of Agriculture and Fisheries:— To consider the provision made in England and Wales for the agricultural education of female students of sixteen years of age and up- wards and to report whether the existing faci- lities are sufficient, and, if not, to what extent and in what directions these should be devel- oped and improved:".</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>PAIdJ.I AS. In some places,,rWias are still in flower, but in many country gardens they have been cut down by frost. When thil happens no time should be lost in lifting them carefully. After having cut them down, dig them up and clear away as much of the soil as possible; then stand them crown downwards to allow the moisture to drain away. Finally, place them in a cellar or such-like place, where they will be free from frost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>WORMS IN LAWNS.</head>
        <p>WORMS IN LAWNS.</p>
        <p>Lime is the best remedy. Pour two, gallons of water over one pound of unslaked lime, 01 if more is wanted, use the same proportions. Stir the whole well up, and let it stand for forty- eight hours. Then when the sediment has settled pour the clear liquid through a rosed water pot during damp weather. This will bring the warms to the surface, when they can be swept up and cleared away.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <head>VEGETABLES AND FROST.</head>
        <p>VEGETABLES AND FROST.</p>
        <p>Now that we may have severe weather at any time care will have to be taken that lettuces and endive, which are stored in frames, are pro- tected from frost, as, if these are nearly full grown, decay sets in very rapidly if they are not given some covering in the way of strawy litter or bracken. A thick covering should be placed over the glass, and if the frost continues for some time, allow it to remain until it has passed away. It is well also to line the sides of the frames with one of the above materials. On the other hand, ventilation should be given on all fine days. Parsley will also require attention. Once let parsley be injured by severe frost or beaten down by a heavy fall of snow decay will soon set in. It must not on any account be coddled, a free circulation of air being all important during damp or mild weather.</p>
        <p>MOVING HARDY PLANTS. When the weather is open and the ground moist, an effort should be made to get hardy plants lifted, divided and replanted. If the plants have been in the same place for a con- siderable time then soil will have become ex- hausted. Steps should therefore be taken after having lifted all the plants and laid them where the roots will not get dry to thoroughly manure and trench the soil. Hardy plants are gross feeders, and their flowering season is greatly prolonged if they are given a good larder and a deep root run. After having trenched and manured the ground, it is well to let it settle for a few days before replanting. By doing the work now the plants are able to form new roots before the very severe weather is with us. In our experience autumn planting is better than waiting until the spring, as then cold drying winds often prevail, and unless watering is attended to growth is very slow.—" Wick," in "Farm and Home."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <head>WHEN GIRLS GROW WEAK.</head>
        <p>WHEN GIRLS GROW WEAK.</p>
        <p>When girls grow deathly pale, weak and miserable, then is the time for parents to take prompt steps, for delay means danger. The girl in her "teens" cannot develop into robust, happy womanhood without abundant red, rich blood in her veins, and it is lack of new, good blood that is the great trouble with nine girls out of ten. They grow antemic (bloodless), de- pressed, delicate and thin, with no appetite, no strength and no interest in life. In Dr. Williams' Pink Pills there is splendid, vigorous health, with glowing cheeks and sparkling eyes for every unhappy, fragile girl who is struggling on to womanhood in a wretched state of health. There is no medicine that can compare With Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for giving strength and dispelling disease. These Pills have a special beneficial influence on the blood, making it rich, red and pure, so that it is able to carry on its work of refreshing the tired body, the over- worked nerves and the flagging appetite. This is why thousands of women, now robust and attractive, are' recommending Dr. Williams' Pink Pills. ' I</p>
        <p>Begin to-day to dispel your anemia, your headaches, your indigestion and feeling of weak- ness by starting a course of Dr. Williams' Pink Pills for Pale People. They are sold by all dealers (accept nothing else); or send to Dr. Williams' Medicine Co., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, 2s. 9d. a box, or 13s. 9d. for six. FREE.—Send a postcard to Book Dept., 46, Holborn-viaduct, London, for a handy treatise on " The Blood and its Work," sent free,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>SOLDIER AND CHRISTIAN</head>
        <p>SOLDIER AND CHRISTIAN</p>
        <p>Baptist Pastor's Tribute to the Memory of "Bobs."</p>
        <p>An eloquent tribute to the memory of Field- Marshal Earl Roberts was paid at the Golborne-street Baptist Church on Sunday evening, when the Rev. H. Ogden delivered an impressive sermon on "Lord Roberts: Soldier and Christian," taking for his text 2 Samuel i. 26: "I am distressed for thee, my brother Jonathan: very pleasant hast thou been unto me: thy love to me was wonderful, passing the love of women."</p>
        <p>- - That lament, said the preazhcr, was the pan of one warrior for another. David's lament 'as as much a classic in literature as was Ohopin's "Marche Funebre" in music. Of Jonathan they might say that no nobler son was born to so unfortunate a father; and, amongst the few names of the Old Testament records, that of Jonathan was of the few that remained unsullied. He was a warrior as gentle as he was brave.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>GENTLE AS BRAVE.</head>
        <p>GENTLE AS BRAVE.</p>
        <p>Of the British warrior now laid to rest, they could say that he, also, was as gentle as he was brave. But that church was not the place in which to chant the praises merely of warriors. Baptists, save in the Cromwellian Army, were almost unknown as warriors—the Baptist Church was a temple of peace! Napoleon had been a warrior. As a man, they might admire him; but, as Christians, they must anathematise him. In war he had been what Rockefeller had been in finance: he marched to his empire over the bleeding, gaping wounds of humanity.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>VARIETIES OF BRAVERY.</head>
        <p>VARIETIES OF BRAVERY.</p>
        <p>Lord Roberts had not only been a warrior, but he had also been personally brave. The fact that he secured the V.C.. in his early mili- tary career proved that. The dare-devilry with which he kept the flag flying; his marvel- lous march to Kandahar; his stemming of the rush of the Afghans—all these were tributes to his personal bravery. But Bernhardi would be brave. He Would crush small nations, as the will crushed the hot steel; he would draw up seven hundred citizens in Dinant, and shoot them in cold blood; he would do right so long as it was an advantage; and, if need be, he would face a Hell of battle. Recruits, fed on Bernhardi, had valiantly come on to the attack when the Prussian Guards had been defeated; as a man at the front said, it would be fine if it were not so horrible. HIS GENTLENESS MADE HIM GREAT.</p>
        <p>They, however, rendered Lord Roberts their tribute, not because he had been a brave war- rior, but because they could say of him—as Scripture said of another—that "his gentleness had made him great." War might be, evi- dently, a horrible necessity; but, as Lord Roberts had proved, it need not be unneces- sarily brutal. A surgeon had, to be efficient, but he need not be cruel : he had to take off the limb, but he need not act like a bull-dog. A. G. Gardiner, exposing the conscription cam- paign, in one of his inimitable articles, paid a delightful tribute to Lord Roberts, as "a man whose humanity overbalanced his war-like training." This they found evidenced in the Boer War; at Paardeburg he would not endorse a wholesale slaughter of men. Maybe he pro- longed the war, but his human instinct had revealed that</p>
        <p>"The quality of mercy is not strained..</p>
        <p>A is twice blessed: It blesseth him that gives, and him that takes."</p>
        <p>AN OLD COMRADE.</p>
        <p>Many interesting--an;JdO"te";-;;;I:e related of Lord Roberts, which but emphasised the ten- derness of his soul. On one occasion, when dining with "dukes' sons and the sons of a millionaire," he saw, at the other end of the room, an old warrior whose medals related his history, and brought to "Bobs's" mind the fights of other days. Re left the "dukes' sons and the sons of a millionaire," and was soon absorbed in conversation with this old pen- sioner, whose whole estate was his modest pension.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>AMONGST. THE INDIAN TROOPS.</head>
        <p>AMONGST. THE INDIAN TROOPS.</p>
        <p>His bearing to the Indian troops revealed the man. Schoolboys had been taught, in the past, that India was a conquered province— conquered by the sword and to be kept by the sword. That idea was Prussian; certainly not British. Had they followed merely the law of conquest in India, "the brightest gem in the British crown" would now be in flaming revolt. To Lord Roberts, and his kind, they owed the loyalty of India. The native troops loved him as a father; and he treated them as a father, rather than as a stiff, stern barrack-sergeant. His last act was to visit his Indian children in France; and they would cherish his last act of thoughtfulness, and carry to the children of the Punjab the story of how "Bobs Bahadur" laid down his life to meet them in the trenches. Mr. Bonar Law said of him: "He was loved of all ranks and all nationalities: he loved them; they loved him." Lord Kitchener, who very rarely spoke about the heart, and who had very little room for sentiment, said of Lord Roberts that he "admired his qualities of head and of heart." When Lord Kitchener spoke of "qualities of heart," they must have been abundant and plentiful.</p>
        <p>"RELIGIOUS GROWTHS." As religious people, they most admired his intense faith. Lord Curzon said that "he was a deeply religious man." Barrack-life was not a fertile soil for religious growths: a hypocrite had short shrift in both barrack ward and officers' mess. What Lord Curzon said of him would shame thousands of clergy and minis- ters. The last letter that Lord Roberts wrote to Lord Curzon spoke about prayer and the value of family prayers; and said that, for fifty-five years, there had been family prayers in his household. The servants and family gathered at the family altar, as Lord Roberts said, without any compulsion, to plead jointly for God's mercy. Fifty-five years of family prayers! No wonder that he had been as gentle as he was brave! When he was carried to his last resting-place kings and princes did him homage; but he (the preacher) thought that perhaps the gaddest homage came from the young women in black, so many of whom had lined the route. With tear-stained faces, wrote the correspondent, they watched the old hero being taken to his last rest. Who were those young women in black? They were the young widows of British soldiers buried in the French trenches; and their tears were mingled with the tears of Lady Roberts—the high-born and the low-born were one in their common sorrow and their common grief.</p>
        <p>" FOR ALL THE SAINTS." At the last service one of the hymns sung was that by Bishop How; no fitter words could complete their tribute that night; and they could say, with conviction in their saying: "For all the saints who from their labours rest, Who Thee, by faith, before the world confessed, Thy name, 0 Jesu, be for ever blessed. Hallelujah. " From earth's wide bounds, or ocean's farthest</p>
        <p>coast; Through gates of pearl streams in the count- less host, Singing to Father, Son ,and Holy Ghost: Hallelujah."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SHOW,</head>
        <p>ROYAL LANCASHIRE SHOW,</p>
        <p>At the annual meeting of the Royal Lan- cashire Agricultural Society at Preston on Wed- nesday it was stated that the society had arranged to hold the next exhibition at Black- burn, and the 1916 show at Lancaster. The Royal Agricultural Society of England, holding its show at Manchesteit in 1916, had decided to allow members of the Royal Lancashire to make entries at till_ same rate as members of the Royal Agricurtural Society. The balance sheet submitted by the Treasurer showed a balance of profit amounting to £lO9, compared with £4,372 last year. The balance in favour of the society to date was £17,685.</p>
        <p>Xmas Presents for the Forces.</p>
        <p>Send your soldier friends a pudding made with ATORA Shredded Beef Suet. Requires no chop- ping and makes the best puddings and, mince- meat. British .Made and Owned. • Your grocer sells it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>CONFIRMATION SERVICES.</head>
        <p>CONFIRMATION SERVICES.</p>
        <p>Bishop Chavasse "Lays Hands "on Nearly 500 Young People.</p>
        <p>A confirmation service was held at the Parish Church on Saturday afternoon, when, in the Presence of a large congregation, the Bishop of Liverpool (the Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse) admin- istered the rite to 304 candidates. Of this ex- ceptionally large number, 86 were presented by the clergy of the Parish Church, whilst 200 were drawn from the St. George's and St. Clement's Mission Churches, others coming from the Grammar School (14), and the Train- ing College (4). The Bishop was assisted by the Rector (the Rev. Canon Willis) and the clergy of the parish. In the evening his Lordship confirmed a fur- ther 74 young people at Orford Church, 42 of them coming from the Orford Parish, 26 from Hollinfare, and six from Padgate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>BISHOP AT ST. BARNABAS'.</head>
        <p>THE BISHOP AT ST. BARNABAS'.</p>
        <p>How Prayer At Home Heartens the</p>
        <p>Fighting Men Abroad.</p>
        <p>A second confirmation service was held in St. Barnabas' Church on Tuesday evening, when the Right Rev. Dr. Chavasse, Bishop of Liverpool, "laid hands " on 163 young persons, drawn from five churches, viz., St. Barnabas', 37 males and 49 females; St. Pauls', 4 males and 20 females; St. Peter's, 11 males and 18 females; St. Ann's, 3 males and 13 females; and St. Elphin's, 6 males and 2 females: The service was conducted by the Vicar (the Rev. J. H. G. Hollingworth), and was attended by a congre- gation which taxed the accommodation of the sacred edifice to the utmost.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>God's Gift In Confirmation.</head>
        <p>God's Gift In Confirmation.</p>
        <p>In his preparatory remarks to the candidates the Bishop emphasised the fact that the gift they were asked to give to God in the act of confirmation was not a gift of money, nor of time, nor of strength; but the best gift they had to give—a gift which God would never force them to give and which He would only accept when it was gladly and willingly offered, viz., the gift of their hearts. And in return for their gift God was waiting to give them a gift that could not be bought by money; a gift more priceless than ever, man could offer—the gift of His Holy Spirit; a gift which would help them to keep the promise they were mak- ing by their solemn act that night, a gift that would help them to be God's faithful soldiers and servants unto the end of their lives. The Abiding Comfort_ in the Trenches,</p>
        <p>After the laying on of hands, the Bishop, addressing the confirmes, said that there were three ways in which they could keep the great gifts which he trusted God had conferred upon each of them that night—by faith, by service, and by communion. Ever must they remember that they were each God's child; that God was their Father. He was sure that the brave soldiers who were fighting for them that very night—fighting and suffering; aye, maybe, dying—found the greatest possible comfort in recollecting that God was their Father; that they were His sons and that His Fatherly Hand was ever over them. Equally so, did the gal- lant sailors, away out on the watery wastes of the North Sea—watching night and day to keep the enemy's ships from our shores—feel. security and comfort in the knowledge that the Fatherly Hand was over them all the time. Again, the knowledge that they were God's children, and that He was their loving Father, brought consolation and courage to the brave nurses and sisters who, close to the firing line, with the booming of the dread guns and the shriek of the shells daily in their ears, went about ministering to the sick and to the wounded.</p>
        <p>Prayers That Touch " Tommy's " Heart.' It was a comfort—a great, an inestimable comfort—to the men fighting for their country to know that at home devout people were pray- ing for them. Officers led their men to the charge, saying: "Remember, they are praying for us in England !" And one of the chaplains had written him telling how, as he sat by the bedsides of the wounded and told them that at noon each day thousands were praying for the soldiers and sailors—praying for them by name in the parish churches—great tears trickled down many a rugged, pain-furrowed cheek.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>, GOD OR KITCHENER ? In Whom Does the Nation Put Its</head>
        <p>, GOD OR KITCHENER ? In Whom Does the Nation Put Its</p>
        <p>Trust? THE QUAKER WAR VIEW.</p>
        <p>War, you will say, brings out courage, but it brings out the beast in a man. His passions of cruelty and revenge, which he has been keeping under here, are encouraged there. No man can take part in killing another, in looting a town, or in devastation, and ever be quite the same.</p>
        <p>These striking sentences, the like of which one seldom hears nowadays, were uttered by Mrs. Agatha Watts, of Manchester, in the course of an address on "Christianity and War," which she delivered at the Friends' Meeting House, Warrington, on Sunday evening. She believed, she said, that all and every war was contrary to God's law, and, whilst making the assertion quoted above, made it plain that she was ap- preciative of the personal bravery of our soldiers, adding that she did not want for a 'moment to belittle them. They were offering their lives for what they believed was right; they were, indeed, men of courage. But every- thing that was contrary to love was contrary to God, and war was -certainly contrary to love. In what had the people of this country been putting their faith? Were they treating God as their Father when they went to their churches and prayed for the success of their troops, and that their enemies, as they called them, might be brought to nought? Was that how they would go to an earthly father? They called God almighty; yet did they trust Him and His methods? Were they trusting in God or in Kitchener? Had they faith in God, or in Dreadnoughts, aircraft and submarines? Sometimes she wondered whether the3l lad faith, or only a great, deadly paralysing fear— fear which was making them serve and use material things.</p>
        <p>CHRISTIANITY'S CHANGED VIEWS. What a change had come over the Christian aspect on warfare—the killing of man by man. For two hundred years after the Crucifixion A was sufficient for a man to say: "I am a Christian and I cannot fight." Those early Christians felt, and understood, that when Christ disarmed Peter in the Garden of Geth- semane He disarmed every one of His followers, and it was not until in the time of the Emperor Constantine, and Christianity became allied with the State, that Christians began to take up arms to kill. Was it not time that they tried to get back to the Christian aspect of those first two hundred years? Before a righteous and just peace could be brought about, continued the speaker, the people of this country and of the others con- cerned would have to cleanse their hearts f urn hatred and from thoughts of revenge. They would have to get down to the bedrock of brotherly love. In the past they had not built their national and international life on the foundation of brotherhood, but had trusted to material things--to treaties and pie so-called balance of power. Had "they not served materialism long enough, and was it not time they tried to serve Christ and follow out His law if love?</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>Plias to the oda</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN SMETHURST AND THE BRIDGE. •Sir,—While all men's eyes and most well thoughts are drawn to the world's battlefields of Europe, and love of race and country is dosninatr lag the national life, there is just the clang% that events and duties which knock at our verJ doors may be forgotten or overlooked. We are told that the Bridge is nearing itgi completion, indeed this is patent to us all, el", I feel I am voicing the spoken and unutterea thoughts of the majority of my townsmen whett,t I venture to suggest that the Council raig', mark its appreciation of the man whose service' have been so faithfully, zealously and ungrudg. ingly given during the many years of its con" ception and consummation. the Alderman Smethurst de-seives" well of - town of his adoption, and it would be at once: graceful and a grateful act to bestow its freed(/' upon him. In many departments of the Couni cil's labours he has given of his best for aloe'. a lifetime, but it is specially with regard to bI9 work and position as chairman of the Bridgegd Committee that he will be best remembered au • appreciated by his fellow citizens. The day of the opening of our fine briag.! draws nigh, and with its coming I sincerl hope will be the announcement that the Coun,„c has honoured itself and one of its veteran hers by inscribing his name on the 11311 of fre men of the borough. " GRATEFUL CITIZLN.</p>
      </div>
