      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0045" n="art0045">
        <head>of Militarism.</head>
        <p>of Militarism.</p>
        <p>The fourth and last of an interesting series of Ilent°_,res. "After the War," was delivered on "iluaY evening at the Christadelphian Meeting x,°°°l, Bold-street, by Mr. Andrew Morris, of nanchester, who took for his subject: "God's el' all for the abolition of every evil: Political, and religious." There .vas a large audi- thee. Mr. Joseph P. Jennings, who occupied ~.LT chair, read as an introduction Psalm lxxii. In which David shows the goodness and glory °f Christ's kingdom and blesses God.</p>
        <p>NATIONAL DEGRADATIONS. Before the present war overshadowed the Said the lecturer, there was a large see- , Vie conditi af tile community e 'twhich was distressed at ans under which the nations laboured. thieve were those who referred particularly to illte burden of militarism, and they were all hnling now a valuable obj,ect lesson on the sub- There had been a maxim that "the best • preserve peace was to prepare for war," so the great nations had been piling up 13:41aMents. The. preparations for wars had en drawing away from the country all the :lest men—the best workers and producers: their maintenance, in gigantic armies, had Tri• Beqneatly fallen upon those who remained. e3' all knew, also, the difficulty there was in ge • tt'llg even the smallest dole from the Govern- , tterrt for any social reforms, and yet tremen- Lle aMounts were spent on militarism. l'ak a another national degradation—dranken- -ess h, • Russia had now prohibited, throughout 1; r whole Empire, the sale of intoxicating ',!luor, because she knew that a sober' army was ;:solutely essential to success, and that the ''ellue would return from other sources. ev9,11 every hand they saw political and social tiat+s; and if these existed in this great Chris- land, what might not the conditions of other d• ritrries be? All attempts to reform society prov_°een. unsuccessful, because if things im- , n_ other.ed in one place they slipped back in •,i ~arl:et God not only had a plan for the abolition h4r4 evil, but had even revealed it. He was to b a people whose character should be in eh ect harmony with those He made—who th°llld be obedient to the divine law. By all °ther creations of God, His law was obeyed real, beasts obeyed it, and it was obeyed in the of Nature and the heavens—and if God's Were transgressed by men, they would have DaY the penalty.</p>
        <p>REGENERATION. It was the world to be regenerated? Ane haa CJews were still here to-day. although t heyl aßes -een persecuted by all lands, through all Tbe.,77e11. England having done., its share.! /toe were in all nations, but not of them; they the. their' own religion, and in England even ir ,crwil poor laer and Boards of Guardians. b efit°riiect of land reform to-day was to give the en to of the land to those who worked it, and Ivo it therefore really belonged. But it mai.ild never have needed adjusting under God's .tberl (Lev. xxv.). His land reform would make it j, 'et day reformers' mouths water; yet • r,-"%been a law of the Jews over 5,000 years "is was going to be revived by the return for :I.Blls Christ to the world; the Jews existed '• Te fulfilment of God's purpose, and Israel -44 Lo be the nucleus of that great political kiss Teial reform (Isaiah ii.). Then, "ye kings, die Son, lest He be angry !" The Atte4, Chaiirnan thanked the public for :their lect„ "ance and attention during the series of</p>
      </div>
