      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <head>ALTAR AND HEARTH.</head>
        <p>ALTAR AND HEARTH.</p>
        <p>Two Things That Count for Everything in Life.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>PRESBYTERIAN DIAMOND JUBILEE.</head>
        <p>PRESBYTERIAN DIAMOND JUBILEE.</p>
        <p>The Diamond Jubilee celebration services in connection with Presbyterianism in Warrington were concluded in St. John's Church on Sunday, the preacher for the day being the Pastor, the Rev. W. Reid. Good congregations assembled both in the morning and evening, and the ser- vices were bright and inspiring. In the evening the Pastor took for his text, "Then shalt thou cause the trumpet of the jubilee to sound on the tenth day of the seventh month; in the day of atonement shll ye make the trumpet sound throughout all your land. And ye shall hallow the fiftieth year and pro- claim liberty throughout all the land unto 411 the ifiliabitants thereof: it shall be a jubilee unto you, and ye shall return every man unto his possession and ye shall return every man unto his family" (Leviticus xxv. 9 and 10). SEPTENNIAL CYCLES IN SCRIPTURAL HISTORY.</p>
        <p>The preacher said he had felt relieved when neither of the two previous preachers had touched on that topic of jubliee, because he felt it to be his own particular province and pre- serve. He felt honoured to meet them that night in celebrating such a great feature in their congregational life. Septennial cycles, the reverend gentleman described as one of the prominent features in Hebrew legislation. They were to be seen in a week of days, a week of weeks, a week of months, a week of years, and a week of weeks of years. Each cycle was found to culminate in a sacred period of rest. He refuted the idea of rest in stagnation, and showed it to be found in work, and to exist in apparent loss which it converted into positive gain, but the experience of true rest depended altogether upon correct relation- ships being established and maintained. When the seventh seven of years had completed its cycle the whole was crowned by a year of jubilee. It commenced on the sacred day of atonement, which lay near to the centre of ~the sacred month, and was declared by the trumpet blast, which one of the later poets described as "The joyful sound." In order to appreciate that description it was necessary to know something of the conditions laid down by the legislation which made provision for a jubilee, and what the true value of that season was to the Hebrews. , THE ONLY LANDOWNER.</p>
        <p>In the first place there was no such thing as private rights in land. The only landowner was Jehovah, Who had, in the first instance, allotted it according to tribe and clan and family. It was natural that in the course of y(er..9 circumstances should arise rendering it necessary to sell, which, if allowed to go on, would, in all probability, mean its complete loss to the tribe, and ultimately the cornering of it by the few. But Hebrew legislation; if carried out, would completely dispose of the difficulty. In the first place the land itself could not be sold. It was crown land in the highest sense, for Jehovah was King. What was sold, therefore, was the usufruct, and the cal- culations made were based upon the crop, and were bounded by the oncoming year of jubilee. Meantime, if it were possible for a relative or for the individual to redeem, the law made pro- vision for such redemption. If it remained im- possible, the year of jubilee brought the trans- action to an end, when the land reverted to Jehovah, Who again placed it at the disposal of the original holders or their descendants. No premium, therefore, was set upon laziness or vice, while enterprise and industry were re- warded. Further, while the existing holders, through indolence, might lose possession, their descendants were not for ever visited with their sins. In God's free gift of jubilee the law of heredity was superseded by that of Grace, and the son came into his chance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>PERSONAL LIBERTY SAFEGUARDED.</head>
        <p>PERSONAL LIBERTY SAFEGUARDED.</p>
        <p>In like manner the preacher showed slavery to have been robbed of its coarsest elements by this legislation, which insisted upon men's ser- vices being bought and sold, and not men them- selves; in the possibility of redemption at any time, and in the certainty of it with the advent of jubilee. The emphasis throughout was laid on the sovereign rights of God in the earth and in men, a fact, said the preacher, while it /night not result in the framing of uniform land laws, would, if remembered, help materially towards solving many problems; as well as on the fact that such legislature emanates only from the relationship existing between God and men in the atonement.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>JUBILEES IN BRITAIN'S STORY.</head>
        <p>JUBILEES IN BRITAIN'S STORY.</p>
        <p>, A jubilee ago England and Russia • stood Ahoulder to shoulder fighting Turkey;' two jubilees ago England and Prussia stood shoulder to shoulder fighting France. Five jubilees ago English Presbyterianism passed ' through the fire, and one jubilee ago the congregation of St. John's came within the embrace of English Presbyterianism. That day they entered upon a new cycle of years, and the question was: "What are we going to make of it?" They had a new life and a redeemed gift given to them to do something with, and the question was what were they going to make of the next 50 years as a people? Would they be content to let it slide and slip, and let their birthright go unim- proved? They had a duty and a responsibility given to them, and they shonld rise to them manfully. How should they bring about im- provement? The only things that mattered were loyalty and faithfulness. There were churches and congregations who had larger com- munion rolls and balance sheets than they had in that church, but there was not a church in Christendom with a larger prospect, if it would only seize it and be loyal and use it as a gift from God. There were two things that counted for everything in life that went side by side— the altar and the hearth. They should be true and loyal to both and they were bound to win. Mr. Clay sang "Nearer, my God, to Thee," in effective fashion, and the choir were responsible for an excellent rendition of the anthem "What I are these?" They also sang special hymns. Mr. G. H. Owen presided at the organ. OVER £6O RAISED FOR THE CHURCH. Financial Result of the Celebrations. The Diamond Jubilee celebrations were con- cluded on Thursday evening, when a large , number. of members and friends of the church assembled to participate in an enjoyable social evening. Brief and bright speeches, all in a thankful and optimistic vein, were made, espe- cial pleasure being expressed more than once at the fact that numerous well-wishers and former members of the congregation who have left the town, have not forgotten the church's jubilee, as was evidenced by letters read by the Pastor, the Rev. Wm. Reid, who presided over the pro- ceedings. The addresses were given by Modera- tors J. G. Tosh and Wm. Jobson, both of whom came from Manchester, as representing the Presbytery. Benefits Accruing from the Jubilee. _</p>
        <p>The Chairman recalled the fact- that the Man- chester Presbytery gave a grant of £6O twards securing the site which the local church now occupied, and said he took that, the first oppor- tunity he had had, of tendering their thanks to the Presbytery, through the two representa- tives present that night. Mr. Reid also said that he had received congratulatory messages from friends in different parts and from minis- ters of some of the local churches.</p>
        <p>Referring to the benefits accruing from the celebrations, the speaker said that one was the fact that the church had been brought more prominently before the citizens of the town. Through the special services members of differ- ing denominations, Anglican and others, had visited the church, had listened attentively, and had joined reverently in the services. With regard to the financial benefits they had secured, they set out, he said, to obtain £6O, and that amount had been raised. (Applauge.) The col- lections for the three Sundays totalled £35. Several ladies of the congregation, by a private effort, had handed over ,R 6 to the fund, whilst the Sunday school was responsible for £3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>Minister's Successful Personal Effort.</head>
        <p>Minister's Successful Personal Effort.</p>
        <p>In addition, he (the speaker) stated at the commencement of the celebrations that he would try to collect ..e2O, and he was pleased to report that he had actually raised .222. (Applause.) All the figures were only approximate. Fur- ther, the income had been increased by about £ll by the sale of the copies of the booklet, "The History of Warrington Presbyterianism." That brought the total up to about £77. As a set-off to this there was an expenditure amount- ing to about .115, which left a balance to their credit of about £6l or £62. Moderators Tosh and Robson also addressed the meeting.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
        <p>shield in front of the German troops as they advanced; many of them were killed by bullets from Belgian rifles. At Allost, lam credibly informed, the same cruel plan was adopted, but the Belgian soldiers cried out to their compatriots in Flemish, "Lie. down." This these did, and the soldiers fired over them and killed many of the Germans. The blacksmith of the village of Sempst, and his son, chose to be shot rather than act as guides for the enemy. The race of heroes is not dead. A farm, in which some Belgian soldiers had hidden, was burnt, and the remains of the son of the house were found inside. The brutes had cut off his legs, and similar atrocities were committed in many. parts._ _</p>
        <p>slo-uld like here to say that having left for Ostend early in the conflict, I did not witness any of the atrocities narrated, but I haye been careful to give only samples of those which are well attested. It is further right to say that there were some Germans, both amongst the commanders and privates, who behaved as civilised men. As to the reprisals by the Bel- gians, I believe there were a few, but these occurred only when cruelties and outrages too terrible to relate were inflicted upon the civi- lians. Never in history has a nation sunk so low as the Germans in their treatment of the Belgians, and, as a Belgian, may I be pardoned if I express the opinion that seldom, if ever, has a nation risen so high as the Belgian people, led by their gallant Bing, in resisting to the death the threats, bribes and cajolry of the Germans. SPIRIT OF THE BELGIAN PEOPLE.</p>
        <p>The spirit of the Belgian people was expressed by our noble King, who said: "To conquer Belgium they must first pass over my dead body." After Louvain and Malines it was Termonde's turn. It was in proximity to this town that the Germans had managed to throw a bridge over the Scheldt. Three times they tried to cross the river; three times was their bridge • destroyed by the Belgians. Furious with the check at the bridge, they bombarded Termonde, which had to surrender, and the Commander ordered the destruction of the town. The German soldiers carried incendiary boxes containing compressed paraffin (I think), which filled the house with inflammable gas. Then they threw in lighted fuses, which fired the houses. The hospital was at first spared, thanks to the supplication of the nuns, but when the Commander heard this, the soldiers were sent back with orders to destroy it. Several of the patients were carried into the garden and saved, but others perished in the flames. This act of vandalism, accomplished on the spot where Termonde had stood, left absolutely nothing. Has our world ever before witnessed such tragedies? Can even the Dark Ages pro- duce instances' of cruelty worse than these? The invasion of Belgium, as well as the de- struction and outrages, continued. Namur capitulated, the forts could not resist the German heavy guns. Our regiments were decimated by the howitzers, which had been placed fifteen miles away, so that our men did not even see the enemy. Of the 30,000 defending the city, it is said 16,000 were killed or wounded. The others saved themselves by escaping through the German lines during the night, abandoning everything. As they had no food they had to eat raw beets, cabbages and potatoes, from the fields. At last they reached the French Army and were received with every kindness, looked after, cared for, and sent to Le Havre, thence to Ostend. I saw them there, these poor men, emaciated, pale, after so many privations. After a few, days' rest at Ostend they joined the army which went to Antwerp to defend that city. The enemy had placed his big 17in. guns to attack the south-east fort. Malines, with Fort Walhem forming the key of the position, suffered the first and most The town was bombarded in three places at once, and'great fires burst out on all sides, the glare of which was seen from all the villages around. Later, part of the town was bombarded from our own forts, with the objeet of dislodging the enemy who had entered, but this failed. The forts themselves were destroyed, and with the aid of 200 guns of great calibre, all concentrated in one point, a passage was made through the first line of defence. The passage of the River Nethe being free, the enemy passed through the second line of forts. The position was unten- able, and the Belgian Army, whose retreat was covered by English Marines, fell back in good order to Ostend. Here they arrived, the heroes of Liege, Haelen, D'Eghezee, Aerschot, and Malines, tired, attenuated by two months of war, carrying their arms encrusted, with blood, their uniforms in rags and black with mud, but on which, here and there, shone a cross of the Order of Leopold, that King Albert him- self had pinned on them, in acknowledgment of their brave deeds—and when we saw this we saw nothing else. However, it was only human that 'they felt ashamed when they met the British troops in Ostend in their smart uni- forms, fresh and clean, and I saw them looking at them with eyes full of envy. But these are the lads that had covered themselves with glory as well as with mud. HOPE RESTORED.</p>
        <p>With their number reduced to half, officers and simple soldiers went to take their place with the Allies at the end of the long line, ready to defend the last inch of Belgian terri- tory, and, if need be, to give their lives for it. This, at the call of a King who will be famous in history for his courage. Meanwhile the enemy pressing his conquest invaded the whole of Flanders, enveloped Ghent and Bruges, and the situation became every day more critical. This greatly increased the stream of fugitives making for the coast. The officials of Malines had come to Ostend and publicly advised their townspeople who had settled in Ostend to leave for England. "It is impossible," they said, "to live at Malines any longer; the town is destroyed, abandoned and pillaged. Make no illusions; go away and do not come back till we call you." The next day before daybreak we left for what, to us, was an unknown shore. As we walked to the steamer, in the light of the street lamps, we saw a sad sight—our Belgian soldiers sleeping on the pavements of the street. We stopped for a moment, saying to ourselves, "we also are without shelter, and do not know where to lay our heads." At eight o'clock in the morning, after a keen struggle, for the quay was crowded, and we were hampered by our bundles, we found ourselves on deck on one of the steamers. When we saw the Belgian coast slowly vanishing out of our sight, a terrible emotion made the tears run down our cheeks—we thought how sad it is to be without a home, and without a Fatherland. We landed in hospitable England, where the people re- ceived us with so much good feeling, pity, charity and love. After a few weeks in your hospitable land we have caught something of the calm, confident spirit of your countrymen, hope again prunes her wings and soars, and, it ;nay be, we will again find our home, and do our part in helping to build a fairer and a better 43elgium.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>PENALISING A PATRIOT.</head>
        <p>PENALISING A PATRIOT.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett and Education Committee's Parijmony.</p>
        <p>_ At the monthly meeting of the Warrington Education Committee on Monday evening Alder- man Bennett drew attention to the following minute submitted by the School Management Sub-committee: " A letter was read from Mr. John H. Foulkes, certificated assistant master in the Boys' Department of the Evelyn-street Council School, and it was resolved that, in the event of his joining His Majesty's Forces, leave of absence be granted to him during the period of his military service, and that his nominee be paid the sum of 15s. per week during that period. It was also resolved that the question of the payments made to teachers who are absent on military service be reconsidered at the next meeting." "If a man gives up his profession," observed the Alderman, "and goes to fight for his coun- try and risks his life, he ought not to be in any worse position than if he remained, at home in peace and quietness." It seemed to him that the amount they proposed to pay Mr. Foulkes, even when added to the amount he would get for soldiering, would came to nothing like the salary the teacher would be drawing in the ordinary way. Ile felt that he was expressing the sincere opinion of the committee when he said that he thought no teacher should suffer financially on account of his patriotism. If on top of the horrors of warfare such a man had to suffer financial anxiety he (the speaker) thought the committee were asking more than they could fairly expect. He thought that in a case of this kind it was up to the committee to do everything they could to make the earn- bee as easily as possible. Answering on behalf of the committee con- cerned, Canon Stevenson said that the matter was to be reconsidered at the next meeting, and assured Alderman Bennett that the words he had spoken would have ever: Consideration.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>How Defeated Germans Took Revenge Upon Unarmed Citizens.</head>
        <p>How Defeated Germans Took Revenge Upon Unarmed Citizens.</p>
        <p>FROM A PEIIKETH REFUGEE'S DIARY.</p>
        <p>I need not describe the beginning, of this terrible war—this all the world knows. I begin when my own country became concerned. Ger- many, pretending that France was concentrating her army to destroy Givet, a town within the southern border of Belgium, asked a free pas- sage through Belgian territory, so as to be able to attack the French army massed on the higher Meuse. Belgium indignantly refused this pro- position, and resolved to defend her neutrality. Immediately the order was given to mobilise the army. The calling of the soldiers was done by ringing all the bells, and this in the middle of the night, which made a very terrifying im- pression. When daylight came we saw nothing but soldiers, who, after their farewells to wives, children, and sweethearts, left, full of courage, to defend their country and homes. 35,000 BELGIANS v. 120,000 GERMANS.</p>
        <p>The mobilisation was very successful, and was completed in forty-eight hours. The rush for the army depot stores was enormous. There the soldiers gathered, some crying, some singing, some swearing, carrying swords and guns, and adjusting capes, hats, &amp;c. There stand Reserv- ists calling out " A has les Alboches" ("Down with the Germans"). Old comrades in arms meet again after years of separation. There are great shakings of hands and talks of the grand exploits they would accomplish together,Priests, monks, nurses, stretcher-bearers of the Red Cross, and soldiers mix in a fraternal commu- nity. It seems as if this terrible -disaster had made all men brothers. King Albert, in his manifesto to the army, said: " Flemish, remem- ber the battle of the Golden Spurs; and you, Wallons, remember the six hundred Franchi- montois." This aroused their patriotism, and four thousand recruits volunteered at once to I march to the frontier. The day war was de-, I clared, some affirm the day previous, the enemy violated our neutrality by crossing our frontier, land before Liege our soldiers attacked the in- vaders with spirit and made the vanguard of their proud army stagger—this colossal machine for destroying life which had been in prepara- tion for forty years. The struggle was terrible. The enemy, to whom a quick passage meant so much, was not yet in possession of his biggest guns, and had to resort to milling huge masses lof men against the still intact Liege forts. Four picked German Army Corps, 120,000 strong, com- posed of the finest of the German Army, fought the 35,000 Belgians. The latter, being well shel- tered, were able with little hurt to themselves, to drive the Germans back several times. Some- times during the night the Belgians allowed the Germans to come almost up to the forts and then opened fire and swept them away. Four thousand Prussians were killed and more dis- abled, and when the Germans asked for an armistice to bury their dead, General Leman made answer, "You wanted the show, and it must go on !" Faced with such a gallant de- fence, the Germans could not get through, and retired northwards along the Meuse. The delay was of inestimable value to the Allies. Furious with this defeat, and pretending an attack was made by civilians on the military, they destroyed the little town of Vise, shooting and assassinat- ing many of the inhabitants. This was the first example of the way in which Germany was going to conduct this war. Vise is situated between Liege and Maestricht, near the Dutch frontier, and here a bridge was built over the Meuse by which the Germans entered Belgium. Meanwhile the struggle around Liege continued, news of the conflict spread, and in our quiet little town of Malines the people got more ner- vous every day. Malines, fifteen miles south of Antwerp, and practically in the outer ring of forts, was a great centre for supplies, and daily hundreds of heavy motors were despatched to the front delivering food and ammunition. Sometimes the noise was deafening, the cars, all covered with mud, looked like monster animals bounding over the roads in the morning fog. The inhabitants of Malines, anxious and de- pressed, arose before daylight each day, ques- tioning each other as to the issues ol the con- flict. Our brave little soldiers at Liege resisted marvellously, and were the first to prove that the Germans were not invincible. One of our soldiers who was badly wounded came back from Liege to our town. He had been fighting for five days and nights, and told us, "The Germans are cowards. You should see them flying away when our boys get at them. When England and France have come to our aid we will throw them all into the Rhine as meat for the fish." This was the spirit animating our little army from the beginning of the hostilities. Their success at Liege exalted their patriotism, and daily more volunteers enlisted. These volunteers were sent to Malines to the depots to be equipped and drilled.</p>
        <p>BELGIAN KING'S SORROW FOR HIS PEOPLE. Two years previously our army had been doubled, but officers and drill instructors were scarce in all ranks, so nitich so that these volun- teers had often to be drilled by simple soldiers and boy cadets. But all went well. These courageous volunteers did their best and were eager to get to the front. I saw them daily drilling before our door, hundreds of grown men obeying the instructions of lads in their teens. Among the volunteers we saw tradesmen and artisans, peasants and gentlemen. ,At resting time all the townsfolk busied themselves in offering refreshments, food, sweets and tobacco. There was a rivalry who would treat them best. The poorest arranged tables in the middle of the streets, where the good housewives tended our soldiers, giving them what comfort they could, including warm foot baths, much appre- ciated after their long marches. Meanwhile the Germans crossed the Meuse near the Dutch fron- tier, their cavalry had advanced upon Tongres and Hasselt, massacring the inhabitants. At Haelen, a little town near Aerschot, they met in battle the Belgian Army. Once more the Belgians inflicted great losses on the Germans; it is said nearly 3,000 were killed. The day after this defeat another division of the enemy went through the same experience in the south of Brabant. These victories raised the courage of our nation, and the newspapers told of the glory of these deeds. But after tile publica- tion of this news there was an ominous silence. The military staff had taken the precaution to allow only the circulation of favourable news. Our Queen left Brussels for Antwerp, where she was soon followed by the members of the Gov- ernment with the national treasures and State documents. A rumour got about that the King purposed residing at the chateau of Senator Empain at Malines. Early' the following morn— ing I went as far as this chateau, and on my way down the King's motor car passed me. In the afternoon the military staff, which had been quartered in Louvain, settled in the Hotel de Ville, Malines. Later there arrived infantry and artillery. Soon followed a procession of refugees from the east, looking weary and miser- able. They made a pitiable sight, carrying or wheeling in barrows the few household goods they had got together. Among them were very old men and women, little children, women who carried their babies in their arms and some who would soon be mothers. There were several cases of premature births of stillborn babes. Some walked, others were in a variety of vehi- cles drawn by dogs, donkeys, or mules. Unex- pectedly King. Albert returned from an inter- view with his generals and found himself ine.the midst of these refugees. He looked at them, and in his face was visible great emotion. He suffered, perhaps, as much as any, for he has a tender, as well as a brave, soul. In the even- ing, when it was bed time. I could not find peace. All night long I heard this terrible procession , of human misery, tramping westward through ' Malines. I had only just got to sleep when a loud ringing at the front door bell awoke us, and someone called my wife. When she re- turned to the room she said, and I shall never forget the anxiety in her voice: "My dear, we must fly at once; one of the officers of the general staff, a friend of Made- moiselle —, has told her there must be no delay."</p>
        <p>A LAST LOOK AT HOME. I had always thought the Germans would march straight on to France without entering llalines or Antwerp; I. therefore, was quite stupefied when I heard that it was our turn to fly. Consternation came over me. I had a feel- ing that it was the last time I would rest in my home. I knew now why the papers had kept silence; it was in order not to alarm the people. The facts were these—once the Germans got their powerful artillery placed, they were able to destroy the forts which dominated the Meuse at the-north and south of Liege. In crossing the bridge which the Germans had thrown over the river, their troops met the Belgian Army in the middle of the country between Louvain, Tirlemont, and Waterloo. The principal action took place between Diest and Aerschot. The Belgians, strongly entrenched, resisted heroically 'and killed thousands of the enemy, but the pressure of enormous masses of Germans com- ing upon them made it impossible for them to hold their ground, and they had to give way and seek shelter in retreat upon Antwerp. A short time after we heard that Louvain was being destroyed, ineendiarism and massacre were going on, and all the inhabitants fled in the middle of the night. This explains why all that night through the rolling of the carts filled with refugees was heard hurrying in the direction of Flanders. It was our turn next; we were a family of five—myself and wife, two young daughters, and an aged aunt of my own. Im- mediately we began collecting the few things we could carry with us. My wife was prostrated, my old aunt stood with folded hands, anxious and deathly white, and I saw by the candle light she was weeping. It was breaking her heart having to leave the home she loved so much. We hastily buried our family papers and valuable jewellery in the ground. We were now ready to start, and gave a last look towards the house in which we had all lived so long and happily together, and which held for us so many sacred memories. The house for which we had worked so hard to rear and furnish we had now to leave. We do not know whether it still stands or is levelled to the ground. Morning began to dawn. We marched on with bowed heads and heavy hearts, carrying our hastily put together parcels. Only our little Rehta, un- conscious of what it all meant, seemed happy, joyfully repeating to herself, "We are going to Ostend." When we arrived at the station the train for Ohent (the junction for Ostend) was already full, and another train had to be got ready. A terrible scramble took place to secure places in this train, and it was wietahsagnrtesatofdtiffihe- icnugltyintitilalet we found dsrwooemre. rtilannythpis train, stand- neighbourhood. These poor people, chased by the Germans, had walked all night and were very weary. Opposite me were two young girls (sisters), with eyes red from crying', who sat limp and forlorn on their luggage. It was heart-breaking to see their misery. It appears that when ready to leave their'village they had met about thirty German soldiers, who chased them, and the girls got separated from their parents. Another woman, sitting opposite me, had fled with her nine children, the youngest of which was only a few weeks old. She also had lost her husband in the flight. War is ever a terrible thing, but war-as practised by the Ger- mans is hell let loose. Many families have been separated, and many. anxious hearts are in Eng- land to-day who know not whether the rest ef their family have reached this haven of refuge. There are some such in the "Belgian Home" I from which I write. How these poor women in the train cried! They did not know where to go. We advised them to get off at Ghent, and to ask assistance of the Refugee Committee, which had been constituted there. Our case was bad enough, but thdOe` were thousands much worse. We little thought that two months later we, too, would have to apply to the committee in London for aid. We journeyed to Ostend through the green fields of Flanderi, which would also in a few months later be devastated by the German Huns.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>RAGE AND RUIN.</head>
        <p>RAGE AND RUIN.</p>
        <p>_ "Fly! It is time," the officer had told us, and he knew why. He knew that not content with burning and pillaging the beautiful cities of Flanders, and the smiling districts in Wallonie, the enemy, in his rage, would leave a track of ruins wherever he passed. After we had been a few days in Ostend, our brother-in- law arrived. He had remained in Malines until it was bombarded. With his family he had hidden in a cellar in a terrible state of anxiety. He had heard shells exploding over his head. A piece of shrapnel came through the roof of a neighbour's house and buried itself in the mattress of the bed in which this neigh- bour was asleep. He did not sleep any more that night. At each discharge of a distant gun a muffled sound was heard, followed by a fierce whiizing, and then an explosion like a thunder bolt. The result of these guns was such that often a whole house was destroyed by one shot. Then a commotion was heard in the town street. It was the Belgian Artillery passing through at a gallop, the Infantry fol- lowing. Our troops made a sortie out of Ant- werp to try and force the enemy out of Malines. A furious battle ensued, which lasted till mid- day. The enemy retreated and was driven back as far as Vilvorde, four miles from Malines. In the afternoon my brother-in-law saw passing motor cars filled with wounded, some dripping blood; and a little later he heard the bugle and drum. It was the sounds of victory .of our soldiers!.</p>
        <p>• Ah, 'the fine lads! They entered Malines in triumph, they had covered their guns with flowers, and the last detachment were followed by carts filled with war trophies, guns, casques, lances, and bloodstained sabres. The inhabi- tants, filled with enthusiasm, emerging from their hiding-places, offered them all sorts of refreshments. The town of Malines, however, had suffered heavily; mans houses were de- stroyed, others had suffered enormously. The churches especially had been aimed at. The Cathedral of St: Rombaut was crippled With boilibs, the magnificent windows and specially valuable pictures destroyed. Only the massive church tower, dominating the little town and the environs, had resisted. It seemed to brave the fury of the Germans. Their bombs had flattened against its massive walls as if they were snowballs thrown by a child's hands, and the church bells still continued to give their cheerful peals in the air. These church bells are said to be the finest in Belgium, and Jef Denyn is considered the finest church bell- ringer in the world, i.e., by the people of Malines.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>GERMAN REVENGE-UPON WOMEN. AND</head>
        <p>GERMAN REVENGE-UPON WOMEN. AND</p>
        <p>CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>The following day the 'Germans, having re- ceived reinforcements, made a counter-attack, and, notwithstanding the Belgians' tenacity, the, Belgian Army had to evacuate the town, and the Germans again took possession. The enemy managed to destroy the bridge on the Senile, cutting off our retreat. A panic ensued; many of our soldiers were drowned in the river, others rushed back through the town, and were mixed with the flying inhabitants It was everyone for himself—men, women and children, in an indescribable confusion, hurried towards Antwerp. A few remained, and next day there arrived- about one hundred refugees from Lou-. vain, telling terrible stories of ruin and out- rage. The German soldiers, the day before, having been forced to retreat from Malines, had fallen back upon Louvain, and, out of revenge for their defeat, behaved like Huns and Vandals, and sacked, pillaged, and burned the town. In a few hours the finest parts of this ancient University city were destroyed. Some of the German officers, drunk with wine, set fire to the beautiful Gothic church, the University, and the Library, and many trea- sures eofsahia•tmbeecoafme Germany.ashes,d stohmise f ever- lastingtqthtehein habitants were made prisoners and sent to Gerpiany, and their wives, many of whom had beeß, along with their babies, bound for several hours to the railway lines, showed to the people of Malines 'the marks on their wrists left by the cords by which they had been tied. Other miserable fugitives arrived from Sempst, a small village in the neighbourhood, where a number of the inhabitants, women and children as well as men, had been placed as a human</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>How a Great Church Arose from the</head>
        <p>How a Great Church Arose from the</p>
        <p>Ashes of a Mighty Empire.</p>
        <p>The story of the decline and downfall of the Roman Empire and the growth alongside it of the great Romish Church were sketched in in- structive and interesting fashion for the benefit of a large audience at the Co-operativi Hall on Saturday night by Mr. J. E. Phythian, who, under the auspices of the Warrington Museum Committee, is delivering a series of popular lec- tures on "Rome, the Eternal City: Its Story and Its Monuments."</p>
        <p>Saturday night's lecture was the second of the series and dealt more particularly with early Christian and medieval Rome.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <head>ROMAN AND BRITISH EMPIRES COMPARED.</head>
        <p>ROMAN AND BRITISH EMPIRES COMPARED.</p>
        <p>People might, said the lecturer in the course of his remarks—which were illustrated by beau- tiful lantern slides—ask the question: How does the Roman Empire compare with the British Empire of to-day? There was a great difference. Rome conquered all the civilised States within her reach, and a very considerable number of the barbarian tribes that were for ever pouring from the East, westwards into what we called to-day Russia, and through Russia into France and Germany, and, indeed, into our own little island. On the other hand, a great part of our Empire consisted of lands that were either un- occupied or were occupied by uncultured natives, with the great exception of India. For our Empire to resemble that of,Rome we should have to rule over the whole of Europe, at least. There were people who suffered from bad attacks of nerves, who thought that the Kaiser intended to emulate the Roman Ctesars and to try to rule the whole civilised world from „England. He confessed that the prospect had Aver frightened him, for the simple reason that the Germans could not rule other people; they were too well satisfied with themselves, and people who were too well satisfied with themselves could never manage other people properly.' If the Kaiser ever managed to set up his rule in London, within fifty years we should have taken over the whole show, Germany as well, because we had the faculty of accommodating ourselves to cir- cumstances.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>ROME AT THE HEIGHT OF HER SPLENDOUR.</head>
        <p>ROME AT THE HEIGHT OF HER SPLENDOUR.</p>
        <p>Continuing, the lecturer said that the great days of Rome were those which coincided with the beginning of the Christian era. It was later that the Empire reached its greatest extent, but it was not always a sign of strength to have far- reaching territory, and in the days of her great- est splendour Rome was already beginning to be weak. Rome touched the height of her splendour under the Emperor Afigustus—the Emperor' who issued the edict that the whole 1 Empire was to be taxed, and that in consequence the people were to be enumerated, the people at that time owing tribute to Rome including Mary and Joseph, because Syria, which included Judea and Jerusalem, then formed part of the territory under the sway of the CEesars. There- after came the decline and fall of the Roman Empite. At the time there were hordes of strong, hardy, wandering people in France and Germany who, when there was no longer room for them in one place, trekked to another—as did thegßoers in South Africa—going ever west- ward from the countries of the East. These barbarians began pouring into the Roman Em- pire, breaking down its civilisation, but at the same time being impressed by it, and gradually fitting themselves into it. At last, in 476, one of these barbarian tribes deposed the Boy Em- peror, and the Western Empire came to an end, but there remained, until 1453, the Eastern Empire, with Constantinople as its capital, which came to an end when the Turks besieged, took and sacked the city.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0061" n="art0061">
        <head>WILL CONSTANTINOPLE RETURN TO CHRISTIANITY?</head>
        <p>WILL CONSTANTINOPLE RETURN TO CHRISTIANITY?</p>
        <p>The entry of Turkey into the present terrible war had been received with considerable re- joicing in some quarters, for there were people who said that now the Turk could be turned out of Europe. There still stood in Constantinople the great church, known as the Church of Holy Wisdom, which was built by the Emperor Jus- tinian in 537. As one of the leading Church- men in this country was saying the other day, he hoped that the church, at present used as a Mohammedan mosque, would, when he next visited Constantinople, have reverted to its original purpose, viz., that of a place of Chris- tian worship. Whilst the power of the Roman Empire was passing, through splendour, to decay, a new power was arising in its heart, and that power was Christianity. To begin with the Church was a brotherhood of poor folk, but gradually the tenets of the sect spread and the people of all classes began to realise that in Christianity was a brotherhood of hopes and faith, a prospect of joy and fellowship in this life, and a great happiness in the life to come. Small wonder that the old myths were discarded, that the philosophies of the time— philosophies that gave the people but little com- fort—were dropped, and that the Christian faith was received joyfully by ever-increasing num- bers of people, and that last, by Imperial edict, it was recognised as a lawful thing. Then it was that the Church began to grow worldly, that it became a political power, stooping to conquer. The Church became strong, and from being persecuted it came to be the persecutor. In order to hold together it had to make rules; in order to prevent divisions it had to propound doctrines, belief in which was enjoined upon the faithful. It came into contact with other religions, and it had forcibly to try and bring them within the pale of its own.</p>
        <p>SPIRITUAL POWER SUCCEEDS TEMPORAL</p>
        <p>Standards of orthodoxy were setup, and gradually the power of the Papacy grew up, and the Bishop of Rome came to be the bishop of bishops, until, within their own time, the doc- trine of the infallibility of the Pope had been evolved. So, as the old power of Rome had decreased, its new power as the centre of a great religious force had gradually risen, and still, in a sense, the world-wide power of the old city was maintained. The Holy Roman Empire had become a mere shadowy thing, but the power of the Pope remained. This evening, at 8 o'clock. Mr. Phythian will deal with the Rome of the Renaissance.</p>
        <p>Photographic Society. Mr. Frost's Nature study lectures are always welcomed by the members of the Warrington Photographic Society, and as it is two years since he last favoured them, his address on Tuesday on the "Struggle for Existence" was particularly appreciated. He showed by means of an excellent series of pictures on the screen how the favoured creatures, whether animals or plants, were provided by Nature with means to protect themselves, to preserve their species and to secure nourishment where otherwise they would fail and ultimately perish. Coming to details, the lecturer, showing his skill as microscopist, illustrated his points by special reference to the bee coinmunity. and again to the construction of feathers used by birds f" different purposes, and, lastly, how the skill of man may be brought into play to assist Nature re in her selection of the fittest. Many slides, particularly those representing on the nest, or approacng it to feed her young, were specially fine. *l4 the f bird</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0062" n="art0062">
        <head>FIFTY YEARS A LOCAL PREACHER.</head>
        <p>FIFTY YEARS A LOCAL PREACHER.</p>
        <p>Death and Funeral of Mr. William</p>
        <p>Gleave.</p>
        <p>Few men have played a more prominent part in the history of local Nonconformity than Mr. William Gleave, whose remains were laid to rest in Warrington Cemetery on Tuesday after- noon, in the presence of a numerous gathering of relatives and friends. For nearly half a century he had been "on the plan" of the War- rington Wesleyan Circuit. His appointment was dated April, 1865, his initial authorisation to preach being worded as follows: "You are authorised to make trial of your gifts in calling sinners to repentance, according to the direction of this plan. May God grant you His blessing.—Yours affectionately, Jno. Morris." On trial at the same time were Mr. E. J. Guest and Mr. Swift. The circuit then con-</p>
        <p>sisted of Warrington, Lymm, Moore, Preston Brook, Whitley, Antrobus, Appleton, Hatton, Penketh, Pulgate, and Sankey Bridges. Mr. Gleave was then number 24 on the list of preachers, but at the time of his death was second, Mr. S. Moss, of Stockton Heath, being his only senior.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0063" n="art0063">
        <head>PREACHED 3,000 SERMONS.</head>
        <p>PREACHED 3,000 SERMONS.</p>
        <p>During the half-century which covered his active career, Mr. Gleave preached something like three thousand sermons. It is safe to say no mit was better known in the Warrington circuit. Among his contemporaries were Messrs. E. J. Guest, A. Lamplugh, W. J. Lawton, Elijah Ford, and Peter Stringer, and others who predeceased him. Mr. Gleave was the oldest class member and the senior trustee of Bold-street Wesleyan Church. He formed one of the procession from the old chapel in Bank- street (where local Weskyans worshipped in the 'forties) to the much more imposing edifice in Bold-street sixty-four years ago. Nearly all the family were musical, and Mr. Gleave's father, as well as himself, brothers and sister (Mrs. J. Gandy), at different times sang in the choir.</p>
        <p>Right up to the time of his death Mr. Gleave enjoyed excellent health. He was actually out the evening before he passed away. In the night he had a heart attack, but it was not thought serious, and in the afternoon he died as he was dozing in his armchair. He was 75 years of age, and leaves a widow and five children.</p>
        <p>For many years Mr. Gleave carried on business as a baker and confectioner at 21, Buttermarket- street (which is now in ruins), and later also in Sankey-street and Latchford.</p>
        <p>I</p>
        <p>IMPRESSIVE FUNERAL SERVICE. The funeral service took place in Bold-street Church, being conducted by the Rev. W. T. Derry (superintendent minister) and the Rev. • H. Williamson. Amongst those present either at thed.church or graveside were: Mrs. Gleave (widow), Mr. W. A. Weave (Southampton), Mr. Walter Gleave (Portsmouth), sons: Miss E. Gleave and Miss L. Gleave (daughters), Mr. John Gandy (brother-in-law) and Mrs. Gandy (sister), Mrs. Thomas Gleave (sister-in-law), Messrs. J. T. Pattison, G. Toft, C. E. Toft, C. Rawling, F. Pendlebury and Harry Gandy (nephews), Mrs. Ride, Miss Danks, Mrs. Hayes, Mrs. Jones and Miss Maggie Gleave (nieces), Alderman Arthur Bennett, and Messrs. G.\ Weaver, G. Wallington, F. Wright, S. Moss, I. Johnson, M. Osborne, J. Robinson, W. H. Bay- ley, J. Upson and J. W. Sherlock. The service was opened by an impressive ren- dering of "0 rest in the Lord" by Mr. Frank Hickman, who also played the Dead March in "Saul" at the cloSe.</p>
        <p>Amongst the many floral emblems received were those from: His wife and children, "In loving remembrance"; "With love to gran Ipa. from Reggie, Douglas and Brian" (Southamp- ton); "To dear grandpa, from Reggie 'awl Ronnie" (Portsmouth); Mrs. Toft, Mr. and Mrs. Pendlebury, Mr. and Mrs. Gandy, Mr. and Mrs. Robinson, Mr. and Mrs. Bailey, Miss Brown, Mr. and Mrs. Charles Toft, Mr. and Mrs. Banks, Mr. and Mrs. Ride. The other surviving son, Mr. F. C. Gleave, is at present on active service with the Expedi- tionary Force. The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons, Church-street.</p>
      </div>
