      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>WIUMWING OVER HONOURS.</head>
        <p>WIUMWING OVER HONOURS.</p>
        <p>We have received several letters this week with reference to a proposal that was made at the General Purposes Committee of the Town Council oil Monday, that there should be a some- what wholesale addition to our already rather long list of Freemen of the Borough.</p>
        <p>The discussion which followed the proposal, we are told,, resulted in a worse scene than any of the. scenes fOr which the Council have become .famous.</p>
        <p>So far as we are concerned, we do not discuss the merits of the gentlemen who have been named to rec,eive the highest honour the town is able fo bestow upon them; though we stand by the original idea that the freedom of a city or borough should be reserved for men of outstanding achievement. A French coming back from a success- ful campaign, a native who has become Lord Chancellor or Prime Minister would be honoured and give honour by lithe conferring of the freedom of a borough..</p>
        <p>But it is not a question whether or not gentlemen who have done local work should be elevated to this tighest pinnacle in municipal life. Camied to its logical conclusion, as we all do our best for the town, such a process would mean that most of us would ultimately beyreemen, and the honour would become as valuable as the German Iron Cross.</p>
        <p>The main and only question is, ,should we, at the present, time, when nationally we are fighting for our very ' existence, at a time when our enemies are actually threatening to invade our I shores, be wrangling over conferring honours upon ourselves and our friends? A political truce was called, but in Warrington the calm appears to have been made use of to bring forward proposals that in honest and straight- forward controversial times would not have been suggested. It is a para- dox that under cover of internal peace we are waging war more intensely than during real war-time. . So serious a matter is the creation of a Freeman that it is necessary by law to hold a special meeting of the Coun- cil to confer the honour, and the pro- posal has to receive a two-thirds majority of those present voting. So impetuous were the proposers upon this occasion that the selected names were forthwith submitted after the fashion that is known as "cut-and- dried." An honour of this. kind, for its own sake, must .not come in this fashion, and the law has provided the</p>
        <p>R, SATIJRDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
        <p>two-thirds majority check of which we have spoken. How the Council, as a whole, viewed the matter may be gathered from the fact that when Alderman TINNION had succeeded in carrying a resolution by a majority of one that a committee of seven be Appointed to go into the ques- tion, no one would consent to serve on the committee.</p>
        <p>There the matter rests awaiting the holding of a special meeting of the Council, and we sincerely hope that such a meeting will not be held until the war has ceased and a troubled nation has leisure to concern itself with these infinitely smaller issues, which ought not now to be raised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>THE</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
          <desc>skipped/gap: fewer than 20 non-control characters after cleanup</desc>
        </gap>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>COUNCILLOR CRAIK AND FAIRFIELD WARD.</head>
        <p>COUNCILLOR CRAIK AND FAIR- FIELD WARD.</p>
        <p>Fairfield Ward feels specially aggrieved by the doings of the Council on the oth inst. For some reason that does not appear on the surface they " dis Missed ' Councillor CRAIK from the Watch Committee, the Street Im- provement Committee, and the Paving and Sewerage Committee. The cry was for new blood, irrespective of the quality of the old blood and the ques- tionable quality of the new. An almost complete analogy would be to recall FRENCH and JELLICOE and pro- mote privates to their places, just to give the privates a chance of proving that they could become Generals and Admirals. It was the " chance " the new blood was after rather than a desire for the dismissal of old,er and tried men that brought about some of the ridiculous decisions, and equally ridiculous pro- posals ; but the best interests of the town were not served. The removal of Councillor CRAIK from these com- mittees has left Fairfield Ward with- out a representative on the Watch Committee or the Street Improvement Committee. Councillor CRAIK was by no means a passive member on the Watch Committee ; he was one of those in whom we could look for a champion of the public interest in police matters. The streets in Fairfield Ward are amongst the best in town, Councillor CRAIK having been very active in the interests of his constituents in this respect. Why stop him. in his good work and rob the ward of its represen- tation? Paving and sewerage in that rapidly-growing locality is of vital moment ; the only representative of the ward how on the Paving and Sewerage Committee is Councillor TAYLOR, who, most unfortunately, is prevented by ill-health from attending the meetings as regularly as he might otherwise do.</p>
        <p>We have heard two excuses for those who have so grievously wronged Councillor CRAIK and the ward he has served so well for twelve years. One was that he was not present at. the meeting, and that being out of sight he was out of mind and forgotten unintentionally. But as the Council remembered to make him a member of the Cemetery Committee—of which, years ago, he was chairman !—that excuse is nonsense. Then they say he did not attend the committees. He attended 13 out of 15 of the Watch Committee meetings last year, 22 out of .33 Street Improvement Com- mittee meetings, and 14 out of 25 of the Paving and Sewerage Committee. Those are good averages, but Dr. NIcKEE, who was one of those promoted to the Gas Committee, did not attend a single Street Improvement Committee meeting out of 33, and other similar cases might easily be quoted to prove that this excuse is also nonsense.</p>
        <p>Councillor CRAIK, who was first returned by an unprecedented major- ity, and has never been opposed since, has declined to serve on the other minor committees upon which the Council " allowed " him to remain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>LICENSEES ON THE WATCH CONIMITTEE.</head>
        <p>LICENSEES ON THE WATCH CONIMITTEE.</p>
        <p>As the many letters which we continue to receive on the above topic show, feeling on the question in town is distinctly rising. A request was made that the facilities for obtaining drink should be slightly curtailed dur- ing war-time, and the only reply. to that rational and patriotic apPeal was the provocative action of the Council in adding a licensee to the Watch Committee. We hope that those interested in the matter—by no means confined to those who are associated with the various Temperance Societies —will proceed with their demands until they succeed. We are not con- cerned with the allegation that drunkenness is no worse than it was before the war. Drink is as dangerous as ever it was, and we have LORD KITCHENER'S sister backing up that gallant officer's appeal to the nation by " pointing out that competent authorities say that between 10 per cent. and 40 per cent. of "our new soldiers are being rendered inefficient through drink and its 'attendant evils.". Russia could not spare more men than a series of big defeats would cost them, and so they shut off the supply of vodka altogether.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>MORE MEN WANTED.</head>
        <p>MORE MEN WANTED.</p>
        <p>LORD KITCHENER is quite satisfied, so far,- with. the way men are joining the new Armies. He also appears to be content with the progress we and our Allies are making in the field. But more men will be needed. We re- cently wrote :—" Final victory will rest with the side that can the longer bring up and fling fresh forces into the fray.' Are we prepared to say that Britain will be the one that shall bring *up the last, and, consequently, the winning, battalions in this 'mighty struggle for national, nay, for domes- tic, existence? That is the only thing that matters for us all now." An adver- tisement in to-day's " Examiner " points out that there is room for our Bantams in the Birkenhead Corps that is being raised—that is, for men too small to enter the ordinary regiments. We' all know what little men can do, and as there is reason to suppose that many of them were chafing at their ill-luck' at not being able to get at the Prussian Guards, it should not take the Bantam Battalion long to fill. There are still a few places to be filled in the Reserve Battalion of our Terri- torials, and the officials at the Drill Hall will be pleased to hear of likely recruits.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>LICENSEES ON THE WATCH COMMITTEE.</head>
        <p>LICENSEES ON THE WATCH COMMITTEE.</p>
        <p>COUNCILLOR HENSHALL'S VINDICATION. To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—l should not have troubled your readers but for the incorrect impression created by Mr. Griffiths' statement, that "Councillor Henshall was neutral and sat upon the fence." The fact is, that the election of the Watch Committee was contested three times—once on Friday, Nov- ember 6th, and twice on Monday November 9th. Both on Friday and Monday I voted ' against "members of the Trade," and should certainly have voted for Alderman Tinnion's amendment had I been present. I was com- pelled to keep an important business appoint- ment out of town, which I made on the natural assumption that the Council meeting would be well over by two o'clock. It will thus be seen I was neither "neutral nor sitting on the fence," and, while I make no complaint, as the Press report might easily lend itself to such a construction, may I respectfully suggest that in future it is not unreasonable to expect that if any of my constituents or friends are in doubt or distress at my action in the Council, they should ask for a personal explanation. While I do not exactly claim "That for all I do I have a reason, For all I say a word in season, And ever ready am to cite Authorities for what I write" still I am confident that a much more satis- factory solution would result than is possible in a public correspondence. I have already acted upon this advice, and seen Mr. Griffiths.—Yours, ROBERT HENSHALL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>PROMISES AND PERFORMANCES.</head>
        <p>PROMISES AND PERFORMANCES.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Allow me to pay my tribute to Alder- men Tilinion and Pierpoint, and Councillors Locker, Shaw, Wilkinson, Skelland and Lindley for aqting upon those principles they professed to believe in, whilst on the other hand my pro- found disappointment with our Mayor and those Liberals who voted for a licensee to be on the Watch Committee. Surely they must know what are the duties of this committee? Know- ing as we do the crime and the evil effects of the drinking of intoxicating liquor, and in face of what some of these men have said when ask- ing for the support of the electors, we thought they, above all others, would not have acted as they have done.</p>
        <p>TLer.„ sir, I must say a word about those who were neutral. They sat on the fence and let it go anyhow. Some of them are Nonconformists, who have taught the young life of our town to sing:</p>
        <p>"Dare to be a Daniel, Dare to stand alone." What explanation they can offer I am at a loss to know. Certainly, we, as electors, will want to ask them some pointed questions at some future date.—Yours, &amp;c., ELI WILSON JAMES. 30, Oxford-street, Latchford.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>LIBERALISM AND THE DRINK # TRAFFIC.</head>
        <p>LIBERALISM AND THE DRINK # TRAFFIC.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of " The Examiner."</p>
        <p>- Sir,—l was much interested in the letters pub- lished last week from my total. abstinence friends, who are again disappointed with the action. of Liberal representatives in the Town Council not voting as temperance men. Is it not time we knew the Liberal party is not a temperpce party, and it is not right to expect representatives more true to professed princi- ples than are the electors? Members of the temperance party have repeatedly at election time placed politics before temperance, so why should we whine when representatives do the same?</p>
        <p>If we really believe in temperance and wish to be consistent, we must take up . politics in agreement with our temperance principles, for at election time politics come first, and this fact makes the suggested reprisals of ", Citizen" seem idle talk.</p>
        <p>The temperance party in Warrington are fairly numerous, but to suggest making their influence felt in the councils of the Liberal party Eieems out of place, so long as non-political is the label of the temperance parties. I am quite as disappointed as anyone at the way temperance opinion is snubbed in War- rington, but we, the rank and file, are to blame and not the Council. We shall get what we want when we persist- ently demand it with sufficient vigour and weight.—Yours, &amp;c., 29, Orford-avenue.</p>
        <p>W. LOWE:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0082" n="art0082">
        <head>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</head>
        <p>COMFORTS FOR OUR SOLDIERS.</p>
        <p>A WONDERFUL TOTAL. Mrs. Edelsten reports The bad weather on Thursday had no effect this time on the number of garments received at the Town Hall, which came to the wonderful total of 514. Again the kindly Borough Trea- surer appeared with 13 good suits, and other substantial clothes for the men leaving for hospital; a large parcel went to Lady Barr for the hospitals at the front, and a further con- signment of warm clothing to Major Willcocks for his hospital ship for the Indians. Miss Broadbent accepted four dozen cuffs for the men guarding the Ship Canal. Generous dona- tions arrived from the Mayoress, the Women's Liberal Association, the Women's Tariff Reform League, and the Penketh and Burtonwood Working Parties, and many others. PICTURE PATRONS' PRESENT OF</p>
        <p>"FAGS." Recently patrons of the Star Kineina have been asked to place, in specially prepared boxes, cigarettes for the men now serving with the 2nd Battalion of the South Lancashire Regiment at the front. The box was opened a few days ago and was found to contain a large supply of cigarettes of all brands. These were parcelled up and sent to the proper quarter, and in ack- nowledgment of the gifts Mr. Edward Connor, the proprietor of the Kinema, has received a letter of acknowledgment and thanks from Lieutenant B. V. Freiler, the acting-adjutant of the battalion, who says:—" Coining as they did, when the regiment was very short of tobacco, I can assure you that they were greatly appreciated." He adds : "The regiment has been in the thick of things, and we have had no time at all in which to thank, earlier, those people at home for the presents they have sent us." Lieutenant-Quartermaster S. Boast has also assured Mr. Connor of the battalion's grati- tude to Star patrons. THE " AMBULANCE " DRAW. The Women Liberals of Lancashire and Che- shire have collected from their associations and friends the sum of .t6OO, with which a motor reiinagtitaoan_—rpaeira ambulance—to be named the "L. and C."—is to be purchased, and sent at once to Millicent, Duchess of Sutherland, for her hospital at Dun- kirk. Towards this sum the women of War- edthae substantial ltantial Women'sameaLatral Asso- of a driaw held last week. By the bw3a3lll-,entlnies prize—a beautiful doll—has not yet been called for by the lucky drawer of the winning num- ber-417; and, if it is not claimed within a reasonable time, there will be another draw for it. In the meantime, it is in the care of Mrs. Osborne, 22, Bold-street. GLOVES AND MITTENS. fieSld Memorialplendidpr progress is is bbyeinthgemTadaee,adaatytheeveCEdrongs- knitting class, which is always largely attended. Everybody is invited to turn up and help in the Grand Duke Michael's glove and mitten scheme. A large consignment was sent on Wednesday from the Women's Liberal Associa- tion, besides whom the following are doing ! valuable work: Crosfield's, Armitage and Rigby, Rylands', Locker's, Richmond's, the Salvation Army, and the Bank Quay Mission. Many of the schools are also helping. Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield kindly provide all the wool used by the knitting class.</p>
        <p>LIBERAL WOMEN BUSY SEWING. The sewing Meetings held on .Monday and Thursday afternoons at the Crosfield Memorial Hall are also proceeding very satisfactorily, and on Thursday consignments of comfortable gar- ments were forwarded by the Women's Liberal Association to the Mayoress and to the Ter ' torials. Mrs. Osborne will be very glad to receive gifts of cash or maaterial.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0083" n="art0083">
        <head>HOSTILITY OF NEUTRALITY.</head>
        <p>THE HOSTILITY OF NEUTRALITY.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>was glad to find that the action of the Liberal members of the Council on this matter had not been allowed to pass unnoticed in your columns. Oh, how difficult it is to find men with backbone enough to stand up on such occasions as these for the principles they advo- cate on public platforms! The lack of back- bone in many public men is one of the lament- able characteristics of the age. So often it is a case of quid pro quo: "You vote for me on this occasion, and I will vote for you on that." I admire the Liberal mem- bers of the Council who voted openly. They were open foes; but what of the "neutral" members—those who did not vote? They ren- dered no help. It is true they did not take sides with the clique that had compacted to oust Councillor Henry Roberts. They did not take the offensive. They just stood aloof as if the principle that was being violated was no concern of theirs at all, and not on account of any hostility, but just on account of their non-intervention, impartiality, the plot was successful.</p>
        <p>Neutrality under certain conditions is tanta- mount to direct hostility. These councillors were neutral when they ought to have been allies. That brings them into the hostile camp. They are not with us, therefore they must be against us. Just as England's neutral- ity would have made her as one of them, had she taken an' attitude of non-intervention when Germany invaded Belgium. Her word no more could have been relied on, or her integrity and honesty of purpose would be questioned. She would have been guilty of the sin of neutrality and would have been deservedly denounced by all right-thinking men. Neutrality is a common weakness, and evi- dently Liberal Town Councillors are not exempt. To abstain from doing good when it can be done is surely as bad as doing actual wrong. "He that is not with me is against me." The Liberal Councillors may say: "I did the cause of progress no harm; I have taken no sides, I have not assisted your enemies. I have been neutral, impartial, a mere looker-on." Yes; "But in the day that thou stoodest aloof when the enemy was busy thou wast as one of them." The priest and Levite modernised. The wounded man lay by the roadside. The priest and Levite passed by on the other side. They did not ill-treat him, did not add to his Injury; they just passed by on the other side and held themselves aloof. They did nothing when they could and should have helped him. The neutral councillors will not probably be condemned for doing wrong, but for not doing right. Men stand aloof, not doing their duty by their country in the hour of peril and crisis, and are holding back that help which it is in their power to give. There are many to-day who are more concerned with their own gain than their country's loss, with their own plea- sures than their nation's peril; those to whom the result of a football match or a horse race is of more moment apparently than the result of an engagement with the enemy; those people to whom a picture show is of more moment than ' an intercessory service for the well-being and success of our troops. The indifference of many at this time is just as reprehensive as was Nero's conduct, who fiddled while Rome was burning.</p>
        <p>The drinking habit is destroying the physical efficiency of our young recruits in the camp centres, and chief magistrates, chief constables, and Watch Committees are standing aloof while this destruction is going on. Our neutral Town Councillors stand aloof while a member of " The Trade" is being elected on the Watch Committee. These non-committals stand aloof. Neutral councillors should be called to judg- ment for their non-committal conduct. We cannot and will not take a non-committal atti- tude on this drink question. The stand-aloof- ness is sucking the life-blood of every Progres- sive movement. Such actions as were witnessed at the Council meeting of men of professed temperance principles voting for, and the neutrals standing aloof when a good stalwart advocate of Progressive principles was being replaced on the Watch Committee by an off- licence holder.</p>
        <p>Oh, that the day of reckoning was at hand! No non-party compact would be allowed to stand in the way of a straight fight with these so-called Progressive Liberal councillors. By the way, is it possible that this non-party com- pact has had something to do with the inroad made on their usual stedfast principles?— Yours, &amp;c., AP. lORWERTH.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0084" n="art0084">
        <head>FIRST BULWARK AGAINST GERMAN HORDES.</head>
        <p>FIRST BULWARK AGAINST GERMAN HORDES.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY EVENING - CONCERT FOR BELGIAN FUND.</p>
        <p>In happier times, Mr. and Mrs. A. H. all the artistes „d bearing all the to give a high-class concert, Crosfield promise finding expenses, in 'aid of the Infirmary Working Men's Committee. Acting on the suggestion of Alderman Burton (chairman of the com- mittee), and with the approval of the com- mittee, Mr. Crosfield decided, in the existing circumstances, to give the concert, but to hand over the proceeds to the Belgun Relief Fund. The concert will be held in the Parr Hall on Saturday, December 12th, and the programme, on which both Mr. and Mrs. Crosfield will them- selves appear, will be full of first-class talent. The Working Men's Committee have charge of the organisation of the concert, and for their funds another concert upon the same liberal terms will be given at a future date. Mr. Payton, assisted by Mr. Crossley, is arranging the programme. Undoubtedly the debt that this country, in common with the rest of the Allies, owes to the small but brave Belgian nation makes it incumbent upon us all to do all we can to alleviate the suffering their fine resistance has brought upon them. Lord Roberts described, in his graphic, soldierly language, on the eve of his death, the part Belgium has played in this great struggle. In an introduction to King Albert's book he says:—</p>
        <p>"In the terrible struggle still raging, to the Belgians must be awarded the palm for freely and fearlessly offering themselves as the first bulwark against the invading hordes of Germany. " Glorious has been their stand, and price- less the time and advantage gained thereby. No acknowledgment of their splendid example can be too liberal, no admiration too lavish, no Compensation for the loss and misery they have endured too' generous. "They have fought heroically for a sacred principle against the frightful + odds; they have suffered up to the limit of human endurance. God grant that there may be yet in store for them a bright and prosperous future, and a prominent place in the van of civilisation and freedom."</p>
        <p>This concert is intended to help the funds that are intended to make the life of the Bel- gian fairly tolerable until the nations fighting on their side have brought them final peace, and placed them well on the *ay to that final bright and prosperous condition of which the late Field-Marshal speaks. There should he a great audience. The gross takings will go to the Belgian Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0085" n="art0085">
        <head>New Miniature Rifle Club.</head>
        <p>New Miniature Rifle Club.</p>
        <p>At a meeting of the employes of Messrs. Fletcher, Russell% and Co., Ltd., on Wednesday, it was decided to form a miniature rifle club, facilities for so doing having been granted by the Board of Directors. Space indoors has been placed at the disposal of the men, which will enable them to have the use of eight tar- gets, if necessary, at a distance of 50 yards. Later it is proposed to have an outside range of not less than 100 yards. Officers have been appointed, Mr. W. Russell being elected presi- dent, with the remainder of the Board, and Mr. W. Banks, the works' manager, as vice-presi- dents. Mr. W. Leach is the hon. secretary. It is hoped to make a start by the Ist prox. The club are applying for affiliation to the National MilliatUre Rifle Assoeiati°ll*</p>
      </div>
