        <p>c, Shutting Off the Water Supply. ats'ilvaccount of the connecting up of the mains the ,arrington Bridge, the supply of water in uistrict situated between the River Mersey "114 the Bridgewater Canal will be shut off '''°rrow (Sunday), from 8 a.m. to 5 p.m. " Light in Darkness.” .I`Tot to be 4Bited deterred by the intense fog which ' deterred on Saturday night, two it'itg privates of Kitchener's Army were noticed .4!lalichester-road reading a "speshul football '`l°4" with the aid of an electric flash lamp!</p>
        <p>1, Police Officers Promoted. ective Summers, of the Warrington Police arreef has recently been promoted to the rank i` 4etective-sergeant, Whilst Acting-Sergeant ilE'onard has been made a full sergeant. P.C.'s 43'es, Howard and Clarke have been made 4cting-sergeants. " A Reduction on Quantily." ith the approval of the Education Corn- 'gee, the School Management Sub-committee evC resolved: "That the fees for the Commer- l!al Institute be amended as follows. viz.: (a) a language course (any two languages), 6d. for the session; (b) for one language only, 8. fxl• for the session. "Why We Went To War." "'by we went to War" was the title of an s,llstrated lecture given in the Church Room, "liockton Heath, by Mr. Henry Lancaster last /141 riot t• During the evening popular and ie songs and duets were rendered by 4 e'ssrs• Arthur Greenhough and Walter Howard hnd the lecturer gave recitations. M. Frank k'ooth was an efficient accompanist.</p>
