        <p>81. The Late Mr. John Riley. • Marrs Chore'', Buttermarket-street, has 1,5;', a devout worshipper and II genercius sup- • eer by the death of Mr. John Riley, which Fitt„,,e,vent occurred at his residence, 9, Paul- on Saturday. The deceased gentleman. Iv:l°,l_l'l'as in his 48th year, was a member of the gam'nown. firm of Messrs. F. and J. Riley, fish, 'PCP and Poultry dealers, of Sankey-street. • funeral took place at the Cemetery on testlaY afternoon. At 811001 Manager's Complaiii I. the, monthly meeting of the Education Nth"inai 'dee on Monday evening Mr. J. Jarman IA-I,hairled that although he was appointed a of St, Peter's School some time .ago, had not been summoned to any meetings.— Nairi,arl Stevenson said that Mr. Jarman-'s hest apn' Would be to communicate with the corre- .la-4dent of the managers of the school.—Mr. TIP:Ian: Is that my business or that of the tacation Committee?—The Chairman (Coun- th°l‘ R. Roberts): The Director will do that. ere is any fault it is not the fault of the -4 "ation Committee. - A Warning to Itinerant Topers. arning to outsiders who come into d War- and fall into the hands of the police (rough drunkenness was issued by the Mayor ("I ~; e,rman Peter Peacock) in announcing the one-I,.'m in a case at the Borough Police Court Iva -34onday, in which a stranger to the town tilp charged with having been drunk and dis- beerlY on Saturday. It was such men as the soner, said his Worship, who made the local of drunkenness appear nore than it tith4l3' would be. He would like it to go forth st' °fferiders of prisoner's stamp would be erelY dealt with in future. A Teachers as Drill Instructors. ti trmng the minutes approved by the Ethica- l:l. Committee on Monday night was the fol- ublalt, submitted by the School Management flo:°_,oltinittee: "A circular letter from the Education,' enclosing a communication tile War Office with reference to the en- 418,iiierlt of teachers as instructors in military it and physical exercises, was submitted, and torqas resolved that a copy of the particulars colt„ained in the communications referred to be sei.;.eYed to all eligible male teachers in the ice of the committee."</p>
        <p>Busy Educationists. Tho Present at the monthly meeting of the 21:,,,862114.—ngt0n Education Committee on Monday 'A were: Councillor Roberts (chairman), lderJ• Jolley (vice-chairman), the Mayor 131 an Peter Peacock), Mrs. C. Jackson Pir•-,-"es, the Revs. Canon Stevenson, Father °II and G. C. Briggs, Aldermen Bennett, biel„ st and Tinnion, Councillors Pemberton, .tp,n'llsan, Archer and Locker, and Messrs. J. 43,14-11,' It• T. Fairclough, J. Unsworth, J. Jar- Phaeton, G. Walker, C. S. Burgess and ac"on Holmes, with the Director of Edu- (Nlr. J. Moore Murray).</p>
        <p>_ . 1"t of the Late Mr. T. Preston Taylor. tiThe' f tineral took place at the Warrington el%e, 1`451. 'crY on Sunday of Mr. Thomas Preston " 9, Lexden-street. As briefly reported ht last issue he me i with an accident whilst w4ric in a railway siding at Dallam on Mon- ay 0,1 last week, and died the same day in the ceri .crow ngton Infirmary. He had been in the tzit-a°Y Of the L. and N.-W. Railway Company s'Ap-v;er 40 years, and in returning a verdict of Tliti;elental death" at the inquest held on sSoa,,-41,437 of last week, the jury expressed their ).'iokfia.4!•43, with the relatives. Chief Inspector on behalf of the railway company, Llatea` „in this expression of sympathy, and l'anv hat Mr. Taylor had worked for the COM- htea",b;i3r 40 years, and had been a good, honest, 4,4nd trustworthy servant. 4,1 e ,4nYlor was associated with the Working 1111a,-;,:lisslon, Bank Quay, where he had been 'orlz—tss in various departments of the church an, or over 20 years he fulfilled the duties sider;isrieh treasurer, and occupied for some con- 7,UPeii"„T time the position of Sunday se hool ~,—"endent. The esteem in which lidl was nianifested by the presence of a largo 'llnrcrtinn at the service held at the Mission ei;;`, Prior to the interment, Mr. T. Dale 41q ;"11R, and also performing the last rites at •Ltqi'l'i.l,4N-eside. In the procession to the Ceme- ',„,"cr " relatives were joined by a large nnin- ,)raihvay employes, the members of the Comjit,etee,and the elder scholars of the 43' school, sent by the following: Wife. 1;Qaa,7.1 ,a,rul Arthur," "Alfred and Gerty," and Arthur," "Neighbours," Mrs. Unlchanan Mr. and Mrs. Monks, "Willie Annie and Alex.," Mr. and Mrs. xr-re°eld" Sarah and Ernest," Mrs. John tj a', and Mrs. Tilling, Sunday school, rs. Moore, Church members, Chris- Zooi ',neavour Society, "From his Sunday /I`ass," Mr. C. Taylor. Manchester; Mr. .;c4ltb,'"B,. Mullins, Mrs. J. Gleave, "From his —4Les," and Mr. and Mrs. R. Tanner.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
        <head>New Firemen.</head>
        <p>New Firemen.</p>
        <p>The following have been appointed as new members of the Warrington Fire Brigade Messrs. Harry Shaw, John W. Hunt, Andrew D. Chilton and William Berrisford.</p>
        <p>2,8,000 Loan for a New School. The Local G6vernment Board have given their sanction to the borrowing of .28,291 for the erec- tion of a new public elementary school in Parr- street.</p>
        <p>New Nuisances Inspector. Mr. John C. Cook, of Darwen, has, subject to the approval of the Town Council, been ap- pointed by a sub-committee of the Health Com- mittee inspector of nuisances, at a salary of 355. per week, with the usual annual increments and uniform.</p>
        <p>Plum Puddings for the Trenches. Largely through the persuasive effort, of the Vicar of Orford (the Rev. G. Vale Ovfen) and Capt. Crane, the members of the congregation of St. Margaret's Church have either subscribed for or personally made Christmas puddings to the total number of 170, for distribution amongst the British soldiers at the front.</p>
        <p>St. Clement's Dedication Festival. The Rev. C. E. R. Brocklebank, of St. Matthew's, Westminster, was the special preacher at the dedication festival in connect- tion with St. Peter's, held on Monday evening. On Thursday evening the Rev. P. J. Evans, late Archdeacon of Zanzibar, lectured on "The Uni- versities Mission to Central Africa."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>Summons Dismissed.</head>
        <p>A Summons Dismissed.</p>
        <p>At the St. Helens Borough Police Court yes- terday a summons against Thomas Langton, of Cobden Farm, Little Budworth, for selling milk containing 9i per cent. of added water, was dis- missed.—For the defence Mr. T. S. Steel, of Warrington, called evidence to show that no water had been added to the milk whilst it was in the possession of the defendant.</p>
        <p>Birth and Death Rates Up. The usual return issued by the Medical Officer of Health (Dr. G. W. N. Joseph) shows that dur- ing the week ended November 21st, 48 births were registered, as against 33 in the correspond- ing period of last year, giving a birthrate of 33.3, as against 23.2. During the same period there were 25 deaths, as compared with 21, the rates being 17.3 and 14.7 respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>Late Lord Roberts.</head>
        <p>The Late Lord Roberts.</p>
        <p>Mr. Arthur J. Thorp, Manchester-road, pos. sesses a letter from the late Lord Roberts, which, he will now prize more than ever. It is as follows: " Englemere, Ascot, Berks, 1 July, 1911. Dear Sir,—Please accept my best thanks for the copy of your Coronation Hymn, which you have been so kind as to send me.—Yours very truly, ROBERTS, F.M. Arthur J. Thorp, Esq.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>Another War Pinch—But a Trifling</head>
        <p>Another War Pinch—But a Trifling</p>
        <p>One!</p>
        <p>Owing to the increased price for ruled paper and exercise books, due to the war, the Educa- tion Committee have decided to allow their con- tractor a little more than the amount he ten- dered for when his quotation was accepted. It has also been decided to allow the contractor extra for pulp boards, owing to the same cause.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>Fire Engine Committee and Alderman</head>
        <p>Fire Engine Committee and Alderman</p>
        <p>Burton.</p>
        <p>The following resolution appears among the minutes of the Fire Engine Committee to be submitted to the Town Council at their meeting next Tuesday :—" That the best thanks of this committee be given to Mr; Alderman Burton for his long and useful membership of this committee, and for the valuable work under- taken by him in re-organising the Fire Bri- gade, and that this committee record its regret that he has been compelled, owing to ill-health, to sever his connection with the committee, and expresses the hope that he will speedily be restored to good health."</p>
        <p>For the Belgian Red Cross Society. Under the patronage of the Mayor and Mayoress (Alderman and Mrs. Peacock), and in aid of the Belgian Red Cross Society, a grand concert will be given in the Royal Court Theatre on the evening of Sunday, December 6th, 'COM- mencing at 8 o'clock. The major portion of the programine will be given by State artistes from the Antwerp and Brussels Opera Houses, who came over to England with letters of introduc- tion to the Lord Mayor of London from the Burgomaster of Antwerp, to tour this country on behalf of the above-mentioned society. They will be assisted by the following: Mrs. Sanders, Miss Elsie Wilkinson, Mr. W. V. Garrod, the well-known actor-playwright, and a special orchestra under the direction of Mr. James Roy. Warringtonians should not miss this op- portunity of hearing some of the best Conti- nental opera singers—to say nothing of the local ' artistes--and at the same time help a deserving Cause.</p>
        <p>Mayor as Bargainer. At the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday the Mayor (Alderman P. Peacock) drew attention to the following minute in the report of the Sites and Buildings Sub-committee: "It was reported that, subject to the approval of the Education Committee, the Town Council proposed to purchase a plot of land adjacent to the school site in Oliver- street with a view to its allocation to this com- mittee, and it was resolved that this proposal be approved, and that a plan of the amended site be submitted to the Board of Education for their approval." His Worship said that he had the instructions of the Council to purchase the property in ques- tion at the price at which it was offered to the Education Committee, but as the result of his negotiationsfegoatctiations he had saved the committee pounds; he had got it for ACI less than he was told to give. (Hear, hear.)</p>
        <p>Corporation Committees' Chairmen. The various committees of the Corporation have elected their chairmen and deputy-chair- men as follows :-'--Finance and Estate, Councd- lor Wilkinson and Councillor Locker; Health, Alderman Tames Evans and Alderman Burton; Sanitary Works, Councillor Wilson and Coun- cillor Hawthorn; Paving and Sewerage, Alder- man Tinniou and Councillor Pemberton; Museum, Mr. T. J. Ridgway and Alderman Bennett; Town Hall, Parks and Baths, Alder- man Bolton and Councillor Osborne; Gas, Councillor Locker and Alderman James Evans; Fire Engine, Cotincillor Gough and Councillor Cunnington; Water, Alderman John Evans and Councillor Lindley; Gymnasium, Councillor Broadhurst and Councillor Wilson; Electricity and Tramways, Councillor Joseph and Council- lor Wilkinson; Small Holdings and Allotments, Councillor Henshall and Councillor Jenkins; Towns: Councillor Pemberton and the Mayor; Bridges, Alderman Smethurst and the Mayor; General Purposes, the Mayor and Councillor Joseph; Street Improvement, Alderman Ben- nett and Councillor Joseph; Markets, Councillor Chantler and Councillor Archer; Rivers, Coon- ether Halliwell and Councillor Henshall; and Watch, the Mayor and Councillor Joseph. Unitarian Wedding. At the Cairo-street Unitarian Church on Tuesday the marriage took place of Miss Edith Alice Smith, the youngest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. J. T. Smith, of 68, Bewsey-street, and Mr. Ernest L. Warburton, second son of Mr. T. P. Warburton, of 7, Lower Wash-lane. The Rev. Irvine Lister officiated, and before and after the service appropriate music was played by the organist (Mr. J. Muir). The bride, who was given away by her father, was attired in a gown of cream charmeuse, with moire silk and pearl trimming, and wore a hat of saxe blue velvet, ornamented with a white ostrich feather. She carried a shower bouquet of lilies and white Chrysanthemums, and was attended by her sister (Miss Mary Smith) and Miss Emily Bell, as. bridesmaids. Miss Mary Smith was cos- tumed in blue silk, with a brocaded velvet coatee and a felt hat, trimmed With velvet and a rich plume; whilst Miss Bell wore a becoming blue silk eolienne dress and a black, felt hat, trimmed with ostrich feathers. Both the bridesmaids carried shower bouquets of pink chrysanthemums and maidenhairi fern. Mr. Henry Warburton, brother of the bridegroom, acted as groomsman. After the ceremony a reception was held at Atkinson's Cafe. The happy couple were the recipients 'of many hand- some and useful presents.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
        <p>Miss Dorothy Browne Married. Grappenhall Church was on Monday the scene of a pretty wedding, to which the contract- ing parties were Miss Dorothy Browne, the daughter of Mr. and Mrs. Arthur Browne, of "Oakville,' Latchford Without, and Mr. Oliver Wheeler, the son of the late Rev. and Mrs. John C. Wheeler, of Birkdale, near Southport. The Rev. H. V. Pigot officiated. The bride was given away by her father, and was attended by Miss Barbara Grimsdale, as bridesmaid, Mr. Norman acting as groomsman. Following the ceremony a reception was held af the residence of the bride's parents, after which the newly- married couple left for Harrogate, where the honeymoon is being spent. Numerous costly wedding presents were received.</p>
        <p>For Refugees and Red Cross Work. By kind permission of Mr. Maznaghten, the Warrington branch of the Amalgamated Musi- cians' Union will give a sacred concert in the Hippodrome on Sunday, December 6th. The object is to assist the Belgian Refugee and Red Cross funds. There is a splendid programme in store, the artistes who have promised their ser- vices being: Miss Marie Houghton, soprano (who, by special request, will sing "The Rosary" and "Annie Laurie"); Madame E. A. Sanders, contralto; Mr. J. Rabbitt, tenor; Mr. Ernest Hastings, bass (late Carl Rosa Opera Company); the Warrington Male Choral Union; and an orchestra of 30, under the conductorship of Mr. Harold March. A large audience is con- fidently anticipated.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Drink In War Time.</head>
        <p>Drink In War Time.</p>
        <p>. -- - ----- At a meeting of the Warrington Free Church Council Executive Committee, held on Novem- ber 20th, the following resolution was passed, and the Secretary (the Rev. H. Ogden) was in- structed to send copies to the Prime Minister and the Home Secretary : _</p>
        <p>" That the Executive vof the Warrington Free Church Council is of the opinion that the In- toxicating (Liquor Restriction) Act, 1914, places an invidious responsibility upon chief constables, especially where the Trade is most influential, and hopes that the Government will take the earliest opportunity of widening the area of re- sponsibility, so that the Act may become gener- ally effective."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <head>CAPTAIN WARING.</head>
        <p>CAPTAIN WARING.</p>
        <p>Death of a Respected and Useful</p>
        <p>Citizen.</p>
        <p>Early last Saturday morning there passed away at Latchford a gentleman who had made a decided mark in the life of the town in which he had resided for twenty years. Capt. Wm. Waring, who came to Warrington in 1893, as superintendent of the Latchford Locks and of the Warrington and district section of the Man- chester Ship Canal, was well equipped for the duties of his post, for he had been closely con- nected with shipping and the sea from his youth up. It was, however, in matters outside his profession that he was best known in town. Of a keenly sympathetic nature, he took a deep interest in ambulance and nursing work, and was, from 1897 to the time of his death, chief superintendent of the Warrington Corps of the St. John Ambulance Brigade. It was largely owing to his untiring energy that the corps attained its high state of efficiency, which has enabled it to send out volunteers to the present war, as well as to the South African campaign. lie was also a Freemason, and, generally, a well-known and highly-respected citizen.</p>
        <p>Eight years ago—in 190.6—Capt. Waring lost his only son, who was in the Indian native police, in Madras, of typhoid fever, a loss from which he never recovered. For two years he had been seriously ill, but his many friends will be glad to know that he passed away quietly during unconsciousness that lasted 12 hours. The funeral took place on Wednesday in the peaceful churchyard at Grappenhall, the Rev. H. V. Pigot (rector) and the Rev. G: C. Briggs, vicar of Christ Church, Latchford, officiating. The chief mourners were: Miss Waring, Mrs. Rabaghati, Mrs. Loftus Allen, Miss Legge, Mr. Loftus Allen, Capt. Williams (canal superin- tendent, Manchester), Mr. Lowen (Ship Canal), Dr. Jago (Capt. Waring's medical attendant), and Mr. Herbert Woods.</p>
        <p>There were also present, representing Ship Canal employes, Capt. Healey (Runcorn), Capt. Acraman (Manchester), and Messrs. J. Batters- by, H, Spears, J. S. Farquharson, G. C. Brown (pier master, Latchford), Marshall (lockmaster), J. Runey, P. Nichol and H. Bann.</p>
        <p>A strong contingent of ambulance men in- cluded Supt. Baldry, Supt. Rose, First Officer Williams, Officer and Secretary A. E. Spann, Sergts. Fell, Banning and Williamson, Corporals Hampton and Broomhall, and Pte. T. Weston, with the following privates present on special leave from the First Western General Hospital, Fazakerley : Makin, Clements, Price, Glenton, Pritchard and Saunders. The nursing division was represented by Mrs. Warhurst (lady superintendent) and Mrs. Fisher (secretary).</p>
        <p>• , Alderman Bennett was also present. Mrs. Broadbent sent her carriage, in which the floral tributes sent by the following were conveyed to the church: "Lydia," "Dolly and Gita," "Billy," "Minna and Loftus," "Sarah," Ship Canal Officials, Ship Canal (Latchford section), St. John Ambulance Brigade, Mrs. Broadbent, Mrs. Dakin, Mr. Farquharson, Mrs. Fletcher and Mr. Mackenzie (Southport), Miss M. Gornall, Miss Legge, Major and Mrs, Macleod (Bedford), Mr. and Mrs. W. Pierpoint, Mrs. W. B. Roberts, Mr. Herbert Woods, The coffin was of polished oak, mounted with massive brass rings, and the inscription: "William Waring, died November 21st, 1914; aged 68 years."</p>
        <p>-The funeral arrangements were carried out by Messrs. J. Mounfield and Sons, Church-street. Capt. Waring, who leaves a widow and one daughter, belonged to a family that came origin- ally from Waringstown, County Down, Ireland. He had travelled nearly all over the world, hav- ing traded between here and North America, South Africa, China, Japan, Australia and New Zealand. He had also travelled on the Con- tinent a great deal, and was very well informed 'upon peoples and their customs. Land marks in his eventful career were: Went to sea as premium apprentice in 1862; made third officer in 1866; given command of a steamer in 1870; towed 350 passengers on board a vessel in distress to place of safety; rescued 25 of the crew of an American ship wrecked on the Paracel Reefs, China, for which President Gar- field presented him with a gold watch.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>"DISMISSEDCOUNCILLORS."</head>
        <p>THE "DISMISSEDCOUNCILLORS."</p>
        <p>CAPTAINS OF INDUSTRY AND THE COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>Warr irr; nTgi not nch ee as edeimte soysr to ‘T permanent e rc:nomahnpe E eenlltex compelled Examiner." to select Ive frequently heard expressions of regret and even condemnation of the apathetic spirit manifested by our industrial magnates towards everything appertaining to municipal administration, and, of course, the arguments advanced and the evi- dence presented were so clear, I have been com- pelled to admit that our local captains of indus- try have failed to realise their duties as true and patriotic citizens by refusing or by exhibit- ing a stand " offish " demeanour, in face of an anxious desire that they should devote a few hours per week of their precious time (I admit) land commercial experience for the benefit of the old town. After the unfortunate incident the other week, " when a councillor of immense value (too valuable for some people's liking)," a councillor of great abilities, commercial knowledge and eloquence, and the one solitary large employer of labour that has sacrificed years of leisure and pleasure for the interest and welfare of his native town, to be replaced on two important committees by the "curious majority," convinces me now that our industrial captains are justi- fied in keeping a long distance from the Town Hall. - As a working man elector of Whitecross I am sincerely sorry the borough has lost the valu- able services of Councillor Roberts on the various committees he served on, and I hope some more clever pen—acquainted with this district—will take advantage of your impartial columns to ventilate the indignation that exists amongst a large section of workers in this ward against the intolerant decision of the inteljec- tual and " curious majority" in the Council Chamber.—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
        <p>WHITECROSS:</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>WAR STORIES.</head>
        <p>WAR STORIES.</p>
        <p>WAR WITH A "HUSTLE" ON. Present Campaign Contrasted With</p>
        <p>Soinh- African.</p>
        <p>"We have had an exceptionally heavy time during the last three weeks; in fact, since October 16th we have not known what a night's sleep is like, as we have been at it hammer and tongs. Men who have been through more than one campaign say they have seen more fighting here in one week than they experienced in months in South Africa." So runs an extract from a letter which Mrs. Potter, of 151, Wellfield-street, Warrington, has received from Driver Thomas Potter, of the Army Service Corps, whose home address is 14, Clare-street, Warrington. HIS NARROWEST ESCAPE—"SO FAR!"</p>
        <p>In the same letter the gallant Driver de- scribes the narrowest escape he has "so far" had. "Six of us and an officer," he writes, "were sent out of the trenches with two wounded men to a farmhouse about 200 yards away. As we carried them across we were sniped at by the infantry of the enemy, but, thanks to their marvellous (?) shooting powers, we arrived safe and sound in the house, with our invalids. That must have annoyed the Germans, for they turned one of their siege guns on to us and let us have two of their coal- boxes.' Both found the mark; the poor farm- house tottered and then collapsed on top of us, and as we lay there for what seemed a lifetime, but was really only a few seconds, I thought my end had come. I did not know whether I was hurt or not, but I started to scratch fora</p>
        <p>way out ..... . . After a while I again found myself in civilisation, and on looking round ‘I saw more bricks and dirt fall away and two of my mates come rolling out. We felt our bodies to see if we were all there, and we found that we were bigger men than before we went into the affair, owing to the bumps we carried. We went in search of the other four, and I am pleased to say we were successful. But what a state they were in!"</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>PRUSSIAN GUARDS' DEFEAT.</head>
        <p>PRUSSIAN GUARDS' DEFEAT.</p>
        <p>Warrington Private Speaks of the Famous Battle.</p>
        <p>Writing from St. Luke's Hospital, Halifax, George A. Joynson, Fothergill-street, a private in the King's Liverpool, details his experiences in the fighting line in various parts of France and Belgium. He relates how the detachment to which he belonged came up with the main body of the Allies as they were retiring from Mons. "They" (the main body), he says, "were all tired out with their long march and they welcomed us quietly. A halt was called, but we had not been lying down more than two hours when we got orders to advance on the enemy. We came into contact with them the same day, but they did not show much fight . . . they were getting a rough time from our big guns. We kept them on the move and they had to leave ammunition waggons and machine guns behind them Batches of prisoners often came in. They were weary-looking lots, but what sympathy could we feel for them, when in every village that we passed through we found fine buildings and cot- tages in flames and could smell the burning bodies of the natives. SAD SIGHT BY THE WAYSIDE.</p>
        <p>"I saw the bodies of p. o•Or woman and a child lying by the roadside awaiting laurial. . . . On September 10th we were too cfhick for them (the enemy) and we were soon'on top of them. We made a bayonet charge up the hills and we had got almost round them, when they showed the white flag. We got orders to fetch them in, but when we were within twenty yards of their trenches they opened fire on us. So we again charged them with the bayonet, capturing about 4.50 prisoners and three machine guns. That was my first experience in the firing line. . . . As they retreated they left their dead and wounded behind for us to deal with. I shall never forget some of the sights I saw. One chap was lying just behind a machine gun with his head blown clean off. • • • When we came into contact with Von Kluck's arm it was like hell on earth—nothing but slaughter. . . . I had given up all hope of coming out of it alive. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>CRAWLED THROUGH A GULLEY RUNNING BLOOD.</head>
        <p>CRAWLED THROUGH A GULLEY RUNNING BLOOD.</p>
        <p>" Capt. K. K. Smith, of the King's Regiment, had to crawl down a gulley which was running with blood. We managed to organise a few of our regiment under cover of the night, and we got orders to entrench ourselves. For nine weary days and nights it rained in torrents. We had no overcoats for protection, but only an oil sheet, and we were forced to lie down in the rain-sodden trenches, very often being numbed with cold. The enemy got the range of us and kept up a heavy shell fire, which took a lot of our poor chaps away. . . . Those who got wounded had to lie in the trenches until night- fall, when they were taken to the field hospital. . . In the Battle of the Aisne we were noth- ing but a target for their guns . . - their infantry would not fight us. . . .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PRUSSIAN GIANTS.</head>
        <p>PRUSSIAN GIANTS.</p>
        <p>"We marched on to Ypres, where we came into battle with the Prussian Guards. Here was a chance for our infantry to get their own back, and they did it time after time. . . . On one occasion we advanced into a dark wood. All at once we heard a flute being played, so we lay down until the order to charge was given. We then rushed at them; there seemed to be thou- sands of them! There were only 160 of us, and they cried out to us to surrender. But our officer shouted out 'Fifteen rounds, rapid!' and they ran like sheep to their trenches. . . . We entrenched within 150 yards of their trendhes, and it was quite fun popping them off when they showed themselves. These Prussian Guards are fine fellows, and it was a common saying amongst our boys, I got a Prussian' Guard 8f t. high, and with a bayonet as long as me!' " The writer goes on to describe how he got wounded in the head by a piece of shrapnel, and, in addition, was hit in the hand by a bullet, adding that after what he had gone through he considered himself lucky to be alive. Pte. Joynson kept a diary of his day's doings in the fighting line. One item in it reads: "As we went along we saw a lot of people lying by the roadside. They had nowhere to go. I gave ray tin of beef and also my biscuits to two little children. They were grad to get it! . . On September 16th we were driving some of the Germans through a wood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>SCOTS GUARDS TRAPPED.</head>
        <p>SCOTS GUARDS TRAPPED.</p>
        <p>"The G-e-rrnaiTis had placed a machine gun in a cottage, the muzzle pointing out of the window. Then they stationed a poor woman at the door as 'a blind.' When the Scots Guards, suspect- ing nothing wrong, came up, the gun mowed them down. . . . But our big gun blew the place up at the finish."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>FROM INDIA TO FRANCE.</head>
        <p>FROM INDIA TO FRANCE.</p>
        <p>Old Wycllffian Tells of Long Voyage to the War Zone.</p>
        <p>Private C. M. Armstrong, of the D Squadron, 17th (Duke of Cumberland's Own) Lancers (In- d Force A), has now returned to former Europe,Expeditionaryanded in France. Ile is a he Wycliffe Schools, and has i pupil andof tl written some interesting letters to his mother, Mrs. Gordon, of 49, Museum-street, Warrington. Sailing from India on the Ss. Islanda, Private Armstrong wrote as follows: "29th October, 1914. Since last I wrote you from larachi we have been steaming merrily on towards Europe. The whole regiment is not on board here though, A and B Squadrons being on the City of Leices- ter, together with Headquarters Staff.</p>
        <p>DEATH OF A HORSE.</p>
        <p>"Of course, our horses are with us, and so far</p>
        <p>_ we have been most fortunate in having lost only one, which we deposited into the briny deep a couple of days after we entered the Red Sea. A Squadron, I believe, have been less fortunate, and have lost two; while among the whole con- tingent between half a dozen and a dozen have died.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>"A FINE SIGHT."</head>
        <p>"A FINE SIGHT."</p>
        <p>"It is a fine sight, and one not to be forgotten or seen again, perhaps, to look out over the sea on to the thirty odd other boats escorted by several cruisers, all bound for the one place— France (probably)—and at night time to see their lights dotted about over a wide area, like so many low-lying stars. Frequently the dot- dash of the Morse code may be seen as one ship signals to another, or when the naval officer in charge of the contingent sends out some calor from his cruiser. Now and again he has sent</p>
        <p>Continued in Next Column.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>WAR-TIME SALE OF WORK. Why Emmanuel Church Found One Necessary.</head>
        <p>WAR-TIME SALE OF WORK. Why Emmanuel Church Found One Necessary.</p>
        <p>The annual sale of work in connection with Emmanuel Church, Bold-street, was held in the Church Institute, Knutsford-road, on Wednes- day, Thursday and yesterday. After very care- ful consideration, it was decided, notwithstand- ing the war, to hold the sale as usual, such a function being necessary to maintain the work which is being done. Mrs. Firth Lindley per- formed the opening ceremony on the first day, when there was an encouraging attendance. The incumbent, the Rev. W. T. Jaye, presided, and he was supported by the Revs. W. Reid and T. T. James, Alderman and Mrs. Tinnion, and a number of church officers. THE STALL HOLDERS.</p>
        <p>The stalls, which had been tastefully and ar- tistically arranged, were under the supervision of the following :—No. 1 Stall: Mrs. Jaye and Mrs. Lees. No. 2 Stall: Mrs. A. Gandy and Mrs. Atherton. Refreshment Stall: Mrs. Booth. Sunday School Teachers' Stall: Mrs. S. Oakes and Miss Booth. Gentlemen's Stall: Mrs. W. Bibby, jun. Flower and Fruit Stall: Mr. E. J. Fludder. No. 7 Stall: Miss N. Gandy. Tea Room: Mrs. Wm. Bibby, sen.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>REPRESENTATIVE GATHERING.</head>
        <p>A REPRESENTATIVE GATHERING.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday the Chairman said that he was greatly encouraged by the fact that every year the church seemed to make new friends at that function. Every church in Warrington ap- peared to be represented, and he thought that was a good thing. (Hear, hear.) It was very pleasing to have Mrs. Lindley with them. She was a regular attender at the sale, but that was the first time she had occupied an official posi- tion. (Applause.) They were obliged to have the sale this year, although some friends had expressed the view that it would have been better to postpone it. They could not possibly do that, because they needed every penny they could raise to meet the outgoing expenditure of the church and institute. They did not ask for money to build a new organ or to put up a new belfry, but to help them to carry on the Lord's work. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>CHURCHES WORSE OFF THROUGH THE</head>
        <p>CHURCHES WORSE OFF THROUGH THE</p>
        <p>WAR.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Lindley said that churches of every de- nomination would be worse off this year than they had been before, in consequence of the war, and, therefore, she felt that they ought to assist each other all they could to help forward the work, and that was why she hoped they would have a splendid sale in connection with that church. (A pplau se.) Mr. John Bibby proposed a hearty vote of thanks to Mrs. Lindley, and said that, in view of the many duties which she had to perform in connection with relief work, &amp;c., occasioned by the atrocious war, they were extremely fortu- nate to have her with them. (Hear, hear.) Mr. Jowett seconded, and the resolution was carried unanimously. Master Charles Dickens then presented Mrs. Lindley with a bouquet. SECOND DAY'S OPENING CEREMONY. Thursday's opening ceremony was performed by Mr. John Griffiths, who was accompanied by Mrs. Griffiths, the Pastor, the Rev. W. T. Jaye, presiding over the brief ceremony. • The Chairman said that he was pleased to report that the proceeds of the previous day's sale amounted to ,157 4s. 6d., which, although a little less than last year's first day—which was a record—was quite up to the average. (Ap- plause.) If Christian Work was Discontinueirl'</p>
        <p>Mr. Griffiths congratulated the workers upon the result of the opening day's sale, which, he thought, was very good, all things considered, at the present time. fie was there, he added, out of sympathy with them and the work they were seeking to do in the town. There were plenty of people, not only in Warrington, but everywhere, who would do nothing unless they were right in the front, but he thought that in connection with work of that kind they should, as Christians, observe the motto of St. 1.4au1, "In honour preferring one another." He made bold to say that work which was given heartily to the Christian Churches was one of the best assets that could be found in the country to- day. They sometimes met people who were discouraged in Christian work, and thought of giving it up, but when he came across such people he always asked them to imagine what would be the effect upon the town if every Christian force and influence were withdrawn for, say, just six or twelve months. The conse- quences of such a thing were unthinkable, and it was because he was convinced, as a Christian, that the work they gave' to the churches was contributing the solid foundations to the life of their country, that he gave of his leisure time. (Applause.) Mr. Atherton proposed, and Mr. Bibby sec- onded, a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Griffiths for their services. £l2O Raised.</p>
        <p>Yesterday the sale was opened by the Pastor (the Rev. W. T. Jaye), who said that the second day's proceeds amounted to ,:635 95., thus mak- ing the total for the first two days ,t 92 13s. 6d. Last evening we were informed that the total proceeds had reached about .t 126.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <head>WAR ITEMS.</head>
        <p>WAR ITEMS.</p>
        <p>REV. H. F. S. COLLIER RESIGNS CHAPLAINCY.</p>
        <p>We hear on good authority that the Vicar of Padgate has resigned the chaplaincy of the Pad- gate Cottage Homes. OFFICIAL RECOGNITION OF TRAINING</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>CORPS.</head>
        <p>CORPS.</p>
        <p>The Preparatory Training Corps which have been organised up and down the country have now received official recognition. Locally, the work of the Corps is proceeding apace, the drills being well attended, and a special section for Orford being in process of formation. Last week-end the members of the Fairfield and Howley Company put in musketry practice on the rifle range on Messrs. Rylands Bros.' Re- creation Ground. MORE WOUNDED ARRIVE AT RADDON COURT.</p>
        <p>Yesterday afternoon seven more wounded Britisher arrived at the Raddon Court Red Cross Hospital, whilst during the week five English soldiers and two Belgian left, conval- escent.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>WAR STORlES—continued.</head>
        <p>WAR STORlES—continued.</p>
        <p>us a little bit of war news—which is quite a novelty when we seem so rar fro ia land.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>TERRITORIALS TO INDIA?</head>
        <p>TERRITORIALS TO INDIA?</p>
        <p>"It is now thirteen days since we embarked, and we expect to be at Port Said either to- morrow night or early the next day. This letter will be posted from there, and I won't be able to write again until we disembark. Where that will be nobody but the one at the head of the Force can tell. All we others must do is to 'Wait and see.' Last night we passed about twelve ships going in the opposite direction. Who or what they had on board I cannot tell, but some of us imagine that they carried TerritorialA to India. lint then we make up all sorts of tales, just to give us something to talk about. "We have an awful lot of work to keep us going, because Nc7e must look after the horses as well as we possibly can under the circumstances —and don't they want some looking after, too! We manage to give them a little exercise each morning when the sea is calm, and so far only once were they unable to get it.</p>
        <p>• HEAT AND THE HORSES. "It was very hot for about a week after we left port, and several of our horses were down with fever and heat stroke. They are packed up so tightly that in such weather it is un- bearable down. below with them. However, for the last two days we have had a splendid head wind blowing, which has bucked the poor beggars up wonderfully. Roll on, when we dis- embark and get on the move! It will take two or three weeks to condition the horses again. . . "We only had it rough one day, but for several days we experienced a fairly heavy swell. There has been no sea sick-ness—no doubt owing to the fact that having plenty of work to do has kept our minds occupied." ARRIVED IN FRANCE. Curiously enough, a letter was received a day before that given above, which was sent from France, and said: "10th November, 1914. At last we (i.e., the Sialkot Brigade Cavalry Divi- sion) have completed our journey—as far as the water part of it goes—and we are now lying off the port of disembarkation. We reached here at dawn this morning, and hope to disembark this afternoon. I must not say anything about ! our future movements, but no doubt you will hear of the regiment before long. I am in per- feet health and quite happy."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>WESLEYAN ANNIVERSARY</head>
        <p>WESLEYAN ANNIVERSARY</p>
        <p>Bold Street Church Celebrates 64th Birthday. VISIT OF CONFERENCE PRESIDENT.</p>
        <p>Celebration of the 64th anniversary of the opening of the Bold-street Wesleyan Church was commenced on Sunday, when there were large congregations at both the morning and evening services. The special preacher for the day was the Rev. B. Lefroy Yorke, whose name is one to conjure with in the denomina- tion. At the morning service the choir rendered the anthem "My soul truly waiteth " (Rea), whilst Mrs. A. Booth sang the solo "Praise the Lord" from Handel's "Esther." In the even- ing Miss Hilda Cragg-James sang with great depth of feeling Carey's setting of the beautiful hymn, "Nearer, my God, to Thee." The anthem was Goss's "The Wilderness," in which the solo-prelude was given by Mr. E. H. Pearce.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>MAN'S NEED OF PRAYER.</head>
        <p>MAN'S NEED OF PRAYER.</p>
        <p>_ Preaching in the evening, the Rev. Lefroy Yorke said it was a good thing to reason; to think things out to their conclusion. Some people were afraid to reason in religion, but he liked people to reason, to ask questions, and to discuss, and his advice was never to repress the spirit of inquiry in their children and never to discourage it in young people. But the end of all reasoning must be prayer. When they had worked things out to their last con- clusion, this was what they came to. Let us pray! He had known many young men and women who were very clever and who were so fond of reasoning that they had reasoned them- selves out of prayer, away from the Church and away from religion. Therefore, he said to his hearers that night that, whilst giving the freest range to their intellectual powers, and whilst placing no restriction upon the reason which had been given them, they must deter- mine that the purpose of all their reasoning should be to lead them to the Throne of Grace and not away from it. In a time like the present, was not prayer their comfort and strength? Did they not feel in this time of upheaval and national anxiety that the Lord of Hosts—the God of Jacob—was their Refuge, and that they must come to the Throne of Grace? The preacher went on to say that be was glad to learn that next Sunday evening it had been arranged to hold a special service in that church, at which prayer would be offered up for the brave young men who, at the call of duty and their country's need, had man- fully responded.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>SACRIFICE AT THE CALL OF COUNTRY.</head>
        <p>THE SACRIFICE AT THE CALL OF COUNTRY.</p>
        <p>It made him feel proud of the country to which he belonged. It renewed one's confi- dence in the nation to find young men, drawn from all classes, disregarding comfort and sac- rificing home ties at the call of their country. The question came to some minds—Why should we pray; does it do any good? His answer was that they could not do without prayer; that they could not live without it. Just let them look at the alternative, viz., that they were shut up on this little planet and that their lives, and all the issues hanging thereto, were at the mercy of mere, ruthless forces. Would that life be worth living—no God of Justice to help them, no God of Mercy to succour them? To the heart of man came the answer: What- ever the logician may say, in the time of need we are bound to come to the Throne in prayer, and God says: "Come as often as you will; come when you will, but be sure and come in the time of need. You are never so welcome as when you are drawn to the Throne of Grace by your anxieties and sorrows." God drew them. to Himself through their needs.</p>
        <p>LIFE'S GREAT AID TERRIBLE BATTLE. Life was a great, a terrible battle—not simply a battle with circumstances, but a battle with their own characters and natures. They needed pity and Christ came into the world not to make God love them, but to show them that God did love them; to show that He felt for them, and felt for them deeply!'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>SPECIAL MUSICAL SERVICE.</head>
        <p>SPECIAL MUSICAL SERVICE.</p>
        <p>A special and largely attended musical ser- vice was held in the afternoon, when Mr. John Taylor presided and a brief and appropriate address was given by the Rev. W. T. Derry, superintendent of the circuit. Miss Hilda enigg-James gave splendid renditions of the solos, "Saviour of Sinners" (Cherubini) and "Lord, to Thee, each night" (Handel), and Miss M. Blaekburne sang "0, Divine Re- deemer" (Gounod). Mr. Bradford Barton played two violin solos, and members of the choir gave "The surrender of the soul" (Cornelius).</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>RED-LETTER DAY.</head>
        <p>A RED-LETTER DAY.</p>
        <p>The anniversary celebrations were continued on Thursday, when the President of the Wes- leyan Methodist Conference (the Rev. Dinsdale T. Young) paid the congregation a visit. In the afternoon he preached an eloquent sermon, based on Rebecca's cry—" What good shall my life do me?" (Genesis xxvii. 46). Later he took tea in the Schoolroom with a goodly-sized company of the Church-members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>CHRISTIANS AND THE WAR. "Should Have Denied God Had We Not Fought."</head>
        <p>CHRISTIANS AND THE WAR. "Should Have Denied God Had We Not Fought."</p>
        <p>In the evening the President gave a striking address in the chapel on the subject of "Chris- tianity and the Present War." Mr. C. E. Parker presided over a large attendance, and, in the course of a few appropriate introductory remarks, said that the thing that had occurred to him as the war had gone on was that the actual questions which had brought it about— questions such as the assassination of an Aus- trian Grand Duke and the violation of the treaty obligations in regard to Belgium—were sinking into a secondary place, and that they saw emerging from the conflict issues which were infinitely greater. They saw in the struggle an antagonism between two practically oppo- site political ideals; nay, more than that, a contest for supremacy between varying moral ideals and Christian ideals. The war brought to a point and challenged Christianity in its I most vital spot, and, as it proceeded, that was the phase of it that would be more and more brought before their thoughts.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>WAR UNCHRISTIAN, INHUMAN AND IRRATIONAL.</head>
        <p>WAR UNCHRISTIAN, INHUMAN AND IRRATIONAL.</p>
        <p>In the course of his address the Rev. Dinsdale T. Young said they must never forget that war was essentially un-Christian; that it was in- human and irrational. Of all the black angels that emanated from the pit of hell war was one of the blackest, and their earnest prayer was that God would speed the day when that black devil should be dashed down into that pit again, never to rise. He had been sometimes afraid during the progress of this war—and more espe- cially when their blood had been stirred to a holy wrath in face of the awful atrocities the enemy had perpetrated—lest the people should get accustomed to war, and, unconsciously, grow even appreciative of it—become, uncon- sciously but really, almost blood-thirsty. Chris- tians must never grow to delight in war. That they should do so was a peril—a peril to be prayed against and watched against. What an unspeakable thing was war? What an awful humiliation it was to the Christian Church that there should be war on the earth, after all the centuries that had elapsed since Christ came with His message of peace. But war was not a disproof of Christianity, as some people said; it was an evidence of the lack of Christianity—it was a proof of the need for multiplying Christian efforts. Let them never lower their testimony as to the intrinsic evil of</p>
        <p>Things That Are Worse Than War. But having said that, he was as sure asklie m was ;peaking to the that night that the Christian Church must make it its duty to say that there were things that were worse than war. There were moral evils, which, if they could not be suppressed otherwise, must, in the last resort, be suppressed by war. Many a time war had been legitimatised; war with all its horrors. If this country had stood aside in the present conflict it would have played the cowards' part. Recreant to righteousness, they would have al i lowed wickedness to run rampant. The Christian Church must make t clear, • firstly, that it detested war; but, secondly, that it believed that God used the horrible instrumentality of war to advance His purposes—that the Lord Almighty condeseend even through war to establish liberty and el%il.</p>
        <p>5</p>
        <p>isation and goodwill, and all that was beautiful and noble and honourable in the midst of the earth. If they did not believe that they would be infidels. But they did believe it; did believe that the Providence of God was working even in the midst of the present horrible war. Was there ever a war in history in which, so early on, the Hand of God had been made so plainly manifest? Already God had come closer to Britain, and to the Churches of Britain, by means of this war. The Church of Christ must continually assert it, that, even in this seeming evil, and from this seeming evil God was- educing good.</p>
        <p>Church Must Be Emphatically Patriotic. Continuing, the preacher said he con2eived it to be one of the just duties of the Christian Church in this war to be emphatically patriotic. Patriotism was one of the clamant demands made upon Christian people at the present time. Jesus loved His land and loyed His people, and one could not be a true Christian and yet not be a lover of his country for the love of one's country was part of the love of God. He sometimes had a fear lest the people in the theatres, the music-halls and the public- houses should be more ardent in their declara- tions of patriotism than were the people in the Christian Churches. It ought not to be so. Let them not be afraid of jingoism, out let their Churches ring with true patriotism in this, the hour of the Empire's peril. Was tiler° ever a people that had such a warranty for patriotism as the people of this land had to-day? If ever patriotism was reasonable and right, it was at this time. They had heard the croakers croak; they had heard them assert that diodern Britain was decadent, that the young men were mere lotus eaters; that the old pluck, and grit, and spirit of sacrifice had gone. Had they? A thousand times no, as, thank God, this time of war had shown to all the world! It was a mercy-gift from God to the nation. Those who had grown pessimistic at the spec- tacle of the aimless dance after pleasure which seemed to have eaten its way into the heart of the nati'un had received a great surprise, a wel- come surprise, for this war had given abundant proof that the ancient British spirit was not dead. Abroad the soldiers were fighting in the trenches as bravely as ever their warrior- ancestors did; at home the people had not been over-exultant when there had been victory, nor over-dejected when reverse had come—they had kept their beads and kept their passions under control; indeed, the splendid manner in which their ' men had conducted themselves on the field, and the fine dignity with which the people at home had conducted themselves made them thank God that they belonged to the British nation. Again, would it not make a pessimist into an optimist to see the political truce now obtaining? There were not many countries that could achieve a thing like that; it was just wonderful that they should have come to realise the time the poet spoke of when "None were for Party and all were for State!"</p>
        <p>A Tribute to the King. "Look at the manner in which the King ha 4 borne himself !" the speaker proceeded. "I honestly think that not enough attention has been called to it. Was there ever a monarch who carried himself more graciously in a trying time, more Christianly, more nobly, than George the Fifth is doing? Every word our King has uttered and everything he has done in this time of war, has been in harmony with the character of a man who believes his Bible and tries to practise its teachings." During the present war, the speaker went on to say, the Church should miss no opportunity of saying—not bitterly, but deliberately and dalmly, and with insuperable emphasis—that it believed that the cause of this country, and of her Allies, was most surely righteou4. They were not, primarily, fighting a nation, but a sect of evil principles—damnably evil principles and principles which, he fain would hope, were not so much represented in the German people as, first of all, in their Monarch, and then in the company of War Lords who surrounded him, and a clique who, in turn, surrounded them.</p>
        <p>The Kaiser a Comic Opera Napoleon. It was difficult to speak of the Kaiser with patience; and let it pass if lie described him as a Monarch with Napoleonic ambitions, but without an atom of Napoleonic ability—a dabbler in all kinds of subjects, who thus got a cheap reputation for versatility; a mounte- bank, who, doing many things, yet did nothing with conspicuous ability, lie, and the lnen with whom he bad surrounded himself, had deluded themselves with a vicious philosophy— a philosophy, the central dictum of which was that might was right. What an execrable theory that was? Let it have sway and there would be an end to liberty everywhere. This country and her Allies, by fighting against that principle, were fighting for liberty for the world, helping to root out a doctrine of devils that was only worthy of hell. Yes, they were not so much fighting a race, as fighting a vicious, anti-Christian, anti-Scriptural, atheistic phil- osophy—fighting a spiritual war because they were fighting an evil theology. ;</p>
        <p>A Warning for England in the War. Touching on German theology—of which they had heard too much in some of the English Churches—the speaker said it began by denying the claims of the Papacy, and was ending by denying the claims of Christ. Could that be true of the Church of Martin Luther? In the war was a warning to England—a warning that a nation might have the higher culture, about which the Germans had talked so much, and yet under pressure of the execrable ideas to which he had referred, that nation might be guilty of deeds which would make even a decent savage hang down his head with shame. "We should have denied our Lord," added the speaker, with emphasis, "if we had not entered as combatants in this terrible war!" What was the duty of the Christian Church in this war? What was the Church to do? He ventured to say that she ought to have done very much what, thank God, she was doing. First of all it was her duty to make a very adequate, a very splendid contribution of men! That she was doing! And in that connection he was proud of the share of the Wesleyan Church. Thirty-two thousand declared Wes- leyans had taken up arms on behalf of their country, and, reckoning the undeOared adher- ents of the Church, no fewer than 60,000 Wes- leyans were at that moment engaged in fighting for their country. . That was something to thrill the Church with pride. A 'second duty laid upon the Churches at this time was th.e duty' of prayer. Whatever else the Churches did during this war they must pray. The proceedings concluded with a vote of thanks to the President for his address and with the singing of the National Anthem.</p>
        <p>RUMMAGE SALE FOR RED CROSS. In aid of the Warrington Red Cross work, a most successful rummage sale was held on Sat- urday in the Kendrick-street Schools. There was no formal opening, but Miss Sylvia Broad- bent, representing the Red Cross Society, made a brief speoch of thanks to all who had gener. ously sent gifts, and also to those ladies who had given their services at the various stalls. The proceeds of the sale amounted to the handsome sum of ,tl5 12s. 7d., which will be divided between the Raddon Court Hospital and other Red Cross work.</p>
        <p>To obtain GLASSES 01 the highest accuracy, so as to secure the maximum benefit Possible :from wearing them, CONSULT Johnston Watson,, SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, 84, BRIDGE ST., Warrington.: Steel Spectacles from 216. Rolled Gold from 8/6. Hours of Business, 9 to 9-30. Saturday, 9 to 9-30. Please Note.—Mr. Watson attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday. 11 to 5-30; Thursday, 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday, 11 to 5-30.</p>
