      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <p>9 (Regd. Trade Mark) COFFEE EMENCE, The Cofee Maid, of Symington's, says: " My motto is: 'Keep the kettle boiling Then I can always have a cup of Symingtoi instantly." THOS. SYMING TON 0 CO.. EdinburAh and London.</p>
        <p>before," said he; "I've been waiting all these years for some simple sign of courtesy, and it's come at last—from foreigners!"</p>
        <p>One's Loss Another's Gain. On Saturday last, I fell in with a Canal Company's official, and he told me With a half- smile that he and one or two others in the office in which he has served for the last score years or so would probably ere long be; reduced to the necessity of " working " a Certain night boat from the East Lancashire border to Man- chester. He went on to explain that on the outbreak, of war the rate of pay to captains and mates on his section was so rednced that quite a number of the men went off in search of other employment. Several of them obtained it readily in Warrington, and are now not only receiving better wages, but are enjoFing the blessing of regular hours, finishing work at the close of the day and having their evenings at leisure. This has caused such a shortage of labour on the canal in question as seriously to embarrass the management; hence the clerks' comical stiggestion that they might have to turn out on to the towpath and take charge of the more important of the boats.</p>
        <p>s 1,154 , 17,11* tit g:',llv</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <head>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</head>
        <p>JOTTINGS BY AN OLD JOURNALIST.</p>
        <p>Longevity. • In his lecturette at Stockton Heath, Dr. Man- son felt called upon to explain the unenviable position of his own profession as a class in the list of .occupational mortality. It has been re- served for me to add, what the speaker's modesty made it difficult for him to suggest, that the doctor's irregular hours and habits, un- avoidable in the case of the general practitioner liable to be called out repeatedly in the night, are calculated to sae one's vitality and shorten life. However, "he lived' long who liveth well," and none is more devoted to the public interest or more self-sacrificing than your aver- .age medical man. There is another apt if hack- neyed quotation: "Whom the gods love die young." Doctors may possibly comfort one an- other with these words, but what of the par- sons? These latter head the "list of occupa- tional mortality " ! To be sure, it is open to them to retort by emphasising the fairly obvious moral. Points of View.</p>
        <p>Next to a country market where the voluble quack still has a chance, commend me to the working class barber's shop with its oratory. I dropped into such a place some days ago, and, of course, the talk was all of war. One who had been in the Army had the floor for the moment, and he was contrasting the liberal allowances made to raw youths entering upon training for Territorial battalions with the inadequate offers , made to induce ex-soldiers to rejoin the colours. Said he: "I've had a communication direct from the War Office asking me to go, but I've taken no notice of it, and I shan't do till they offer better terms. These young 'uns that's joined the 'Ter- riers' won't handle a rifle this side Christmas, and see what money they're getting; whereas if I went I should only have a fortnight's or three weeks' drill, and then I should be sent off to the front. I'm not having any; not me!" There's a fine example of patriotism for you. Of course, it is easy enough for an armchair critic to write thus—just as it is for one who has passed the age limit to dictate to younger men as to their supreme duty in the present crisis. An- other in the company just referred to told of a fellow hotly denouncing shirkers. "If I might have my way," he declared, "every chap under 35 who at a time like this refused to enlist should be itfimediately shot as a traitor to his country."</p>
        <p>"You only seem a youngish man yourself." commented a hearer, "and you seem healthy and fit; what age might you be?" "Me?" returned the enthusiast, "I'm 38!" These incidents came to mind again as I read the report of the Philomath's debate.</p>
        <p>Another " Fishy" Story.</p>
        <p>The poor excuse which a local prisoner cori- victed of stealing fish evidently thought better than none somehow reminded me of a miscel- laneous collection which I have by me, and in an old number of an American paper I found it reported that a remarkable incident happened daring a certain schooner's halibuting trip, there being included in the catch a small halibut which bore a distinction much out of the ordin- ary. Two years ago one of the crew brought up a baby halibut on his trawl, and it was thrown overboard. Before releasing it, however, the fisherman cut the initials "L.R." plainly upon its back. Recently some of the crew were sur- prised when a halibut was hoisted on board bearing the familiar initials "L.R." Instantly some of them recalled the incident of two years ago; the fish had been caught again by the crew of the same craft There seems to have been a sort of suspicion that the man did not cut all his initials on the fish—that, not to put too fine a point upon it, he omitted "1.A." from between the other two letters.</p>
        <p>Why this Thusness? Warrington Guardians seem to me to be hope- lessly behind the age in that matter of the Workhouse inmates' Christmas beverage. Such discussions as they had at their last meeting really belong to the last century. As a young reporter I reckoned them among the hardy an- nuals, but gradually the temperance party effected a change in this respect, and I am bound to say that in both a Yorkshire and a Lancashire workhouse of which I had this ex- perience it was decidedly for the better. In those unions a return to the former conditions would no more be thought of by the Guardians than it would be desired by the inmates. It was a revelation to me when I was first told that our local institution was so far behin'a the times. Mr. T. Dale, one of the three members who had the pluck to protest against the mis- taken, old-fashioned practice, in making a pro- posal to bring the Warrington Board into line with the enlightened custom of up-to-date author- ' , ities, took occasion to cite the Bishop of London in support. I might add the testimony of several other bishops, including their Lordships of Liverpool, Ripon and Lincoln. The latter wrote a while ago: " I thought the matter had teen settled for ever for British people when our late King .per- mitted healths ' to be drunk and toasts given in the Services without intoxicants." The Lady of the Lamp. "All is going well. The ideal nurse con- veys that impression by every tone, every ges- ture, each. expression of her face. A confident sense of safety enwraps the patient. There is nothing to trouble about; there can be nothing to trouble, about, with that strong gentle hand smoothing the pillow, that restful, reassuring voice in the ear, and above, that calm, wise face, grown saintlike with the practised virtues of a true lady of the lamp." Something like this I read a year or two ago in an article by one who wrote from abundant experience; and it enables me specially to commend to the readers' sym- pathetic consideration the announcement of Mrs. Ridgway's intended lecture on "Florence Night- ingale" (vide advertisement).</p>
        <p>Our Belgian Guests.</p>
        <p>All who were able to do so have gladly placed empty property at the disposal of local Distress Committees for the especial benefit of unfortu- nate Belgian refugees, and these have been assured of hospitable entertainment in this country. Wounded soldiers of the same nation- ality 'who have been sent here for hospital treatment have likewise been cordially wel- comed by multitudes of grateful admirers. In a Manchester cafe the other day I witnessed a scene which, although not a word passed, testi- fied eloquently to the mutual good feeling now prevailing. A nurse entered with three wounded Belgians, and the interesting quartet seated themselves at a small,table. A gentleman who had put on his hat preparatory to departure hesitated as if anxious to convey sympathy and thanks to the strangers, and then, as if he had discovered a way of overcoming the language difficulty, walked up to the group. Touching one of the foreigners on the shoulder, he simply raised his hat. The three soldiers at once sprang to their feet and smilingly returned the salute, and, having established complete understanding without a syllable being uttered. the Englishman with another bow went on his d coun way. Since then I have met an old man who was evidently rather bet ei4teredwoeurnedbe_ cause three Belgians whom he had a little farther up the road had raised th mtheier hats to him. "Nobody's ever done t</p>
        <p>Ly;</p>
        <p>WELCO Fcßi FETE PURE BRITISH COCOA SAVE THE, COUPONS</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0132" n="art0132">
        <head>FOREIGN MISSIONS. Their Work Detailed at Wycliffe Anniversary.</head>
        <p>FOREIGN MISSIONS. Their Work Detailed at Wycliffe Anniversary.</p>
        <p>" CHRISTIANITY WILL WIN."</p>
        <p>Successful missionary anniversary celebrations took place last week-end at the Wycliffe Con- gregational Church, Warrington, when a visit was paid by the Revs. R. Griffith (Madagascar) and A. H. Caren (South China). THE VISITORS WELCOMED.</p>
        <p>•--- —..-..- This deputation was welcomed-at a meeting held on Saturday evening in the Memorial Buildings, at which, despite the unfavourable state of the weather, there was a good attend- ance. Refreshments, which had kindly been Provided by the Ladies' Sewing %Meeting, were served. Afterwards Mr. J. W. Slack occupied the chair, there being also present on the plat- form the Revs. T. T. James and C. P. Pitt (pastor and assistant pastor).</p>
        <p>MISSIONARY CAUSE " NOTDEAD YET." The Chairman welcomed their visitors. It did not seem very. long, he said, since they met for their last missionary celebrations. The London Missionary Society had then been en- gaged in raising £lOO,OOO, most of which sum they now had. Evidently the cause of missions was not dead yet. Some people said that, now the world was so highly civilised, there was no need for missionaries, but he was afraid that if they closed all the churches the nation would 8i )fl 'go tc the dogs." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>THE CHINESE REVOLUTION. In replying, the Rev. A. H. Caren said that during the last three years they had had an anxious time in China owing to the revolution— which had made China a republic after it had been an empire for ov. 4,000 years! They claimed that Ch.ristianiry had brought about that improvement; the people had destroyed all the gods, opium smoking was now prohibited, and Christianity had also prevented the revolu- tionists from torturing their victims. There vas now going on a swaying backwards and for- wards between Christianity and the other Chinese religions; but in the end Christianity is wild win. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>MISSIONS AND THE WAR.</head>
        <p>MISSIONS AND THE WAR.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. Griffith also responded. They weie face to face with a great war, and the ques- tion was often asked: "How will this war affect Christian missions?" Was it going to make the people disloyal to their L0....1 and Saviour? He (the speaker) was encouraged by what he had seen and heard in going about the country; they were on the verge of a religious revolution. Referring to his missionary work in Madagas- car, Mr. Griffith said the great difficulty was the influence of the French who occupied that island. He would not depreciate the brave people who were fighting by our side; but for the past ,years the missionary in Madagascar had not had official recognition. The French there had become materialistic—though they could not be blamed in the circumstances—and had, for the time, separated themselves from Christian- ity. However, the missionaries had not been discouraged, and there were more Protestants in Madagascar than in the whole of France. (Ap- plause.) The Rev. T. T. James thanked the Chairman for his kind services and the speakers for their inspiring addresses. A collection taken on behalf of the London Missionary Society, realised £3 ss. 11d.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>SUNDAY SERVICES. Sweeping Eastern Wale of Civilisation.</head>
        <p>THE SUNDAY SERVICES. Sweeping Eastern Wale of Civilisation.</p>
        <p>In the church on Sunday morning the service was conducted by the Pastor, who took for the text of his sermon, "Thy kingdom come" (Matt. vi. 10). These words, said the preacher, implied the whole scope of their missionary ideal. With their conception of what the great. religions— such as those of the East—had been, there was need not only for brave pioneers, but also for great scholars. There were certain races destined to die out, but the Eastern nations had a great part to play in the future of the world. The attention of the people was at pres- ent concentrated on Europe; yet, perhaps, future history would come chiefly from the East, for a great wave of civilisation had swept from West to East. They had here two lines of approach to the great missionary ideal; they must take the Gospel to the dying nations because they believed in the individual character of the soul, and to the awakening nations because Christian- ity had a great part in the evolution of the future.</p>
        <p>The choir effectively rendered the anthem, "0 ! for a closer walk with God" (Myles B. Foster), Mr. W. H. Payton presiding at the organ.</p>
        <p>AMONG THE CHILDREN. During the afternoon the Rev. R. Griffith visited the Sunday school, and entertained the children with a delightful address about the boys and girls of Madagascar. Several special hymns were sung, Mr. T. F. Wallington offi- ciating at the organ. Mr. W. Brown (the super- intendent) presided.</p>
        <p>"REGENERATION OF FRANCE." The pulpit in the evening was occupied by Mr. Griffith, who had preached at the Elmwood-. avenue Church in the morning. The text was, "Speak unto the children of Israel that they go forward" (Exodus xiv. 15)., He said the words were applicable to the triumphant little church of Madagascar. They could not neglect the smaller things; Madagascar was a strategic. point in the world—a strategic point for Christ! It would play a great part in the regeneration of France; right would be might, and it was a great cause.</p>
        <p>COLLECTIOkS OVER £6O. Tlie evening anthem was "As the hart pants" (Afendelssohn), and Mr. Payton again accom- panied on the organ. The day's collections totalled £62 10s. Bd., making the whole Wycliffe contribution to the London Missionary Society 465 16s id</p>
        <p>- The Rev. A. H. Caren preached in Runcorn on Sunday, and addressed a well-attended meeting at Ellnwood-avenue Church on Monday night Upon the subject of his work in China.,</p>
        <p>Scholars' Teeth. The following recommendation of the Medical Inspection Suh-comnaittee was approved by the Education Committee at their meeting on Mon- day: "That Mr. lie(Hey H. Taylor be tempo- rarily appointed school dental officer at a salary at the rate of £B4 per annum, from the 20th October, 1914, until such time as a permanent school dental officer is appointed."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</head>
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS.</p>
        <p>" DON'T HIT MOTHER." Children's Appeal To Their Father.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court on Thursday, before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair) and other magistrates, William Henry Grundy, 5, WinwiCk-road, pleaded not guilty to assaulting his wife, but guilty to, assaulting her sister, Miss Eliza Clark, of Earlestown.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel appeared for the complainants, and said defendant was now working at Messrs. Crosfield's. There were five children, and one of them, who was seriously ill, resided with its grandmother at Earlestown. On Sunday Mrs. Grundy went over to see the child and camel back with her sister at about 11 o'clock. De- fendant was in bed and the two sisters slept in a separate room. When Mrs. Grundy called her husband at 5.20 on Monday he took no notice. She prepared ,tea for him, but, seeing that he was not getting up, she went back to bed. She called the lodger, who slept in the same room as her husband, at 7.15, but even • then Grundy would not get up. He eventually went downstairs—at about B.4o—in a very vio- lent temper and began to quarrel with his wife, asking her why she had not called him. She said she had and he then used most disgusting language and rushed at her, giving her a vio- lent blow on the temple and knocking her down. The wife's sister was upstairs and heard the children say "Don't hit mother." Mrs. Grundy said "I cannot stand this- I shall go for Mr. Wainwright again!" and the sister shouted from upstairs: " Yes, I should. It would he the best thing !" Defendant rushed upstairs to the sister, who was in her night attire, and called her vile names. He kicked he'? in the back, caught hold of her head, and said "Come out of this . . . . bed." He threw her on to the floor and dealt her several blows on the head. The wife appealed to him not to hit her sister, but, before the girl had time to get her clothes on properly, he pushed her through the back door and his wife through the front. They complained to a police officer. Mrs. Grundy and the girl gave evidence, bear- ing out this statement, and Detective-Sergeant Reddington said that when he saw defendant he admitted having assaulted the girl because she interfered. Defendant was in a violent temper and threatened to hit the girl in his (the officer's) presence.</p>
        <p>DEFENDANT'S VERSION. Defendant said he went to bed at about 10.30 p.m. on Sunday night, knowing that his wife had gone to Earlestown with the next-door neighbour. There was no necessity for the sister to come at all. She had caused endless bother and seemed to have a power over his wife. When they came home on Sunday they stayed downstairs " jangling " and giggling until after two in the morning, ,whilst his wife's sister was jeering and referring disparagingly to him until three o'clock in the morning in the next room. His wife did not call him at five o'clock, and when he was arguing with her downstairs the sister came down, dr,rsel, and said: "Go on, Bess; smash his face in!" and put her fist in his face. He lost his temper and struck her across the cheek with his open hand. He denied punching her at all, and Added that he did not touch his wife.</p>
        <p>The magistrates agreed to dismiss the case brought by the wife, and for the assault on the girl they imposed a fine of 155., including costs, the Chairman saying that a great deal of unhappiness might be obviated by the exer- cise of a little fairness on each side.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>"ANXIOUS TO JOIN THE ARMY."</head>
        <p>"ANXIOUS TO JOIN THE ARMY."</p>
        <p>Medically Unfit Man's Artful Ruse.</p>
        <p>At the Blugh Police Court on Tuesday, James White, of no settled abode, was changed with falsely pretending to be a deserter on November 20th.</p>
        <p>The Chief Constable stated that P.C. Bartley arrested the prisoner on the day in question, and when challenged he said he was. a deserter from the Lancashire Fusiliers. Prisoner was brought before the Court on Saturday morning and pleaded guilty to being a deserter, and he was ordered to be detained, pending the arrival of an escort. However, from inquiries made, it appeared ;that prisoner had been discharged from the Army on medical grounds. Whilst at Blackburn he attempted to join again, •lnit, knowing that' he would be rejected on medical grounds, he made an arrangement by which another man passed the doctor and handed prisoner the necessary papers. However, the prisoner went away from the barracks, suspect- ing that the Commanding Officer was aware of the arrangement.—Prisoner, who said he .was anxious to get in the Army, was sentenced to seven days' imprisonment.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</head>
        <p>BOROUGH POLICE COURT</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Councillor H. Roberts (in the chair), Messrs. T. Locker, J. Rymer Young, and C. Jackson Holmes. . Confrontation Scene in a Pawn Shop.—Sarah Rawlinson, a married woman, of 12, Morris- court, was charged with obtaining by false pre- tences on November 11th one pair of boots and one pair of shoes, valued at 14s. 6d., the pro- perty of Thomas Broadhurst, of 76, Mersey- street.—The evidence showed that prisoner went into Mr. Broadhurst's shop, and by saying she was a regular customer and was well known to Mr. Broadhurst, the boy in charge allowed her to take the property. She gave her address as 12, Ellesmere-street, which proved to be false. She later went to a pawnshop and pledged the property. It was later identified as belonging to Mr. Broadhurst, who was in the pawnshop when the woman was sent for. She again stated that she knew Mr. Broadhurst and when that gentleman remarked: "Do you know I am Mr. Broadhurst?" she replied: "Oh! How you have altered !"—The prisoner, who said she intended to pay for the boots, was ordered to pay a fine of 405., or go to prison for one month.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY.</p>
        <p>_ Before Mr. J. Rymer Young (in the chair), Mr.</p>
        <p>W. T. Williams and Dr. Naden. Horse Owner's Error of Judgment.—Peter .Wilkinson, Long-lane, Orford, was summoned for permitting a horse to he worked whilst in an unfit state, and his own son, Frank, was summoned for cruelty to the animal by working it.—Acting-Sergeant Armstrong sail that he stopped the younger defendant and pointed out that the horse was lame in the left fore-leg. He said it had been in the field and had caught a chill, and that it was only suffering from stiff ness.—lnspector Dee (R.S.P.C.A.) said the horse was very lame and was suffering from a -navicular disease. The foot was hot and swollen. The elder defendant said he had not seen it like that before. The horse had been destroyed.—The father told the Bench that he thought the foot was only stiff, and considered that a little exercise wodid do it good.—Both eases, were dismissed on payment of costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>DRINK IN WAR TIME. Free Church Council and the Licensing Justices.</head>
        <p>DRINK IN WAR TIME. Free Church Council and the Licensing Justices.</p>
        <p>To the Editor of "The Examiner."</p>
        <p>Sir,—At the Warrington Free Church Execu- tive meeting, held on Friday, November 20th, the following resolution was unanimously passed:—</p>
        <p>"The executive deeply regrets that the peti- tion, containing 2,500 signatures, asking the Chief Constable and magistrates to limit the hours of sale for intoxicating liquors has not been complied with." The executive considered that naturally the police have been lenient both to women and soldiers during the recent excitement; that the urgent request of Lord Kitchener ought to have had more weight with the authorities, and that in time of war the country cannot afford the present expenditure on alcohol. The executive was surprised that the example of Russia and France had so little influence upon those responsible. The signatories in- cluded Canon Willis, the rector of the Parish Church; the Rev. Father Wilson, rector of St. Mary's, Buttermarket-street; and the Rev. T. T. James. the president of the \Warrington. Free Church Council. Yours, on behalf of the Warrington Free Church Council,</p>
        <p>H. OGDEN, Secretary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>t; DISTRICT pi Ew3</head>
        <p>t; DISTRICT pi Ew3</p>
        <p>PAD GATE.</p>
        <p>Wesley Guild.—A very interesting paper en- titled "Stories of Old Songs" was given at the meeting of the Guild on Monday evening by Miss Upson, of Warrington. The songs re- ferred to by Miss Upson in her paper were sung by the following members of the Guild :—Mrs. Stewart, the Misses J. Ward and L. Mason, and Mr. W. J. Bate. The Rev. A. J. Morton pre- sided, and Miss Bennett, Miss Stewart and Mrs. W. J. Bate were the accompanists.</p>
        <p>Manchester-road To Be Improved.—Mr. Den- ham presided over a good attendance at a Parish Meeting held in the Church Schools on Thurs- day evening. In answer to a question, the Clerk (Mr. Wright) said that the County Sur- veyor had promised to attend to Manchester- road as early as possible. It was going to be tar-macadamed all the way down, and a thor- oughly good road would be made of it.—The Chairman: A good deal of money is being spent on it at the present time.</p>
        <p>To Help the Prince of Wales' Fund.—Sl the Parish Meeting on Thursday it was decided that Messrs. Denham, Mather, Brimelow, Plin- ston, England and Blackwell form a local com- mittee for. the purpose of raising money in various ways for the Prince of Wales' Fund. The money collected will be paid into the Dis- trict War Fund Account.—lt was explained that there were two working parties making garments in the district, so that Padgate had not been behindhand.—The suggestion of the Chairman (Mr. Denham) that a house-to-house collection be made was approved by the 'meet- ing.—Alderman Bennett mentioned that there were lots of people who did not now contribute to any fund, and expressed the opinion that the enormous obligations with which we were faced should be borne by the Government so that all taxpayers would have to pay. The low sum already contributed seemed ridiculous when compared with the .27,000,000 per week being spent 'on sheer destruction. • They wanted to see that neither the soldiers nor their depend- ents suffered at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>"HIDEOUS, BARBAROUS, AND A DISGRACE."</head>
        <p>"HIDEOUS, BARBAROUS, AND A DISGRACE."</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett's Description of Existing Drainage System.</p>
        <p>£350 SCHEME APPROVED.</p>
        <p>A temporary sewerage scheme, to last about ten years, and to cost £346 or .1350, was approved by the parishioners at a parish meeting, over which Mr. Denham presided, on Thursday even- ing.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>"THE HARDY ANNUAL."</head>
        <p>"THE HARDY ANNUAL."</p>
        <p>After other matters had been discussed, The Chairman, referring to the main topic, said: "Now we come to the hardy annual." In view of serious complaints to the Local Govern- ment Board, it was, he continued, absolutely necessary that they should do something to re- move the nuisance which existed. The pro- posals of Mr. Newcombe (the surveyor) and Mr. Mather were approved at a recent Parish Council meeting, and they were recommended to that meeting for approval. In regard' to the nuisance around Bruche Hall, it was proposed to pick up the drainage at a point where the pit was, and carry it in a straight line across the field, where there would be a tank to collect the sewage, and the effluent would go into the brook. The ditch in Padgate-lane. by Mr. Houghton's garden, would be treated, and it was proposed to connect the drains on either side of the road and run them alongside the ditch to a tank in the meadow, and then to the brook. It was proposed to pipe the ditch by the Police Station, so that the smell would be removed from the road. Another tank would be put behind the churchyard, and the effluent would again go to the brook. The £350, which was the estim- ated cost, would have to come out of the rates, and a rate of is. in the .2 for two half-years would meet the case.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>QUESTION OF AMALGAMATION.</head>
        <p>A QUESTION OF AMALGAMATION.</p>
        <p>Alderman Bennett characterised the nuisance at present existing as "hideous, barbarous, a disgrace, an abomination and a danger to health." The present scheme was only a make- shift. If the question of the extension of the borough boundaries matured in the next few years, as he believed it would, they might then get a more comprehensive scheme. His family had been cited as the chief offenders, but that was not so. Nevertheless, they would have to pay a large proportion of the rate, but they did not mind that, so long as the nuisance was removed. They would not cry out about the rate, but would bear their fair share.</p>
        <p>Mr. Brimelow said the present proposals would be a waste of money. He had gone into• the matter, and did not think a proper scheme would cost as much as had been stated.</p>
        <p>The Chairman mentioned that the engineering difficulties were very great. If the village was situated twenty feet higher there would be no difficulty. A full scheme would involve an out- lay of £15,000, and their rateable value was only £B,OOO, so that it would cost more than 'they were worth.</p>
        <p>_ The question of the effluent going into the river was raised, and Mr. Mather said the In- spector of Rivers Pollution had been round and would make a report later. Mr. G. Walker said they were assured by Mr. Newcombe that the septic tanks would make the effluent as clear as water. Although the pro- posed scheme was not perfect it was the only one within the financial possibilities of the township.</p>
        <p>A BAR TO PROGRESS. Several members pointed out that the existing nuisance was preventing the extension of the village, as builders would not erect new property where there was no proper sewerage system. Mr. E. England moved that the proposals be approved, and said he was under the impres- sion that( on the whole, Padgate\ was the healthiest suburb of Warrington, though he must admit that there were places where a nuisance did arise. Mr. Antrobus said that the scheme would only benefit the one class—those who were commit- ting the nuisance. He suggested that cesspools should 'be constructed, as they would be all right if properly cleaned out. _</p>
        <p>Alderman -Bennett described the view that they should submit to the obvious nuisance in- definitely as a medizeval one. The Parish Coun- cil were trying to get over the difficulty at a small cost.</p>
        <p>After further discussion, Mr. Mather sug- gested that property and land owners should be approached with a view to getting them to sub- scribe something towards the cost. Alderman Bennett mentioned that he was in favour of a universal income tax for all im- provements.</p>
        <p>Mr. Plinston seconded Mr. England's pro- position. which was carried unanimously, with the addition, agitated for by several members, that the nuisances at Brook-lane and Fearn- head-lane be considered as early as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>GREAT SANK EY.</head>
        <p>GREAT SANK EY.</p>
        <p>Choral Society's Social.—A successful social yvas held in connection with the local Choral Society on Saturday evening, the Rev. J. R. Jones presiding over a company of upwards of a hundred members and friends, and making an appeal for the funds of the Girls' Friendly Society, who, he explained, wanted money to buy wool to work into garments for the soldiers at the front. The first part of the evening was devoted to games, and the latter part to music and dancing, lqessrs. Ray Bros., of Great Sankey, being the musicians.</p>
        <p>For " Mr. Atkins" Christmas Box.—ln aid of Princess Mary's Christmas Fund, the Band of Hope children gave a highly successful con- cert in the St. Mary's Sunday School on Friday evening of last week, when the Rev. J. R. Jones occupied the chair, and, in the course of a few remarks during an interval, mentioned that over forty of his old choristers had joined either the Army or the Navy, to fight not only for glory, but also for the homes they had left behind. Miss Clarke and Miss Darlington were accorded a hearty vote of thanks for the trouble they took in promoting the concert, the pro- ceeds of which amounted to £B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>The Poor Rate..—The magistrates at the Altrincham Sessions on Monday sanctioned a poor ;ate for Lymm for the ensuing half-year of Is. 6d. on buildings and 9d. on land, the same as for the last half-year.</p>
        <p>A Prisoner of the Germans.—News has been received in Lynam this Week that Private Wil- liam Massey, of the Ist Battalion. Cheshire Regiment, is a prisoner of war having been captured by the enemy, at Mons. Private Massey, who has written that he is all right, was originally reported amongst the missing.</p>
        <p>Injury Added to Injury.—Last week-end one of the wounded soldiers staying at the Lymm Red Cross Hospital met with a nasty, accident. It appears that he was riding on the step of Mr. Boumphrey's motor car, en route for the railway station, when he fell off and received serious injuries to the head. He was taken back to the hospital, where he is progressing favourably.</p>
        <p>Missionary Gatherings.—The Congregational Church held their annual Missionary Service on Sunday, the 15th inst., when the Pastor, the Rev. J. A. Penaliggon, preached on Missionary matters, and on Tuesday last a public meeting was held, at which the Rev. A. H. Caren, a re- turned Missionary from Poklo, China, gave an address on work in South China. The Rev. T. T. James, of Warrington, also addressed the meeting. Mr. J. Hardy Holt proposed a vote of thanks to the speaker, which Mr. James seconded. The Rev. J. A. Penaliggon presided.</p>
        <p>Wednesday Footballers in Manchester.—The Lymm Wednesday football team paid a visit to Manchester this week, and there made a one- goal draw with the Post Office eleven in a Man- chester and District Wednesday League engage- ment. Early on in the game the Lymm team took the lead, thanks to H. Dodson. Twenty minutes later the Manchester team equalised. The scores were even at the interval, and, for the remainder of the play, both defences proved superior to the attack, no further scoring being registered. There will be no League match next week. There is a movement on foot to arrange a charity match with the Lymm Sat- urday eleven, to be played probably on Christ- mas Day, when the proceeds will be given to the local Red Cross Hospital Committee. Red Cross Hospital Committee's Supper.—At their own, expense the members of the Lymm Red Cross Hospital Committee sat down to an enjoyable supper in the Masonic Hall on Wed- nesday evening, Mrs. Watkin (the president) being in the chair. After the repast Mr. Wat- kin made a strong appeal to young men to join the Army. Songs were rendered by Mrs. Sleigh, the Misses Davies (High Leigh) and Brundrett, and Messrs. W. Whittle (Warring- ton), H. Odber, H. Gibbon and Rixon. Mr. Cooke presided at the piano.—During the even- ing a representative of the Working Men's Committee handed over a cheque for £25, repre- senting two weeks' contributions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0145" n="art0145">
        <head>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH.</head>
        <p>PRIMITIVE METHODIST CHURCH.</p>
        <p>Successful Anniversary Celebrations.</p>
        <p>Anniversary services in connection with the above place of worship were held on Sunday and Monday. On Sunday two sermons were preached by Mr. Hinde, of the Primitive Methodist Theological College, Manchester, to good congregations. A public tea was held on Monday evening, to which a large number sat down. Mrs. J. Ed- wards had charge of the arrangements, being assisted by Mesdames Welford, E. Moss, It. Priestner, Knowles, J. 11. Warham and Champ. and many other triends</p>
        <p>„ The schoolroom was well filled for the even- ing lecture. Mr. G. L. Welford presided, and, in his opening address, spoke about the pre- sent crisis and the issues that might result therefrom, especially in the East. it was. he said, interesting to speculate as to what might be, and upon that speculation to watch closely each day, through the papers, the happenings that would lead to the final settlement. between all the nations concerned.</p>
        <p>The Rev. R. W. Ferguson conducted the devotional exercises.</p>
        <p>Following In His Footsteps. The Chairman called upon Mr. F. Short (the Provincial Secretary of the Polytechnic Holi- day Tours), of Altrincham, to give his lee curo on "Round About Jerusalem." The Lecturer, whose remarks were illustrated by limelight views, which were thrown upon the canvas by Mr. Harry Short, interested his audience for an hour and a half, taking them in imagination to the following places of inter- est, which he had visited many times :—Jaffa, the Flains of Sharon, the Hills of Judea, the Holy City, the Tower of David, the Road to Bethlehem, Rachael's Tomb, the Pools of Solomon, the City of David, the Church of the Nativity, the Field of the Shepherds, Jericho, the Dead Sea, the Jordan Valley, Bethany, the Mount of Olives, the Valley of the Bidron, the Golden Gate, the Temple Area, the Dome of the Rock, the Pool of Siloam, the Jews' Wailing Place, via Dolorosa, Pilate's House, the Damascus Gate, Solomon's Quarries, Jeremiah's Grotto, Jerusalem (on the side of the North), Calvary, and the Garden Tomb. Hearty applause at tlie conclusion of the lec- ture expressed the appreciation of the audience. A comprehensive resolution of thanks was passed, on the proposition of Mr. G. L. Welford, seconded by the Rev. R. W. Ferguson, and a most successful church anniversary was con- cluded by the singing of the Doxology and the Benediction. The total proceeds will be about £25.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0146" n="art0146">
        <head>CITIZEN SUNDAY. 'Big Procession Attends Special Service In Parish Church.</head>
        <p>CITIZEN SUNDAY. 'Big Procession Attends Special Service In Parish Church.</p>
        <p>Citizen Sunday was celebrated in the usual manner at Lymm, a lengthy procession com- posed of members of the various local bodies and societies that go to make up the corporate life of th 6 little town parading the §treets and then attending en masse the special after- noon service in the Parish Church.</p>
        <p>The procession was marshalled in the vicinity of the Cross, and, headed by the Lymm Sub- scription Band, wended its way through the tortuous streets of the tiny town to the fine old church. Immediately behind the instrumen- talists, who, by the way, were under the con- ductorship of Mr. W. Daniels, came the mem- bers of the Lymm, Heatley and High Legh Troops of Boy Scouts, under the charge of Scoutmasters Nash, Surridge and Farrar, and Assistant Scoutmaster Timperley. The boys were also accompanied by the Secretary of the local Scouts' Association (Mr. Jones) and pre- sented a very smart appearance. The Lymm and District Defence Corps was strongly repre- sented in the procession, the men giving proof by the smartness of their marching that they have profited by the Saturday afternoon drills which they have recently been undergoing. They were under the command of Captain Kershaw, and Messrs. G. R. Williamson, Crompton and Holmes.</p>
        <p>Members of the National Reserve, in com- mand of Mr. Casse, and of the Lymm Fire Brigade; in charge of Superintendent Kirk- patrick, were also in attendance. The Urban District Council was represented by Councillors T. H. Warburton (chairman), Gotobed, F. Ingham, Critchley, Groves and bolt, and, among others, the following officials: Messrs. William Mullard (clerk), D. Mort (surveyor), C. Mort (a'ssistant), West (gasworks manager), )4lercer (registrar), and Dr. De Jong (medical officer). The local Magisterial Bench was represented by the Hon. J. E. Cross, Mr. A. Watkin, Mr. Callender, and Mr. W. Peter Rylands; with whom was Mr. Harris (magistrates' clerk). . . .</p>
        <p>The Tradesmen's Association—the promoters of the affair—was largely represented, and three of its members—Messrs. Daniels, Hillier and Evans—acted as marshals.</p>
        <p>IN THE CHURCH. The Selfishness of Loneliness.</p>
        <p>At the church every available seat had to be requisitioned to accommodate the congregation.</p>
        <p>The service was conducted by the Rector (the Rev. G. B. Thurston), assisted by the Revs. Horace Gray and Tudor Evans, whilst the les- son was read by the Rev. E. E. Lovell (the local Baptist minister).</p>
        <p>The Rev. R A. Bell, of St. Ignatius, Salford, preaching from the words: "The whole family" (Ephesians iii. 15), said that the father was the imperial master of every subject in his small kingdom—the family—but although all were subservient to him, it was the mother who made the home complete, for she provided what was lacking in her husband's nature. Amongst the children there might be one weaker than the rest, but the strong, the healthy and the vigorous ones kept watch over the weaker day by day and night by night, remindful that it was their privilege to use their strength in the right direction. By so doing they were pre- vented from becoming selfish and hard-hearted.</p>
        <p>Self-Sacrifice the Keynote' of Patriotism. The keynote of family life was self-sacrifice, because one felt an interest in all the others. That was also the keynote in patriotism. A life was not lived in the real sense if it was lived* the spirit of selfishness. Self-concentra- tion, loneliness brought about by one's own expressed will and desire, was the outcome of an unconverted heart, and not only so, for individualism of that description was extremely painful from every point of view, because a man who lived a solitary life of his own free- will did not realise citizenship, nor patriotism, to the full. As in family life, so in the every- day life of the world, man must take an inter- est in helping those who are in a worse condi- tion than himself. He could take them into houses in his own parish which were a disgrace and were not fit habitations for any living being. He was not acquainted with Lymm. Possibly there were similar houses in that dis- trict, and if there were it was the bounden duty of every man who called himself a citizen to see that those houses were made fit for habi- tation.</p>
        <p>The sick needed nursing, but, besides those, there was crime to be dealt with. The clergy and the laity must join together in dealing with that, for it was impossible to turn a man away from sin by the simple process of giving him six months' imprisonment. Very often im- prisonment only hardened a man's nature,</p>
        <p>The Reclamation of the Errin9. • He should be told of the life of Christ, of His sufferings and His voluntary sacrifice, and, if the sinner had any heart left at all, it would melt as he thought that Christ was even con- tent to suffer death on the Cross for such a one as him.</p>
        <p>He believed, the preacher went on, a good many men had left that district to take uP arms, but there were many others who had not. Over and over again he was told, in his own parish, that a man ought to do as he pleased, and his reply to that contention was: "No! He ought not. A man ought fn do as he ought to do." After referring to the death of Lord Roberts—" that noble patriot "—the preacher said that men were talking of univer- sal peace. It was miles off; in fact, there would be no such thing until the love of Christ was in every man's heart.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0147" n="art0147">
        <head>£lO for Three Local Charities.</head>
        <p>£lO for Three Local Charities.</p>
        <p>_ The collection, which amounted to £lO 55., is to be divided between the Rector's Sick and Poor Fund, the District Nurse Fund, and the local Convalescent Home Fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>MOORE.</head>
        <p>MOORE.</p>
        <p>Concert for Red Cross Funds.—A highly suc- cessful concert was held in the Milner Church Institute on Monday evening, in aid of the funds of the local branch of the Cheshire Red Cross Society. An excellent programme was presented. Songs were contributed by the Misses R. Tanner, E. Done, Sloane, Peake and A. Lawton, the Rev. W. Scott Adams and Mr. M. Cartwright, whilst the Misses A. Millington and K. Rowlands gave a pianoforte duet. Pin- suti's beautiful quartet, "In this hour of softened splendour," was effectively rendered by the Misses A. Lawton and A. Millington and Messrs. Rushton and M. Cartwright. Miss Hutchings gave two solos. Special features of the programme were three humorous sketches in which the following took part :—Misses A. Millington, W. Done, M. Eustance, E. Done, G. Done, E. Tanner, R. Tanner, Edith Done and A. Hankey. The sum of X 6 lls, was raised for the purpose in hand.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>HATTON.</head>
        <p>HATTON.</p>
        <p>FOR WOUNDED " TONIMIES."</p>
        <p>" Oaklands ", to be Used as a Convales-</p>
        <p>cent Home.</p>
        <p>Through the kindness of Mr. and Mrs. Piers Jones, " Oaklands," Preston Book, was offered as a home for Belgian refugees, but, as this part of Cheshire has been proclaimed a pro- hibited area, no Belgian refugees can be re- ceived, and so " Oaklands" is to be used as a convalescent home for wounded soldiers. A large and influential committee has been ap- pointed to undertake the arrangements, and it is hoped that shortly twelve soldiers will be received, and, no doubt, from time to time, Belgian soldiers will be among the number.</p>
        <p>The convalescent i home wll, of course, in. volve heavy expense, towards which the fol- lowing generous weekly subscriptions have been already promised :—Mr. C. Tinsley, £1; Mr. E. Davies, Mrs. E. Davies, Miss Milner, Mr. j. Lea, The Era vny ad, an Mrs.ntsd st 3Airr ms Walton. i wt ay nne Jon Whitley, Mr. H. Greenall, Mrs. Pgeiers(w.Jaolnteosn, Vicarage, Lea), Wr - es Mrs. J. E. Secker, Mrs. E. Ridgway, Mrs. Trevi: thick, Mr. Ziba Armitage, Mr. W. Bleckly, Miss Sloane, and Mr. W. Sloane, ss. each; Miss Armi- tage, Miss B. Dutton, and Mr. Earp, 2s. 6d. each. :efajrcsobn;pesS:e</p>
        <p>Gifts and Loans Offered and Required. Mr. T. Houghton, Daresbury, has promised ss. worth of groceries; Lady Greensll is giving all the coal; Mrs. Charles Jones, Preston Brook, is sending 12 large loaves every week. The Hatton people have given Mrs. J. Whitley .0 for the home and numerous offers of vege- tables and crockery are forthcoming. The Moore Work Party are making night- shirts and bed jackets for the inmates. The committee thank those who have offered to lend or give beds and also those who have prom:se-I gifts of crockery, jam, &amp;c. The following articles are'-still required : Tablecloths, towels, basins, pie-dishes, pans, spoons and forks. Any desirous of assisting by making a small weekly subscription, or by giving personal service, are asked to communi- cate with Mr. E. Davies, honorary secretary, Daresbury Hall; or any of the following ladies :—Mrs. Greenall, for Moore; Mrs. Whit- ley, for Hatton; Mrs. Seeker, for Daresbury and Newton; Mrs. Broome and Mrs. S. Whitlow, for Preston Brook.</p>
        <p>There will be an American tea at the Milner Church Institute on Thursday, December 3rd, the proceeds of which will be given to the home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0150" n="art0150">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>Sunday School Workers Wed.—The marriage was solemnised in the Wesleyan Chapel on Wednesday of Miss Ada Bates, daughter of Mr. George Bates, of Chapel-road, Penketh, and Mr. Mark Ray, of Laurel Bank, Penketh, the Rev. H. T. Dixon officiating. The bride, who was given away by her brother (Mr. John Bates) wore an exceedingly becoming costume of brown satin cloth, trimmed with cream silk and lase, and a velvet hat, with ostrich plume to match. The Misses Alice um! Lily Bates (sisters of the bride) made charming brides- maids, being attired in pale blue Irish poplin and brown velvet, with black velvet hats, trimmed with swansdown. Mr. Noah Bay (brother of the bridegroom) acted as best man, and Mr. A. Clatworthy presided at the organ. The•young couple, who have been for some few years active workers in the Sunday School and Chapel, had proof of their popularity in. the numerous and varied presents they recelved, among them being a number of substantial cheques. The honeymoon is being s; .rat at Blackpool.</p>
      </div>
