      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <head>N.U, PLAYERS' WAGES.</head>
        <p>N.U, PLAYERS' WAGES.</p>
        <p>To Be a Matter of Arrangement Between Clubs and Men.</p>
        <p>RELIEF FUND FORMED.</p>
        <p>The position which has arisen as the result of certain players and referees having refused to accept the reduction of wages and fees pro- posed some few weeks ago—consequent, largely, upon anticipated reductions in "gates" owing to the war—was considered at a meeting of representatives of the Northern Union Clubs, held in Manchester on Tuesday night. - At the outset it was stated that some of last Saturday's matches were played under protest, and, after considerable discussion, it was re- solved to cancel the earlier decision to reduce wages and fees all round, and to make reduc- tions of wages a matter of arrangement between the clubs and their players.</p>
        <p>HOW RELIEF FUND WILL BE RAISED. Further, it was decided to form a Northern League Clubs' Relief Fund, and to make' an applicatipn to the Northern Union for a grant of ..tl,OOO, and to the British Playing Fields Society for £5OO, to form the basis of the fund. When ,the receipts at any Northern Union League match amount to .t5O and not over £lOO a contribution of 2? per cent, of the gross takings must be made to the fund. When the receipts are over .2100 the contribution will be five per cent. The Northern Union are re- quested to order the same contributions from receipts at cup-ties, and if the money available dots not adequately meet the requirements of the clubs the Northern Union will be asked, without the calling of another special meeting of the League, to contribute another .Ql,OOO. GRANTS FOR NECESSITOUS CLUBS. _ .</p>
        <p>It was further decided that when a club which finds itself unable to pay full wages arranges with players for a reduction of wages the amount so deducted has to be forwarded to the League Relief Fund, which will be under the control of the League Committee, and from which grants will be made to necessitous clubs. Referees' and touch-judges' fees, together with the usual railway fares—says another decision arrived at—will be paid from the relief , fund, and it is left to the referees to make any reduc- tion in their fees that they think advisable for the benefit of the fund. The decision arrived at will be a source of satisfaction to the committee of the Warrington Club, who, all the way through, have contended that any reduction in wages should be made by mutual Consent between the players and their respective committees, and should not be arbi- trarily forced upon the clubs. Byer since the decision re the 25 per cent. reducti6n was arrived, at on October 20th, the Warrington. Committee have used their influence to get it upset. At the meeting at which that result was achieved the Warrington Club was represented by its honorary secretary, Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, who, by the way, seconded the resolution rescinding the decision to reduce referees' and touch- judges' fees. On the proposition of the Warrington Secre- tary it was also decided at the meeting that the players' wage lists sent to the League Secretary be returned to the clubs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>RYLANDS' RECS. v. BRADFORD AND CLAYTON.</head>
        <p>RYLANDS' RECS. v. BRADFORD AND CLAYTON.</p>
        <p>At Bradford. Team meet at Central Station at 12.45 p.m. : R y land s' Recs.: F. Iforrocks ; J. Griffiths, H. Povey, A. Horrocks and A. Rowlinson; G. War- ren and J. Gallop; J. Brandvi-ood, J. Whittaker, F. Totter, E. Smith, W. Shaw, F. Baguley. Re- serves, A. Penn and J. Dumbell.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0157" n="art0157">
        <head>YOUNG CHILDREN.</head>
        <p>YOUNG CHILDREN.</p>
        <p>Importance of Care Before Reaching</p>
        <p>School Age.</p>
        <p>ADULT SCHOOL DISCUSSION.</p>
        <p>At the fortnightly current events class of the Sankey Bridges Adult School on Sunday after- noon, Mr. Wilfrid Rowland opened a discussion on "Children between 1 and 5 years." He pc...toted out the beneficial results which had followed to babies from concentrated public attention. He pleaded for more close attention to the wel- fare of the children who had survived the first year of life, but had not yet reached school age. The record of Lancashire regarding child life was not what it ought to be, as revealed in sta- tistics. Mental development was dependent upon the proper development of the brain, the organ of the mind. Nothing could make up in later life for a failure in normal brain gro,vtli during the first seven years of life. Trained observers had noted that brain growth was of on arrested where the whole physical development had been held back. Many of the deaths amongst little children were preventable, e.g., tuberculosis ought to be made in time as rare as typhus fever. There was not nearly enough done for convalescent children. After severe illness they ought to be freshly fortified against further attacks of disease. With regard to food, there was much ignor- ance and carelessness. Milk was not used suffi- ciently as a food, while tea was frequently given to tiny children with evil result. A great deal of money was wasted by well-meaning poor par- , ents on patent foods. Cleanliness was a factor of vast importance in child life. It was easy to condemn people for lack of cleanliness, but when means were limited it was perfectly natural to spend money on food and clothing, before soap. The lack of facilities for getting hot water vas a serious thing :11 many poor homes. _</p>
        <p>The discussion covered a wide area, but most interest seemed to centre in the provision of baths. It was suggested that a number of small bath-houses in various parts of the town might serve a more useful purpose than the prc33ut centralised system. One member pointed out that the extra water rate on houses containing baths was, in effect, a tax upon cleanliness. The total public revenue derived from this source was probably not great, and it was questionable 1 whether there was any net ga:n to the commu- nity from the standpoint of public health.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0158" n="art0158">
        <head>IRELAND OF ANCIENT DAYS.</head>
        <p>THE IRELAND OF ANCIENT DAYS.</p>
        <p>Ireland was the subject of the lecturette at the Stockton Reath Men's School by Mr. W. Parker. It was not the Ireland of yesterday's controversies, but the Ireland of ancient days, when it was the Western Sun of Religion and Civilisation. The lecturer spoke of the long reign of the Gaels with their freedom and tribal law, and their strong individuality that ab- sorbed successive invading hosts of Danes and Normans. He showed how in far-off days, music, literature and art flourished, so that the Emerald Isle became famous throughout Europe. The Book of Kells was mentioned as being the most beautiful volume in the world. The strains of the Irish harp were to be heard in East Europe, while Irish artistic work in stone and gold and silver were evidences of her ancient refinement. In the discussion it was suggested that modern Ireland owed some of her weaknesses to the late retention of the tribal or clan system, as against the more centralised government which the Normans imposed upon Saxon England, to her lasting welfare.</p>
        <p>Proof. The Judge: "What proof have you that this chauffeur was intoxicated?" The Country Policeman: " lle stopped his car at a drinking trough for horses."</p>
        <p>imi</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0159" n="art0159">
        <head>FOILED BY THE FOG.</head>
        <p>FOILED BY THE FOG.</p>
        <p>Association Clubs' idle Afternoon NOTES BY "LINESMAN."</p>
        <p>On Saturday Warrington and District was plunged into murky gloom during the whole day by a dense fog, which caused a wholesale postponement of the League fixtures down for settlement. In two instances, however, had the visiting teams put in an appearance the games could have been played. The Football Association's rule on the matter ?aye that, irrespective of the weather, all teams should turn up at the dressing-room of the club on whose ground the matches should be played, and that the referee is the official who has to decide whether it is fit to play or not. Consequently, the players commit a grave error of judgment when they decide amongst them- selves whether they shall travel or not. In the Earlestown. district the fog cleared away a few minutes before three o'clock, and the appointed official in one instance said he could see the whole length of the playing pitch until half-past four. That being the case, it shows. how much both the White Star and Latchford Baptist teams wtre at -fault in not making the journey to their opponents' grounds according to rule. In my notes of the previous week I referred to the non-appearance of the Latchford Bap- tist team to fulfil their fixture with the Long- ford United Club. Of course, it was well known that the Baptists were expecting the greater portion of their team to consist of players from Earlestown, and during last *week- end it came to my knowledge that these players made the journey to Warrington, but instead of going to the Longford ground, they went to watch the Rugby match between Warrington and Rochdale. Last week, again, only two of the Earlestown players arrived at the Mission ground, but none came from Warrington. Surely the management of the Baptists' Club must have got into a very bad way if they cannot raise a team from their own members in their own district. .</p>
        <p>„.._. All these matters will come up for discussion at the monthly • meeting of the League, which will be held during the week, when the default- ing clubs should be made to give a satisfactory explanation to the committee, failing which strict measures should be enforced, to prevent, if possible, similar occurrences in the future. The Hollins Green men turned up in readi- ness for their match, only to find a telegram awaiting them, stating that their opponents, the Newton Cons, had missed their train. The Stockton Heath and Broadheath Lads teams duly put in an appearance on the ground of the first-named, in readiness to carry out their North Cheshire League engagement, but the referee (Mr. W. Smith) opined that owing to the. fog it would be impossible for him to follow the game, and therefore declared the match off until some future date. Great Sankey are due to meet the Calder and Mersey combination in a Widnes and District Alliance tie to-day, and the match should draw a good crowd to the Penketh Recreation Ground. The Calder and Mersey team at present top the Alliance chart.</p>
        <p>GREAT SANKEY PLAYERS IN A 'BUS ACCIDENT. The Great Sankey team journeyed by 'bus to Cronton on Saturday afternoon for the purpose of keeping an engagement with the locals. Half-way between Penketh and Farnworth the party drove into a particularly dense fog bank, and before they got out of it a motor car from Wigan crashed into ;their vehicle, knocking one of the horses down and stunning it. The bonnet of the 'bus was damaged and the wind-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>MISSING MINUTE.</head>
        <p>A MISSING MINUTE.</p>
        <p>Question at Education Committee's , Meeting Causes "Slight Breeze."</p>
        <p>Something in the nature of what is generally spoken of as "a slight breeze" arose at the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening. Councillor Pemberton referred to the fact that the minutes of the School Management Sub-com- mittee went from No. C. 4244 to No. C. 4246, whilst later on minute C. 4253 referred the mem- bers to minute C. 4245. [Minute C. 4253 read: "Further correspondence from the managers of the Trinity School (see resolution No. C. 4245) having been submitted, the Director was given instructions thereon.] It was, the Councillor said, a very awkward situation for the members. They were asked to pass the minutes of a com- mittee which said "See resolution No. C. 4245," and when they turned to see what that minute was they found that it was secret. The Chairman (Councillor Roberts): You ought not to make suggestions of that kind! The committee have done nothing they are ashamed of. Councillor Pemberton (with emphasis) : I give my time to the committee, and I want to know what is going on. The Chairman: Your suggestions as to the reasons why a committee do, or do not, certain things are not suggestions which are going to be entertained from this chair. You are not en- titled to make a suggestion as to the reasons— Councillor Pemberton: I asked what the reasons were. Are they ashamed of them? The Chairman: You may ask, and go on ask- ing, but you'll get no reply ; at least, not to a question of that kind.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>AN UNFOUNDED SUSPICION.</head>
        <p>AN UNFOUNDED SUSPICION.</p>
        <p>Canon Stevenson, the chairmirn of the sub- committee, subsequently explained that the facts were quite simple. Some few months ago the committee agreed with the managers of Holy Trinity School that the infants who were then in the Cairo-street School should be placed in the Holy Trinity School—thus effecting some economy—and the Cairo-street School was to close. Both children and teachers were to be moved into the Holy Trinity School, thus form- ing, for the first time in the history of that school, an infants' department. It was hoped that the infants' department would form a feeder for the senior department, but when the time came for the children to go up, it was found that the parents, with one exception, pre- ferred to send them to other schools. The man- agers of Holy Trinity School considered _that the Head Mistress had persuaded or influenced parents not to send their children to the Holy Trinity School. That suspicion was communi- cated to the School Management Sub-committee. They investigated the matter, and had before them a written statement from the lady con- cerned, in which she said that in no case, either directly or indirectly, had she influenced any child to go to any other school than Holy Trinity. That fact was communicated .to the correspondent of the managers of the "Holy Trinity School, and the School Management Committee received a letter from him at their last meeting, in which he said that he accepted the assurance that had been given. There, for the moment, the matter rested.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>Local Leather Trade.</head>
        <p>Local Leather Trade.</p>
        <p>"Business last week," reports "The Leather Trades Review," "seemed to divide itself into two sections; the demand for deliveries being insistent, while forward buying was steadied and new contracts were not so numerous as usual lately. It may be that factory require- ments are now fairly covered for orders already placed, and prices are on too high a scale to attract speculation. This slight slackening in buying has no effect on the market, as most tanners are so far sold up as to keep them working at high speed for the next three or four months, with the certainty that their ware- ' houses will be kept clear of stock all the time. The home trade is showing more activity, light bends in particular having sold well, and while prices have certainly not advanced propor- tionately to the heavier classes, quotations are from 4d. to 6d. higher than they were in the summer. Prices are exceedingly strong all round, advances being a matter of almost daily' occurrence, and it is very rare indeed that an order can be repeated at the previous figure." '</p>
        <p>screen was smashed to atoms, but, fortunately, the driver escaped with no more serious injury than a out arm. The horse, too, was only slightly hurt, and, after a short halt, the foot- ball party were able to complete their journey.</p>
        <p>INJURED PLAYER'S RECOVERY.</p>
        <p>A. Perrin, of Hollins Green, who had the</p>
        <p>_ misfortune to get his leg broken a few weeks ago, when playing against Fairfield Athletic, and was taken to the Warrington Infirmary, has so far recovered that he was allowed to go to his home on Saturday.</p>
      </div>
