      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <head>WINTER ANGLING.</head>
        <p>WINTER ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Where Local Piscators are Finding Sport.</p>
        <p>FORTHCOMING N.A.A. MEETING.</p>
        <p>The members of the Warrington Working Men's Angling Association (N.A. Centre), who indulge in winter fishing, generally select the Gowy as their happy hunting ground, but this last week or so Llangollen has been the favoured spot, and although a little later on, no doubt, the Gowy will come into its own, one must admit that Llangollen has turned out more specimens than the Gowy in comparison with the number of members that visit both places. The Shropshire Union Canal is drawing many .members, despite the fact that the best fish has been taken from the river. Taking the mem- bers on the whole, it would seem that they pre- fer canal to river fishing. Whether this has come about through the Association not visiting the Weaver one cannot say; but if members know that their season's contest is going to take place in a river, it certainly has a tendency to draw anglers to that particular kind of stream for their week-end sport, as by so doing they are `:killing two birds with one stone," as the say- ing goes, in that they get both sport and prac- tice.</p>
        <p>Llangollen has not provided the roach re- quired to upset calculations, and it will have to be a good one to do so, as 11b. 7oz. 6dr. roach are not to be caught every day, but there are a few members who are willing to take small odds that they hook one before December 31st.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>COOPER'S ARMS CLUB REVIVED.</head>
        <p>COOPER'S ARMS CLUB REVIVED.</p>
        <p>The Cooper's Arms Angling Society has made a fresh start, and we are informed that the secretary pro. tem. has already signed on twenty new members. If the officials selected put the ordinary amount of enthusiasm into their work there is no reason why the dill) should not again take as prominent a part in local piscatorial affairs as, for instance: the Bay Horse or the Gladstone Arms societies.</p>
        <p>THE N.A.A. GENERAL MEETING. The N.A.A. have decided to hold their annual meeting in the Old Music Hall, Market-place, to-day (Saturday), when it is expected that there will be present representatives from all the big fishing clubs of Lancashire, "Cheshire and York- shire. The agenda is a very heavy one, and, among others, the old question of the close season is to be brought up. Although the N.A.A. have passed a resolution to observe such a season, and have done fairly well this season in keeping to it, there are loop-holes in the regula- tions drawn up that will doubtless cause no little comment at the forthcoming meeting. Members will learn through this column how matters stand after the meeting. Warrington anglers have always otserved the close season, except in the matter of pikes and eels. But, then, the angler who goes in specially for pike claims that that particular finny denizens of our rivers should also be protected, in spite of the fact that fishermen rent streams where they I could well do without him. There can be no I denying the fact that the pike is a coarse fish, and if the F.F.P.L. will only take the matter up and make it a sine qua non that all societies must obey their ruling in its entirety, anglers will know what they may and may not do with regard to him. But what are they to do when they are ordered from headquarters to obey the close season, and at the same time they know of some members who belong to two different Associations taking pike and ignoring the rule? If this matter is definitely settled at the meet- ing it will mean the doing away with one ques- tion upon which Warrington anglers have long been divided. A.S.</p>
        <p>No man is happy who does nat think 11;inse1f so.</p>
        <p>A baby seems to grow more and more like its father every day it doesn't.</p>
        <p>About the most senseless thing in the world is to offer a reward for the return of a lost opportunity.</p>
        <p>Bishop Mallalieu, of the Methodist Church, says he would like to see all the nude statues in Boston's Museum of Fine Arts used for paving the streets.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>SCHOOL "WEAR AND TEAR."</head>
        <p>SCHOOL "WEAR AND TEAR."</p>
        <p>Education Committee Again Consider</p>
        <p>Oft-Debated Point.</p>
        <p>The following minute figured among those submitted by the Sites and Buildings Sub-com- mittee for approval at the monthly meeting of the Education Committee on Monday evening</p>
        <p>- "A question as to the charges for fair wear and tear, which were fixed by the committee in February last, having been raised, it was re- solved that the proportion of the cost of making good any damage due to fair wear and tear of the schools under section 7 (1) (d) of the Educit- tion Act, 1902, be charged as regards six-sevenths to the local authority, and as segards one- seventh to the managers; and that a previous resolution be amended accordingly." Mr. Jarman said that the matter had been dis- cussed in conimittee and the members were given to understand that up to the present the arrangement had been five-sevenths as against two-sevenths. Therefore, it would appear that a new charge was being proposed against the local authority. As the local authority did not use the non-provided schools on Saturdays, nor during the evenings, he failed to see any particu- lar reason why the proposed alteration should be made, and why the local authority should be saddled with the extra one-seventh, ,so far as repairs were concerned. Not only had the managers the use of the schools on Saturdays and Sundays, if they desired them, but they could also use them every evening during the week, and consequently it did not seem to him that two-sevenths was an unfair charge. PROFITS OUT OF RATEPAYERS WRONG. Alderman Tinnion proposed as an amendment that the matter be referred back. He saw lights burning in certain of the schoois in ques- tion on a good many occasions during the winter —suggesting that they were used for a number of purposes unconnected with the Education Committee—and he thought that the low charge of one-seventh was unfair to the ratepayers. He did not see why the managers should make a profit out of the ratepayers, and that was the reason why he proposed that the matter should go back to the sub-committee for further con- sideration.</p>
        <p>.11101=01•10=101=1011MOMOMMO011 MONIOMOOMMIOM011•0•1110=011=01</p>
        <p>Mr. Jolley seconded. Mr. R. T. Fairclough said that the school of which he was treasurer used to be heated by eight stoves and fireplaces before it came under the Education Committee. When it came under their control the committee thought that rather extravagant to have all those fires going, and so the managers spent over £lOO for the benefit of the committee, in putting in a boiler and pipes. As to the number of times the school was used of an evening, he found that it was utilised three times in six months for the purposes of a sick and burial club, whose meetings lasted about twenty minutes each. Considering that they paid a shilling for each of those twenty minute meetings, that they found the light and the schools, and tha they not only got no rent, but had to go round begging for money to pay for certain things which were imposed upon them, he absolutely failed to see why they should also be made to pay for the Saturdays on which they did not use the buildings. Re thought the proposed charge a most unjust one, and he was surprised that any member of the committee should consider that they ought to pay two- sevenths for the use of the building on one day in the week. The thing was absurd.</p>
        <p>WHAT THE OLD ONE-SEVENTH WAS FOR. In answer to questions, the Director (Mr. J. Moore Murray) explained that before the alloca- tion had only been in respect of the painting and decorating, and was equal to one-seventh. In February of this year the Sites and Build- ings Sub-committee allocated the proportion of all charges due from the managers at two- sevenths. Mr. Jolley, explaining his reasons for second- ing Alderman Tinnion, said the matter ought not to be considered as a thing by itself; it was part of a much larger question, viz., the finan- cial relationship between the local authority and the managers of the non-provided schools. As one who took an active part in settling the original terms, he objected to any alteration without full and complete consideration; the question should not be considered piecemeal, but in its entirety. On the amendment being put, eleven voted for and eleven against. The Chairman, "following excellent prece- dent "—as he explained—gave his casting vote for the amendment, adding, "the matter will take no harm through re-consideration." The minute was accordingly referred back.</p>
