      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>Summary of Authentic News From All</head>
        <p>Summary of Authentic News From All</p>
        <p>Involved Areas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0174" n="art0174">
        <head>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER.",</head>
        <p>SPECIALLY COMPILED FOR "THE EXAMINER.",</p>
        <p>SEVENTE</p>
        <p>OUR HOLD ON YPRES.</p>
        <p>GERMANS ONLY GET THERE AS</p>
        <p>PRISONERS.</p>
        <p>The German attacks on the Allies, and par- ticularly upon the neighbourhood of Ypres, held by the British, for the ostensible purpose of reaching Calais, has slackened, but the lull has given " Eye Witness" with General French's staff leisure to send us several dispatches ex- plaining at length how the situation stands. His messages are full of satisfactory informa- tion. Dealing with events up to November 19, by which time winter had set in in earnest, he declared that the position of the Allies at Ypres is stronger than it has ever been. On the 19th the Germans, while massing for an attack, were caught by our artillery with devas- tating results. The Germans advanced obliquely across our front, and suffered so severely from gun and rifle fire that 1,200 dead were left in front of a length of about 600 yards of our trenches.</p>
        <p>"Eye Witness" corrects mis-statements to the effect that the enemy had penetrated the Allies' line at Ypres and had been driven out again. Ypres is, and has been since the battle-front was drawn before it, in the hands of the Allies. No German has succeeded, except as a prisoner or a a spy, in entering the town or even in getting near it.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0175" n="art0175">
        <head>CONDITION OF THE TRENCHES.</head>
        <p>CONDITION OF THE TRENCHES.</p>
        <p>The condition of the trenches, says the "Eye Witness," became after the first fall of snow " wretched beyond description." The men had to stand in half-frozen slush. "The problem of how to enable them to keep their feet warm and dry is now engaging serious attention."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0176" n="art0176">
        <head>VATS FOR BATHS.</head>
        <p>VATS FOR BATHS.</p>
        <p>At one place, owing to the .kindness of the proprietor, certain works have recently been placed at our disposal, says "Eye Witness," as a wholesale bath-house, lavatory, and repair shop. In the works are a number of vats large enough to contain several men at one time, and they serve most excellently for the provision of hot baths for the men on relief from the trenches. Whilst they are enjoying a bath, their clothes are taken away. The undercloth- ing is washed or burnt and replaced by a new set, whilst the uniform is fumigated, cleaned, and repaired, and buttons are sewn on and re- pairs done by a gang of women who are em- ployed for the purpose. At this installation some fifteen hundred men are catered for every day. . . . Weary, unshorn, and haggard, they are coated with mud, a good deal of which has crusted on them, and some are splashed with the blood of their comrades, or of the enemy. When they come out, clean, refreshed, and reclothed, they are different beings, and not only is this a good thing from the point of view of the happiness and comfort of the individual; it is a distinct gain in his fighting value and an asset to the force to which he belongs. Nevertheless, bodily the men are in good con- dition. Food in abundance has reached them regularly, except in a few cases such as are in- cidental to trench warfare. GERMANS' APPALLING LOSSES.</p>
        <p>A later communication from "Eye Witness" illustrated by quotations from diaries taken from captured German soldiers the appalling losses sustained by the enemy and the moral effect upon his troops of the Allies' artillery fire. Yet another dispatch from "Eye Witness," dated November 23, describes the hardships suffered by troops in the trenches as a result of the severe cold, which has also interfered with aerial work. The presence of snow on the ground makes clear the position of trenches, and against the white background men, transport, and ,railway trains stand out with dangerous distinctness.</p>
        <p>Our line has now been so strengthened that the men in the trenches can be relieved regu- larly and frequently, and thus gain necessary rest.</p>
        <p>CHEERING NEWS FOR THE TROOPS. The news of the destruction of the Emden naturally caused immense satisfaction amongst all ranks; and at one place where the opposing trenches were especially close together it was greeted with cheers and at once thoughtfully passed on with comments to the enemy. The result was that our trench was fired at heavily for some time. The reaction produced in France by a British success in the Indian Ocean may interest some of those who took part in the naval action.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0177" n="art0177">
        <head>YPRES IN FLAMES.</head>
        <p>YPRES IN FLAMES.</p>
        <p>German Revenffe for Non-Success. On Sunday there were violent bombardments of Ypres, which destroyed the Hotel de Ville and the central market, set the town on fire and destroyed its belfry, its cathedral, its markets, and many of its houses—the price that Germany exacts for successful resistance.</p>
        <p>Further afield similar vengeance was dealt out to Reims and Soissons.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0178" n="art0178">
        <head>CHANGE OF GERMAN PROGRAMME. _</head>
        <p>A CHANGE OF GERMAN PROGRAMME. _</p>
        <p>The German -moderation in attack has been accompanied by German activity in their rear, and it was surmised that they were massing for another onslaught, this time in the neighbour- hood of Lille. Throughout Monday and Tues- day the pause continued. On the Belgian railways unusuat activity was observed, while near the coast thousands of troops are gathering.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0179" n="art0179">
        <head>AN IMPORTANT ADVANCE.</head>
        <p>AN IMPORTANT ADVANCE.</p>
        <p>" From the North Sea io--ip-r-e,"-s-itid Wed- nesday's Paris bulletin, "there has been no further infantry attack." North-east of Ypres the Allied line has been pushed forward be- tween Langemarck and Zonnebecke—a very im- portant advance.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0180" n="art0180">
        <head>SLACKENING.</head>
        <p>SLACKENING.</p>
        <p>Yesterday a notable slackening off by the enemy all along the line was reported.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0181" n="art0181">
        <head>ZEEBRUGGE BOMBARDED.</head>
        <p>ZEEBRUGGE BOMBARDED.</p>
        <p>GERMANS GET THE HINT TO GO.</p>
        <p>Germany has been boasting that Zeebrugge was to be fitted up as a naval base to facilitate operations against England. Formal notice was taken of this on Tuesday, when two British battleships knocked the place to pieces and seriously interrupted the plans of the Germans. It was stated that two Allied squadrons were engaged. One bombarded Zeebrugge, doing severe damage to the harbour-works and locks and setting fire to many !buildings. The other squadron co-operated with a land force which attacked the German positions near Rieuix-at. Two German batteries were silen.ed. The Allied squadron finally withdrew, having one torpedo-boat seriously damaged. German sub- marines attacked the squadron which bom- barded Zeebrugge.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0182" n="art0182">
        <head>NORTH FRANCE.</head>
        <p>NORTH FRANCE.</p>
        <p>GERNIAN ATTACKS THAT NEVER</p>
        <p>SUCCEED.</p>
        <p>Paris reports "a very warm day" in the Argonne on Monday, where the enemy made "a number of very hot attacks" without success. The French made progress in the Argonne and gained ground at Four-de-Paris, a point of much strategical value. From La Bassee to Soissons the battle-front has been quiet. Further east, near Berry-au- Bac and in the Argonne, the perseverance of</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0183" n="art0183">
        <head>:ENTH WEEK.</head>
        <p>:ENTH WEEK.</p>
        <p>the French has carried them forward slightly. Near Verdun and in the region of Pont-a- Mousson there has been more activity. A Ger- man attack north-west of the great French frontier fortress was repulsed. The German:, asked an armistice, which was refused. Arnaville, in the Pont-a-Mousson region, a town right on the Franco-German frontier and within 10 miles of Metz, has been bonibarded by the French artillery.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0184" n="art0184">
        <head>BRILLIANT INDIAN TROOPS.</head>
        <p>BRILLIANT INDIAN TROOPS.</p>
        <p>Capture of German Officers, Men and</p>
        <p>Guns.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday, in the neighbourhood of La Bassee, the Indian troops recaptured some trenches from which the enemy had driven them on Monday. The Press Bureau on Wednesday afternoon issued a special communication deszribing the feat of the Indian troops. Not only were the trenches taken back from the Germans, but three German officers and more than 100 men, with one mortar and three machine guns, fell into their hands.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0185" n="art0185">
        <head>GREAT AIR FEAT.</head>
        <p>GREAT AIR FEAT.</p>
        <p>BRITISH AVIATORS ATTACK ZEPPELIN FACTORY.</p>
        <p>On Saturday a flight of aeroplanes under the command of Squadron-Commander E. F. Briggs, of the Royal Naval Air Service, with Flight. Commander J. T. Babbington and Flight-Lieu- tenant V. S. Sippe as pilots, flew from French territory to the Zeppelin airship factory at Friedrichshafen, on Lake Constance. All three pilots in succession flew down to close range under a heavy fire from guns, mitrail- leuses, and rifles, and launched their bombs according to instructions. _ _</p>
        <p>Commander Briggs is reported to have been shot down, wounded, and taken to hospital as a prisoner. Both the other officers have re- turned safely to French territory, though their machines were damaged by gun fire. They report positively that all bombs reached their objective, and that serious damage was done to the Zeppelin factory. This flight of 250 miles, which penetrated 120 miles into Germany, across mountainous coun- try, in difficult Weatkel• conditions, constitutes with the attack a fine -Aat of arms.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0186" n="art0186">
        <head>CROSS OF HONOUR.</head>
        <p>CROSS OF HONOUR.</p>
        <p>A dispatch from-B-elfo;i describes the enthu- siasm which prevailed among the general popu- lation and the soldiers there on Sunday, when, during the review, the Governor pinned on the breasts of two returned British airmen the crosses of the Legion of. Honour. The modest bearing of the recipients aroused much corn ment.</p>
        <p>_ It is understood that the cross has also been awarded to. Squadron Commander Briggs:_</p>
        <p>Later details from the frontier near Fried- richshafen state .that four bombs fell on the Zeppelin works._</p>
        <p>The Germans deny that any damage was done. On the occasion of the British air raid on the Dusseldorf Zeppelin it was said in Germany that the damage was trivial, whereas an eye-witness stated later that both shed and airship were destroyed and several officers and men killed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0187" n="art0187">
        <head>GERMAN SUBMARINE SUNK CREW HOISTS WHITE FLAG AFTER BEING RAMMED.</head>
        <p>GERMAN SUBMARINE SUNK CREW HOISTS WHITE FLAG AFTER BEING RAMMED.</p>
        <p>'"ie German submarine U 18 was reported on the northern coast of Scotland on Monday morn- ing. At 12.30 a British patrolling vessel reported having rammed her.</p>
        <p>She was not sighted again until 1.20, when she was seen on the surface, the crew on deck, and flying the white flag. Shortly after this she foundered just as the destroyer Garry came alongside and rescued three officers and 23 of her crow, one only being drowned:</p>
        <p>The UlB represented- one of the latest types of German submarine boats. At the beginning of the year the U 21 was the last turned out, this being a boat of 800 tons, carrying two guns in addition to torpedo tubes, and steaming 17 knots on the surface and 12 submerged. ANOTHER SUBMARINE DAMAGED. _ _ .</p>
        <p>On Tuesday at noon the German submarine U 17, making towards Bergen, was met by Nor- wegian torpedo boats. The captain of the submarine declined assist- ance. lie said his engine was damaged, but he would repair at Bergen. On being told that he would he interned if he entered Bergen, he left again, being accompanied outside territorial waters by the Norwegian torpedo boats.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0188" n="art0188">
        <head>DESTROYER REPORTED SUNK.</head>
        <p>DESTROYER REPORTED SUNK.</p>
        <p>Collision With Danish Steamer. On Monday morning the Danish steamer Anglo-Dane, belonging to the United Steamship Company, and bound from Strettin to Copen- hagen, collided at the southern entrance to the Sound with the German torpedo boat S 124. ' The torpedo boat was carrying no lights and was unobserved. The Auglo-Dane cut half-way through the torpedo boat between the funnels, and an explosion occurred. The S 124 sank, but two other German torpedo boats arrived and rescued her crew.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0189" n="art0189">
        <head>BATTLESHIP BLOWN UP.</head>
        <p>BATTLESHIP BLOWN UP.</p>
        <p>H.M.S. Bulwark blew up in Sheerness Har- bour on Thursday morning, apparently from an explosion in thg,magazine. In announcing this disaster in the House of Commons, Mr. Churchill stated that only 12 men of the crew were saved. The loss of life is between 700 and 800</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0190" n="art0190">
        <head>GREAT RUSSIAN VICTORY.</head>
        <p>GREAT RUSSIAN VICTORY.</p>
        <p>At the time of going to press the nation was anxiously waiting for official confirmation of a great Russian victory in Poland. If such a vic- tory has been won, as many correspondents state, the Russians will soon be on the highway to Berlin. The German march upon Warsaw has, we know, been stopped, and the only ques- tion is as to the punishment meted out to the Invaders. It is officially announced that there have been captures of 8,000, 5,000 and 3,500 Ger- mans, and so on, and that the Russian invasions of East Prussia and Austria are progressing satisfactory; but what is wanted is official news of a German catastrophe, in the centre. The latest Russian official report stated that "in the Lodz battle, which eontinue3 to develop, the advantage is secured for our troops." Lord Kitchener on Thursday night told the House of Lords that after a hotly-contested battle the reinforced Russian troops have. been able "to check and defeat the ffermans with, I believe, heavier losses than they have sustained before."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0191" n="art0191">
        <head>EGYPT.</head>
        <p>EGYPT.</p>
        <p>An Indian camel corps is officially announced to have had an "Affair of outposts" with the enemy in Egypt, and to have done well. Bedouin and Turkish raiders had previously been re- ported in the Sinai peninsula.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0192" n="art0192">
        <head>JOIN, ri% 9 The Army of people who are LO • Wearing our famous Overcoats.</head>
        <p>JOIN, ri% 9 The Army of people who are LO • Wearing our famous Overcoats.</p>
        <p>Grand Clothing Hall</p>
        <p>BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>I J_</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0193" n="art0193">
        <p>THE EXAMINER, SATURDAY, NOVEMBER 28, 1914.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>EAST AFRICA.</head>
        <p>EAST AFRICA.</p>
        <p>The news from East Africa is by no means satisfactory. A force from British East Africa landed on the coast of German East Africa on November 2, and attacked an important Ger- man railway terminus. Information that the place was weakly held turned out to be incorrect. The first attack of our men, carried out by a battalion and a half of infantry, was beaten off. Reinforcements arrived and the attack was renewed on Novem- ber 4th. . . _</p>
        <p>The 101st Grenadiers forced an entry into the town, while the North Lancashire Regiment and the Kashmir Rifles also fought their way to its outskirts. Heavy fire from the houses com- pelled a retreat. The losses were so heavy and the, position so strong that it was considered useless to renew the attack. The force re- embarked and returned to its base to prepare for future operations.</p>
        <p>PRISONERS SHOT.</p>
        <p>CAMP RIOT QUELLED AT DOUGLAS</p>
        <p>A regrettable incident took place on Thurs- day afternoon last week at the Alien Detention Camp at Douglas, where there are 3,500 pri- soners.</p>
        <p>The guard, consisting chiefly of the National Reserve, were standing within the room separ- ating the men from the kitchens. At each side 1 and at the outside were the remainder of the I guard. The aliens evidently determined that their easiest way of escape was by way of the kitchen, leaving the guard in the rear. The plot had evidently been carefully thought out, and at a given signal, by a window being broken with a chair, the aliens attacked the guard with their cups and saucers, tables, and anything which they could obtain. The guard fired a volley into the air, but, this failing to quell the riot, a second volley was fired, and four aliens fell either dead or so seriously wounded that they died directly afterwards. Twelve others who were badly wounded were conveyed to hospital at Douglas, where one had to have his arm amputated, and another is so seriously injured that little hopes are entertained of his recovery. As soon as the aliens saw that the guards meant business and that the guns they carried, although of an old make (the Martini carbine), were deadly weapons, they stopped their rush and held up their hands in surrender. The doctor and the remainder of the guards were quickly on the scene and all possible help was given to the injured men. It was then discovered that one of the prisoners had broken through a window in the gallery with the in- tention of escaping over an adjoining field. He ran along the roof, and, stepping on a glass roof, he fell through on to the stone floor below, breaking his skull. This brought the number of killed to five.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>FOR BELGIAN REFUGEES. i</head>
        <p>FOR BELGIAN REFUGEES. i</p>
        <p>Another Warrington Hostel Got Ready.</p>
        <p>The sub-committee who have been busily engaged for the last fortnight furnishing Nun- nerley House, St. Austin's-lane, the new Bel- gian refugee home, wish to express their grate- ful thanks to the many 'kind friends who have come forward in such a generous manner that the house has been furnished most comfortably, with only a small call on the general fund. All who have visited the house have expressed ' themselves highly pleased. It is expected that the refugees will arrive in the course of a few days, and the committee will be most grateful for any gifts of provisions, fish, fruit and vegetables from time to time. Lady Greenall has undertaken to bear the cost of all the butchers' meat required for the home.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0196" n="art0196">
        <head>SUBSTANTIAL HELP FROM A WHIST DRIVE.</head>
        <p>SUBSTANTIAL HELP FROM A WHIST DRIVE.</p>
        <p>A successful whist drive and dance in aid of the Belgian Refugees' Fund was held in Cairo- street Schoolroom. About 200 people competed in the whist drive, Mesdames Jeffreys, Locker- bie and Cummins being the successful ladies, while Messrs. Cummins, Lightfoot and Hall secured the gentlemen's prizes. Prizes, sub- scriptions and refreshments were given by friends. Dancing afterwards was enjoyed, Mr. Hale, who volunteered his services, proving an efficient pianist. During the evening, through the kindness of Mr. Turpin, of the Hippodrome, Mr. Ford entertained the company with songs and recitations. Mr. B. Bate was in his usual good form. Messrs. Boscow, Newns, Mercer and Pickton were able M.C.'s. It is expected that a sum of .118 or £2O will be banded to the fund.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0197" n="art0197">
        <head>CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION HELP.</head>
        <p>CALEDONIAN ASSOCIATION HELP.</p>
        <p>In aid of the Belgian Refugees' Fund a suc- cessful whist drive and dance was held in the Cairo-street Schoolroom on Thursday evening, the event being promoted by the Warrington Caledonian Association. The prize-winners were:— Ladies: 1, Mrs. Cotterel ; 2, Mrs. Rob- inson; 3, Mrs. A. Taylor. Gentlemen: 1, Mr. J. Bennett; 2, Mr. Royston; 3, Mr. Morley. At the finish Mr. .T. Fulton-Smith (the president) gave a short address, and Mrs. J. Tennant pre- sented the prizes for the whist, as well as bowling prizes won during the season, which went to the following :—Ladies: Igrs. W. Mur- ray, Mrs. T. Tennant, Mrs. Melbourne, Mrs. Stirling, Mrs. Robertson, Mrs. Marshall, Mrs. Donnelly, Mrs. Bowman, Mrs. Bennett. Gen- tlemen: Messrs. P. Lyall, Glegg and Rogers. A vote of thanks was accorded the pianist, Mr. Louis Hale.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0198" n="art0198">
        <head>LIEUT.-COL. GREEN KILLED.</head>
        <p>LIEUT.-COL. GREEN KILLED.</p>
        <p>A Former "0.0." at Orford Barracks. The brief intimation which appeared in the casualty lists at the beginning of the week that Lieut.-Colonel Malcolm Charles Andrew Green —better known here as Major Green—for his promotion to a lieutenant-colonelcy only dated from a few weeks ago—had been killed in action, occasioned much regret in Warrington, where the deceased officer was well known and widely respected, he having held the command at the regimental depot at Orford Barracks for a matter of two years just prior to the outbreak of the war, during which time he made his residence at The Glen, Latchford Without, with his wife and three children, the eldest of whom is only five years of age. He left Warrington for Tidworth on that recent memorable night when a rousing send-off was given to a contin- gent of reserves of the South Lancashire Regi- ment, and was drafted to the front only ten days before he met his death. Prior to leaving War- rington he had been given the command of one of the new service battalions, consisting of men who had enlisted in Kitchener's Army, but a fortnight or so before lie was killed he was ordered to France to take charge of the 2nd Battalion, a distinction he highly prized, as the battalion had been giving a splendid account of itself in the field and had worthily upheld the glorious traditions of the regiment of which lie was so proud, and whose praises he was never : tired of singing, either in public or in private. THE COLONEL'S CAREER. The late Lieutenant-Colonel was born in 1871. and gained his first commission at the age of twenty. He was made a lieutenant in 1894, a captain in 1900, and a major in 1909, and was an adjutant of volunteers from 1903 to 1907. Be took part in the South African War, being pres- ent at operations in Natal in 1900, including the action at Laings Nek. He received the Queen's medal, with four clasps. HIS INTEREST IN HIS MEN. Lieut.-Colonel Green took a personal interest in all his men—from the almost time-expired man down to the most raw recruit—their wel- fare ever being his first consideration, whilst the men repaid his kindly interest in them by loyal and devoted service.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1914-11-28_WidnesExaminer_art0199" n="art0199">
