        <p>As previously announced, a lecture on the Woe and work of Florence Nightingale will be ffiArey by Mrs. Ridgway at the Parr Hall on lifen";c evening, December 7th, under the patronage to the Mayor and Mayoress, and in aid of "5. Redden Court Hospital for wounded sOl .ere So far there have been 47 patients admitten .1, the hospital. • Altogether there are 33 beds Wits able, and there are at present 15 Belgian arlDo British wounded in occupation of them. hospital, under the Matron (Mrs. Reynolds) fl,:er her staff, not to mention the Quarterr; urn° (Mrs. Aylmer Frost), has already earned a o for efficient management, and the visit/11,r medical men, Drs. Bower and Hutton, are tiring in their attendance on the patients. I,id establishment is therefore doing a very fine patriotic work; and Mrs. Ridgway's lecture ",,t aid of the funds—the hospital is to a gre, extent supported by voluntary contributiene ought to receive generous support. THE HOUSE LENT RENT FREE. e Raddon Court is lent for the purpose,°;,it- hospital rent free by Messrs. Greene% v•-i- -ley and Co. As the house had not been ee,'„ta pied for some years, a great deal of work i're to be done to make it habitable. Walls neric scraped and whitewashed, and the wood Ireri painted, &amp;c. This was done by voluareti, workers, and the place was then fitted our,,l::,its gifts and loans of furniture, &amp;c., from Inl•• individuals.</p>
        <p>WORKED AS A SUB-BASE HOSPITAL-0 Lady Greenall and Mr. Ralph Johnson bald been particularly generous to the hospital, Mr. Richardson is supplying the coal ,n,g nominal fee, whilst the Steam Laundry is the washing for the patients free of char n. When ready, the temporary hospital was,tl,r spected by the military authorities, and al meats 1134 complete satisfaction at the arrang Offiei' been expressed, was accepted fox the War by Colonel PitzGerald. It is now being w°,1.1,,‘ • in conjunction with the Base Hospital O. .ill chester, and helps to relieve the num-oil:111,4 heavy congestion there. As will be rowep realised, considerable funds are needed to e A the hospital in an efficient state. There 1' a, great deal of wear and tear to be made eot. and numerous minor expenses have to be riliec- Those who have not heard Mrs. RidgwaY g ture should take this opportunity- of doing,;:y She is well known not only locally, but in 1114'er. parts of England, both as a lecturer and and as the authoress of many helpful and chill ing volumes of poetry. HE TWENTY-EIGHT WOUNDED MEN T</p>
        <p>INFIRMARY. There are now 28 wound soldiers bel f attended at the Warrington Infirmary. Ove ° ~,3 the soldiers, who had been there some time A-0 had made excellent recoveries, were discharg on Monday, and there have not been an/ Pe arrivals.</p>
        <p>A KIND ENTERTAINER. , Miss Etta Bodmer, of Newton-le-Wilt-V,`; continued her visits to Raddon Court Red u 4, Hospital on Monday and Wednesday afterioaii3•l4.. and highly delighted the .reettPeratiflt "Tommies" with her singing. For the benelo of the Belgian soldiers, she rendered eecelo• foreign songs, thereby earning their heal • applause. CONVALESCENTS LEAVE LYMPA•i Fifteen of the wounded soldiers who a!'tol been staying at the Lymm Red Cross 110,90 had so far recovered that they were able leave, on Wednesday, for brief furloughs 1) their homes.</p>
