        <p>PADDINGTON V. FAIRFIELD UNITED. At Padgate-lane; kick-off 2.45: Paddington: J. Harrison; E. Holmes (capt.) and J. Chamberlain; W. Holmes, J. Taylor and W. Spencer; W. Garnett, S. Taylor, T. Wood- head, J. Darbyshire and C. Smith. PADDINGTON RESERVE v. LATCHFORD BAPTISTS' RESERVE. At Paddington; kick-off 2.45:— Paddington Reserve: T. Knight (captain); J. Dwerryhouse and E. Vickers; B. Chadwick, 4. Brown and J. Adcroft ; T. Wainwright H. Wil- kinson, J. Holcroft. S. Summers and J. Picker- ing. Reserves, C. Forrest and Warburton.</p>
        <p>CRICKET. A POOR LANCASHIRE COUNTY SEASON. In the annual report of the Lancashire County Cricket Club it is stated that the re- sults of the past season are disappointing. Bad weather on many occasions and the effect of the war upon the attendances at matches in August are assigned as reasons. The balance sheet shows a loss on the season of .81,314 12s. 3d. Included in this deficiency is the guarantee of .8500, plus a donation of .820 and the expenses arising out of Huddleston's benefit, which real- ised .11896. The gate receipts for that match amounted to 08343. Donations and subscrip- tions to the special fund, amounting to .84,307, were received, and that sum, together with 84 life members' subscriptions, £1,323, had been credited to the general cash account and not treated as part of the year's income. ' The committee gratefully acknowledge the assistance given by Lord Derby, whose appeal enabled them to reduce considerably the Mort- gage on the ground, to add appreciably to the investment reserve and to augment the mem- bership, Of 25 matches played six only were won out- right, and Lancashire fell to the eleventh place on the championship list. This position must be regarded as exceptional, but there were ex- ceptional circunistances. Apart from the gener- ally recognised need of a first-class bowler, there were early misfortunes which emphasised the weakness. The absence of three of the regular bowlers severely handicapped the side from the outset, and entailed much extra work upon Whitehead. THE CARE OF THE FEET. A South Rhodesian correspondent of the r Bristol Times and Mirror" gives the follow- ing, practical advice :—The care of the feet is undoubtedly one of the most important consid- erations for a marching soldier. Sore feet send hundreds to hospital. Let every recruit soak his feet for half an hour in a basin of water— cold will do—with a double handful of common salt, to make a strong brine; do that for three days, and he can then march 1,000 miles with perfectly sound feet. Let him have woollen socks, and be sure boots are not too small. In the cold grease the boots. If at any time while on campaign the soldier can sleep with his boots off, so much the better. 'Twas Ever Thus. "Patty," said grandma, "I think it about time you stopped playing with boys. Little girls ought not to care to play with boys, when they're as large as you." "Oh, that's all right, grandma. Why, the bigger we get, the better we like 'em !"</p>
